window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["computer-architecture"]=[{"id": "CO-U2-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a hardwired control unit, the control signals are generated by", "options": ["a control store that holds microinstructions", "combinational logic fed by the instruction register, the status flags and the timing signals", "an interrupt vector table indexed by the interrupt number", "a microprogram counter together with a mapping ROM"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A control unit is hardwired when its output signals are produced by a fixed combinational network. The inputs to that network are the instruction opcode, the condition flags and the timing (T-state) signals.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The instruction register supplies the opcode bits that identify which instruction is executing.</li><li>The status flags supply the conditions on which conditional operations depend.</li><li>A sequence counter and decoder supply the timing state, so the same opcode yields different signals in T0, T1 and so on.</li><li>The network ANDs and ORs these inputs into the individual control lines, so no memory is consulted while the instruction executes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: combinational logic driven by opcode, flags and timing signals (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options A and D describe a microprogrammed unit, where the control word is read from a control store. The two designs differ precisely in whether a memory lookup sits on the control path.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which property of a hardwired control unit makes it preferable to a microprogrammed one?", "options": ["Its control logic can be changed by rewriting the contents of a control store", "It needs no clock signal", "It supports long, complex instruction sets with less hardware", "Its control signals come from fast combinational logic, so the control path is short"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The trade-off between the two control designs is speed against flexibility. Combinational logic answers in gate delays; a microprogrammed unit must first read a microinstruction from the control store.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A hardwired unit evaluates a fixed Boolean function of opcode, flags and timing state, so its delay is a few gate levels.</li><li>A microprogrammed unit adds the control-store access time to every step, which is what makes it slower per control word.</li><li>The price of that speed is rigidity: any change to the instruction set means redesigning the logic.</li><li>Hence speed, not modifiability or hardware economy, is the hardwired advantage.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a short, fast combinational control path (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A is the microprogrammed advantage, not the hardwired one; option C is also false, because long complex instruction sets are exactly where microprogramming saves hardware. Every control unit still needs a clock.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A hardwired control unit must generate 6 distinct timing states, one per clock cycle. What is the minimum number of bits in its sequence counter?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sequence counter with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> bits drives a decoder with <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> outputs, so it can distinguish at most <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> timing states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two bits give 4 states, which is fewer than the 6 required.</li><li>Three bits give 8 states, and 8 ≥ 6.</li><li>So the counter needs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log<sub>2</sub> 6⌉ = 3</code> bits, leaving two decoder outputs unused.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing 6, the number of states, instead of the number of bits needed to encode them. A counter stores a code, not the count of states.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An instruction set has 12 instructions and its longest instruction needs 9 timing states. The control unit decodes the opcode with an instruction decoder and steps through the states with a sequence counter. What is the minimum width of the opcode field and of the sequence counter?", "options": ["4 bits opcode and 4 bits sequence counter", "3 bits opcode and 3 bits sequence counter", "4 bits opcode and 3 bits sequence counter", "12 bits opcode and 9 bits sequence counter"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both fields hold a binary code, so each needs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log<sub>2</sub> n⌉</code> bits for <code class=\"mm\">n</code> distinct values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Opcode: 3 bits address only 8 instructions, so 12 instructions need 4 bits (16 codes, 4 unused).</li><li>Timing states: 3 bits address only 8 states, so 9 states need 4 bits (16 states, 7 unused).</li><li>Both fields are therefore 4 bits wide.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4-bit opcode and 4-bit sequence counter (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding 9 states down to 3 bits because 8 feels close enough, or sizing the opcode by the number of instructions rather than by the number of codes needed to name them.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about hardwired control are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["It is faster than microprogrammed control because the control signals come from combinational logic rather than from a control store.", "It is easy to extend when the instruction set changes, because a new instruction only needs new microcode.", "It uses a sequence counter and a decoder to generate the timing states of the instruction cycle.", "A single combinational network can generate every control signal, so no control store is needed."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Hardwired control is a fixed Boolean network: fast, rigid, and driven by opcode, flags and timing state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) No memory access sits on the control path, so the control signals settle in gate delays. True.</li><li>(B) Adding an instruction means redesigning the logic and rewiring the network; new microcode is the microprogrammed route. False.</li><li>(C) The sequence counter produces T0, T1, ... and its decoder output is ANDed into the signal equations. True.</li><li>(D) That absence of a control store is exactly what defines the design. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing hardwired control is also the flexible option. Speed and modifiability pull in opposite directions here, and microprogramming is the answer to the second.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A hardwired control unit sequences 7 timing states, T0 to T6, from a 3-bit sequence counter feeding a 3-to-8 decoder. How many decoder outputs are never used?", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">k</code>-bit counter decodes into <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> outputs; if the machine uses fewer states than that, the surplus outputs stay idle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A 3-bit counter produces 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 codes, so the decoder has 8 outputs.</li><li>States T0 to T6 use 7 of them.</li><li>8 − 7 = 1 output, the code 111, is never asserted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 unused output.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the states as the decoder size, or assuming the spare code is harmless. An unused state must still be handled, since a glitch on the counter can land the machine there.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An instruction needs 20 distinct timing states, and the design decodes every state directly from a binary sequence counter. What is the minimum counter width, and why is a narrower counter unsafe here?", "options": ["4 bits, because states can be reused under different flag conditions", "5 bits, because 4 bits decode to only 16 states while 20 distinct states are required", "20 bits, one bit per state", "4 bits plus a 2-to-1 multiplexer on the decoder output"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A counter of width <code class=\"mm\">k</code> can name <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> distinct states. If every state must be decoded uniquely from the counter alone, the width is forced by the state count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Four bits decode to 16 codes, and 16 &lt; 20, so two states would share a code and the control signals of the two would be asserted together.</li><li>Five bits decode to 32 codes, and 32 ≥ 20, so every state gets its own code and 12 codes remain spare.</li><li>Hence the minimum width is 5 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 bits (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A describes a real but different technique: reusing a state under different flag conditions requires extra decision logic and a branch, which the question excludes by fixing the decoding to the counter alone. Without that extra logic, 4 bits silently merges states.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a microprogrammed control unit, the microinstructions are held in", "options": ["the main memory, alongside the program being executed", "the control store, also called the control memory", "the data cache", "the general-purpose register file"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A microprogrammed control unit replaces the control logic with a small memory whose words are control words; each word is a microinstruction that asserts a set of control signals for one step.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The control store is addressed by the microprogram counter.</li><li>Each location holds the control word for one micro-operation.</li><li>The sequencer decides the next address, so the microprogram walks through the steps of the current instruction.</li><li>Main memory and cache hold program and data, never control words.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the control store / control memory (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the control store with main memory. It is a separate, usually read-only array inside the control unit, and its width is the number of control lines rather than the machine word size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A microinstruction format that assigns one bit to each control line, with no encoding of mutually exclusive signals, is called", "options": ["a horizontal microinstruction", "a vertical microinstruction", "a diagonal microinstruction", "a two-level or nano microinstruction"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Horizontal and vertical microcode sit at opposite ends of the width-versus-decoding trade-off. A horizontal word is wide and fully decoded; a vertical word is narrow and encoded.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One bit per control line means no decoder is needed and every signal can be asserted independently.</li><li>That independence lets many micro-operations run in parallel within one microinstruction, which is the horizontal advantage.</li><li>The cost is a very wide control word, since the word must carry a bit for every control line.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a horizontal microinstruction (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming wider always means faster in a way that makes vertical code useless. Vertical code trades parallelism for a small control store and is often preferred when the store is a bottleneck.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A control store holds 1024 microinstructions addressed by the microprogram counter. How many bits must the microprogram counter have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The microprogram counter supplies the address of the next microinstruction, so its width must span every location of the control store.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1024 = 2<sup>10</sup>.</li><li>Ten bits name 1024 distinct addresses, exactly the number of locations.</li><li>No spare code remains, so every address is a legal microinstruction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1024, the number of locations, instead of the logarithm. Also watch the power-of-two boundary: 1023 locations would still need 10 bits.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A microinstruction consists of 40 control bits and a 10-bit sequencing field. The control store holds 512 microinstructions. What is the total capacity of the control store in bits?", "options": ["25,600 bits", "20,480 bits", "51,200 bits", "22,528 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Control-store capacity is the word width multiplied by the number of words, and the word width includes every field, not only the control bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Word width = 40 + 10 = 50 bits.</li><li>Number of words = 512.</li><li>Capacity = 512 × 50 = 25,600 bits.</li><li>Cross-check: 512 × 40 = 20,480 control bits, and 512 × 10 = 5,120 sequencing bits; the sum is 25,600.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 25,600 bits (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option B counts only the control field and forgets the sequencing field, which is part of every stored word.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about microprogrammed control are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Vertical microprogramming encodes mutually exclusive control signals into fields that a decoder expands, giving a narrow control word at the cost of decoding delay.", "A horizontal microinstruction is wide and can assert many control signals in parallel within a single microinstruction.", "A new instruction can be added by writing new microcode, without redesigning the control logic.", "A microprogrammed control unit always executes an instruction faster than a hardwired unit."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Microprogramming buys flexibility with time: the control word comes from a memory, and encoding it narrowly adds a decoder in series.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Encoded fields shrink the word, and the decoder adds delay. True.</li><li>(B) One bit per signal allows simultaneous assertion, which is the horizontal strength. True.</li><li>(C) The instruction set is defined by the control store contents, so microcode changes are enough. True.</li><li>(D) The control-store access time makes the microprogrammed unit slower per control word, not faster. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating microprogramming as a pure improvement. It is a trade: flexibility and simplicity in exchange for speed and, in the horizontal case, control-store width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A control word has three encoded fields, each of which selects exactly one of 10 mutually exclusive control signals, plus 6 control lines that each require their own bit. What is the minimum width of the control word?", "options": ["18 bits", "24 bits", "30 bits", "36 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An encoded field holding <code class=\"mm\">n</code> mutually exclusive choices needs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log<sub>2</sub> n⌉</code> bits and a decoder to expand it; a signal that must be controlled independently needs a bit of its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each encoded field must name one of 10 signals: ⌈log<sub>2</sub> 10⌉ = 4 bits.</li><li>Three such fields take 3 × 4 = 12 bits.</li><li>The 6 independent lines take 6 bits.</li><li>Total = 12 + 6 = 18 bits.</li><li>Cross-check the encoding capacity: each 4-bit field has 16 codes for 10 signals, so 6 codes per field are spare, which is acceptable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 18 bits (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C comes from adding 10 + 10 + 10 instead of encoding, i.e. from using one bit per signal and forgetting that the field is decoded. Option B comes from dropping the 6 independent lines altogether.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An ALU has 3 function-select inputs. How many distinct operations can it select?", "options": ["3", "6", "9", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Select lines form a binary code, so <code class=\"mm\">k</code> of them name <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> distinct operations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Three select bits produce codes 000 through 111.</li><li>That is 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 codes.</li><li>Each code routes one ALU function to the output, so eight operations can be selected.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 operations (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3, the number of select lines, or 9 by counting 3 × 3. The select lines are a code, not a count.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In an n-bit ALU, the zero flag is normally produced by", "options": ["ANDing all the result bits", "ORing all the result bits", "NORing all the result bits", "XORing all the result bits"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The zero flag must be 1 exactly when every result bit is 0, which is the definition of a NOR over all bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If any bit is 1, the OR of all bits is 1 and the result is not zero.</li><li>So &ldquo;all bits zero&rdquo; is the complement of the OR, that is the NOR.</li><li>In hardware an n-input NOR is usually built as an OR tree followed by an inverter, which keeps the delay logarithmic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: NOR of all result bits (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> ANDing the bits detects the all-ones case, not zero. A zero-detector must react to the OR being 0, so the flag is the inverse of an OR, not an AND.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a single-bus CPU, a register-to-register ADD traverses: source register output 2 ns, bus 1 ns, ALU 5 ns, bus again 1 ns, and destination register setup 2 ns. What is the minimum clock period in ns?", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period must cover the longest combinational path between two registers, plus the setup time of the register at the end of that path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Register output to bus: 2 + 1 = 3 ns.</li><li>Through the ALU: 3 + 5 = 8 ns.</li><li>ALU output back onto the bus: 8 + 1 = 9 ns.</li><li>Destination setup: 9 + 2 = 11 ns.</li><li>The bus is used twice because a single bus carries one value at a time, so the two transfers cannot overlap.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding only one bus delay because the bus is one physical wire. It is one wire used twice, so both uses cost time in the same cycle.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A 32-bit ripple-carry adder is built from full adders in which the carry takes 2 ns per stage, and the sum bit of a stage takes a further 3 ns after its carry-in has settled. What is the worst-case delay to the final sum bit S31, measured from the arrival of the operands?", "options": ["62 ns", "64 ns", "65 ns", "96 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a ripple-carry adder the carry propagates serially, and each sum bit needs one more delay after its own carry-in is valid.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Carry into bit <code class=\"mm\">i</code> is valid at <code class=\"mm\">2i</code> ns, since each stage adds 2 ns.</li><li>Bit 31 needs carry <code class=\"mm\">C31</code>, valid at 31 × 2 = 62 ns.</li><li>The sum bit then takes a further 3 ns: 62 + 3 = 65 ns.</li><li>The final carry-out C32 would be valid at 64 ns, which is why 64 is the answer only if the question asks for the carry.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 65 ns (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 64 ns, the carry-out time, instead of the sum time; or multiplying the sum delay by 32, which would wrongly assume the sum bits also ripple.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about datapath timing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The clock period must be at least the longest register-to-register combinational delay plus the destination register setup time.", "Inserting a pipeline register can shorten the clock period, at the cost of more cycles of latency for the same instruction.", "A single-bus datapath executes an ADD in fewer clock cycles than a three-bus datapath.", "A pipeline register placed between the ALU and the destination register shortens the combinational path that fixes the clock period."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period is set by the worst register-to-register path. Pipeline registers cut that path into shorter pieces; buses and their sharing decide how many transfers a step needs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) That inequality is the definition of a valid clock period. True.</li><li>(B) Each register halves the logic between two registers, so the period falls while the instruction needs extra cycles to traverse the added stage. True.</li><li>(C) A single bus must be reused for each operand and for the result, so it needs more cycles, not fewer. False.</li><li>(D) Placing the register at the ALU output ends the ALU-to-register path there, so only the shorter segments bound the period. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing pipelining reduces the total work of an instruction. It reduces the clock period and raises throughput, but a single instruction takes more cycles than before.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A datapath register has a clk-to-Q delay of 3 ns, the ALU takes 6 ns, the result multiplexer takes 2 ns, and the destination register needs 2 ns of setup time. What is the minimum clock period?", "options": ["11 ns", "13 ns", "15 ns", "8 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A register-to-register path is bounded by four delays in series: clk-to-Q of the launching register, the combinational logic, any multiplexer on the result path, and the setup time of the capturing register.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Launch: 3 ns.</li><li>ALU: 3 + 6 = 9 ns.</li><li>Result multiplexer: 9 + 2 = 11 ns.</li><li>Setup: 11 + 2 = 13 ns.</li><li>13 ns is therefore the shortest safe period, giving a clock of about 76.9 MHz.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 13 ns (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A drops the multiplexer, and option D drops both the clk-to-Q and the setup time. Every element on the path between the two register edges must be counted.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The width of a system bus is conventionally quoted as the number of", "options": ["address lines", "data lines", "control lines", "interrupt request lines"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A bus is described as an 8-bit, 32-bit or 64-bit bus according to how many bits it can carry in one transfer, which is the data-line count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The data lines move the operand itself, so their number sets the transfer granularity.</li><li>The address lines set the addressable range instead: <code class=\"mm\">n</code> address lines reach <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>n</sup></code> locations.</li><li>Control lines carry timing and command signals and are counted separately.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: data lines (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing bus width with address width. A machine may have 32 data lines and 40 address lines; only the first is called the bus width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a synchronous bus, every transfer is timed with respect to", "options": ["a handshake between the master and the slave", "the ready signal of the slowest device on the bus", "a common clock shared by all devices on the bus", "the interrupt acknowledge cycle of the CPU"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Synchronous buses include a clock line and fix every event to a clock edge; asynchronous buses replace the clock with request and acknowledge handshakes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All devices derive their timing from the same clock, so protocol steps take a known number of cycles.</li><li>That makes the bus simple and fast for devices that can meet the timing.</li><li>Its weakness is that the cycle must be long enough for the slowest device, or a ready/wait line must stretch it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a common clock (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options A and B describe the asynchronous bus, where handshaking lets each device take the time it needs. A ready line can appear in a synchronous bus, but only as an exception to the fixed timing.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A synchronous bus has a 32-bit data path and a 100 MHz clock, and completes one transfer per clock cycle. What is its maximum bandwidth in MB/s? (Use 1 MB = 106 bytes.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "400", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bus bandwidth is the bytes moved per transfer multiplied by the number of transfers per second.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>32 bits = 4 bytes per transfer.</li><li>100 MHz gives 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> transfers per second.</li><li>4 × 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> = 400 × 10<sup>6</sup> bytes per second.</li><li>That is 400 MB/s in decimal megabytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 400 MB/s.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 3200 by using 32 bytes instead of 4, or 419 by dividing by 2<sup>20</sup> when the question specifies decimal megabytes. Also watch the cycle count: one transfer per cycle is given here, and a multi-cycle transfer halves or quarters the figure.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A bus has a 64-bit data path, a 66 MHz clock, and requires 2 clock cycles per data transfer. What is its effective bandwidth?", "options": ["264 MB/s", "528 MB/s", "132 MB/s", "4224 MB/s"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective bandwidth is the transfer size divided by the time one transfer occupies, so a multi-cycle transfer must divide the raw clock rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>64 bits = 8 bytes per transfer.</li><li>Two cycles per transfer means 66 × 10<sup>6</sup> / 2 = 33 × 10<sup>6</sup> transfers per second.</li><li>8 × 33 × 10<sup>6</sup> = 264 × 10<sup>6</sup> bytes per second.</li><li>So the effective bandwidth is 264 MB/s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 264 MB/s (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option B ignores the two-cycle transfer, and option C halves twice by also treating the 64-bit path as 4 bytes. Divide the clock once for the cycle count, and use the full data width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about bus organisation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A three-bus datapath can read both operands and write the result within one clock cycle without an intermediate temporary register.", "In a single-bus organisation each transfer occupies the whole bus for a clock cycle, so the micro-operations of an instruction are serialised.", "A wider data bus shortens the transfer time of a single word even when that word already fits in one transfer.", "A bus with several potential masters needs an arbitration mechanism to decide which one drives it."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of buses fixes how many values can move at once; bus width fixes how much one value can be; arbitration fixes who may drive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Two source buses feed the ALU and a third carries the result back, so no operand has to wait for the bus to be freed. True.</li><li>(B) One bus means one value per cycle, so operands and result are moved one after another. True.</li><li>(C) Once the word fits in a single transfer, extra width cannot shorten that transfer; it only helps wider operands or block transfers. False.</li><li>(D) Contention must be resolved, whether by daisy chain, polling or independent requests. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating bus width as a universal speed-up. Beyond the width of the data being moved, extra lines buy nothing on a single-word transfer.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about bus arbitration are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Daisy-chain arbitration grants the bus by physical position on the chain, so a device nearer the arbiter wins a tie.", "Independent-request arbitration gives each master its own request and grant line, and any priority policy can be implemented.", "Polling arbitration uses a counter to select masters in turn and needs no separate grant line for each master.", "Daisy-chain arbitration gives every master the same worst-case access latency."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three standard schemes resolve contention: daisy chain (grant propagates physically), independent requests (one grant per master), and polling (the arbiter asks masters in turn).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The grant signal passes through each device in order, so the nearest device sees it first and captures it. True.</li><li>(B) With a grant line per master the arbiter may use fixed priority, rotating priority or any other rule. True.</li><li>(C) Polling drives a common select code from a counter, so one set of lines serves all masters. True.</li><li>(D) In a daisy chain a far device must wait for every nearer device to pass the grant along, so latency depends on position. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming daisy chaining is fair because the grant is passed down the line. It is a fixed priority scheme, and the last device in the chain can be starved indefinitely.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit is designed from a control step matrix. What do the rows and the columns of that matrix represent?", "options": ["one row per instruction; one column per control signal", "one row per microinstruction; one column per micro-operation", "one row per timing state (T-state); one column per control signal", "one row per control signal; one column per condition flag"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The control step matrix is a two-dimensional table of the control function: one row for each timing state and one column for each control signal, with a 1 in cell (T<sub>i</sub>, S<sub>j</sub>) meaning that S<sub>j</sub> is asserted while the unit is in state T<sub>i</sub>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The unit steps through one timing state per clock, so the number of rows equals the number of T-states.</li><li>Each column corresponds to one control signal that the datapath needs, such as <code class=\"mm\">PCout</code> or <code class=\"mm\">MARin</code>.</li><li>A 1 in a cell means the signal is asserted in that state; a 0 means it is not.</li><li>Instructions do not form the rows: they are decoded separately into instruction lines that gate the rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">one row per timing state (T-state); one column per control signal (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Swapping the axes, as in option A, is the usual slip. Microinstructions (option D) belong to microprogrammed control, where the control word is stored rather than computed.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit sequences its 11 timing states with a one-hot ring counter that uses one flip-flop per state. How many flip-flops does the counter require?", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A one-hot (ring) sequencer carries one flip-flop per state, so its width grows linearly with the number of states; a binary counter grows logarithmically but needs a decoder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One flip-flop is needed for each state, because exactly one flip-flop is active at a time.</li><li>With 11 states that is 11 flip-flops.</li><li>A binary counter would need ⌈log<sub>2</sub> 11⌉ = 4 flip-flops plus a 4-to-16 decoder and 11 output gates.</li><li>The one-hot form trades flip-flops for the absence of a decoder and of glitch-prone decoded lines.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">11</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 4 by recalling only the binary counter. The question fixes the one-hot design, where the count of flip-flops equals the count of states.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A control unit is implemented as a PLA with 12 inputs, 8 product terms and 10 outputs, where every input and every output has one programmable connection to every product term. How many programmable connections does the PLA contain?", "options": ["80", "96", "176", "216"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A PLA has two programmable planes: the AND plane forms the product terms from the inputs, and the OR plane sums selected product terms into each output.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>AND plane: every one of the 12 inputs is connected (or not) to each of the 8 product-term lines, so 12 × 8 = 96 connections.</li><li>OR plane: every one of the 8 product terms is connected (or not) to each of the 10 outputs, so 8 × 10 = 80 connections.</li><li>Total = 96 + 80 = 176 programmable connections.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">176 (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only the AND plane and answering 96, or using the input count twice and answering 12 × 8 + 12 × 10 = 216. The OR plane is fed by product terms, not by inputs.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit sequences its T-states with a counter feeding a decoder. Which of the following statements about that arrangement are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An n-bit counter with an n-to-2n decoder can provide up to 2n timing states.", "If the END signal clears the counter at the end of every instruction, the next instruction restarts at T0.", "A one-hot ring counter produces the timing states directly and needs no decoder.", "A one-hot ring counter needs fewer flip-flops than a binary counter for the same number of states."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The sequencer is a counter plus a decoder, or a one-hot shift register that replaces both; either way the number of states is bounded by the hardware that generates them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: n flip-flops carry 2<sup>n</sup> codes and the decoder turns each code into one state line.</li><li>Option B is true: clearing the counter on END is what makes the state sequence repeat for the next instruction.</li><li>Option C is true: in a ring counter exactly one flip-flop is high at a time, and that flip-flop is the state.</li><li>Option D is false: a ring counter uses one flip-flop per state, while a binary counter uses ⌈log<sub>2</sub> n⌉, so the ring counter uses more.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reversing the flip-flop trade-off: the one-hot form needs more flip-flops, and buys its saving by removing the decoder and its decoded lines.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A hardwired control unit decodes a 4-bit opcode into 16 instruction lines, but the instruction set defines only 9 instructions. The control functions are minimised treating the 7 undefined opcodes as don't-care conditions. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The minimised functions may assert a control signal for an undefined opcode, which is harmless only because a correct program can never fetch one.", "Undefined opcodes must be treated as 1 so that the AND plane stays consistent.", "Using don't-cares cannot change the number of product terms in any control function.", "Leaving the 7 decoder outputs unconnected forces every control function to be 0 for those opcodes."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A don't-care condition lets the minimiser choose either value for that input combination, so the resulting expression is free to be 1 there; the choice is only safe when the combination cannot occur.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each undefined opcode is an input combination that the specification does not constrain, so the truth table entry is X.</li><li>Karnaugh-map or Quine–McCluskey minimisation may then set that entry to 1 if doing so merges implicants, which yields a smaller network.</li><li>The minimised function is therefore not guaranteed to be 0 for those opcodes: the hardware has no defined behaviour for them.</li><li>That is acceptable precisely because a correct program never fetches an undefined opcode; a program that does reaches unspecified behaviour.</li><li>Option C is false because don't-cares often, but not always, reduce the product count. Option D is false because an unconnected input does not constrain the function that the minimiser derives.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">The minimised functions may assert a control signal for an undefined opcode (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading 'don't care' as 'must be 0'. It means 'either value is acceptable', which is exactly why the minimised hardware may behave arbitrarily on that opcode.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The signal MARin must be asserted in timing state T0 of every instruction, and in T3 only when the instruction is a memory-reference instruction, for which the decoder produces the signal MR. Which expression implements MARin?", "options": ["T0 · MR + T3", "(T0 + T3) · MR", "T0 · T3 + MR", "T0 + T3 · MR"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each clause of a control function is the AND of the state in which the signal is asserted with the instruction conditions under which it is asserted; the clauses are ORed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The T0 clause is unconditional, so it contributes the single term T0.</li><li>The T3 clause holds only for memory-reference instructions, so it contributes T3 · MR.</li><li>OR the two clauses: MARin = T0 + T3 · MR.</li><li>Check: in T0 the expression is 1 whatever MR is; in T3 it is 1 exactly when MR = 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\"><code class=\"mm\">T0 + T3 · MR</code> (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing T0 · MR, which drops the unconditional T0 clause, or T0 · T3, which asks for both states at once — impossible for a counter that is in one state at a time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a microinstruction format, which field supplies the address of the next microinstruction when control does not simply fall through to the next word?", "options": ["the control field", "the condition-select field", "the opcode field of the machine instruction", "the sequencing (next-address) field"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A microinstruction that can branch needs a sequencing field: a condition-select part names the flag to test, and a next-address part gives the target address.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The control field holds the bits that drive the datapath's control lines.</li><li>The condition-select field only chooses which status flag is tested; it carries no address.</li><li>The opcode field belongs to the machine instruction, not to the microinstruction.</li><li>The next-address (sequencing) field is the one loaded into the microprogram counter when the branch is taken.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the sequencing (next-address) field (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing the condition-select field with the address field. Select names the flag; the address field names the destination.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a microprogrammed control unit each microinstruction occupies exactly one clock cycle of 5 ns. A machine instruction is executed as 6 microinstructions, and a program executes 106 such instructions. What is the total control-store time in milliseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "30", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When every microinstruction takes one clock, the time for a machine instruction is the number of microinstructions times the clock period.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One microinstruction = 5 ns.</li><li>One machine instruction = 6 × 5 ns = 30 ns.</li><li>10<sup>6</sup> instructions = 10<sup>6</sup> × 30 ns = 30 × 10<sup>6</sup> ns.</li><li>30 × 10<sup>6</sup> ns = 30 × 10<sup>−3</sup> s = 30 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">30</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 5 ms by using one microinstruction instead of six, or 0.03 ms by treating the answer as seconds and forgetting the factor of 10<sup>3</sup> between ns and ms.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a microprogrammed control unit, what is the function of the mapping (microprogram) ROM placed between the instruction register and the control store?", "options": ["It holds the control signals for each timing state.", "It stores the condition flags used by conditional microinstructions.", "It translates the instruction opcode into the starting address of that instruction's microprogram.", "It sequences the T-states of the hardwired control network."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The opcode is not a control-store address, so a small ROM converts it into the address at which that instruction's microprogram begins.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At the start of every instruction the microprogram counter must be loaded with the first microinstruction of the routine that implements it.</li><li>The opcode is the ROM's address input; the ROM's data output is the corresponding starting address in the control store.</li><li>Control signals are held in the control store itself, and flags come from the status register, so options A and B describe other blocks.</li><li>Option D describes a hardwired sequencer, which does not exist in this design.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">It translates the instruction opcode into the starting address of that instruction's microprogram (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the opcode can address the control store directly. It cannot: the mapping ROM exists precisely to make that translation, which also lets several opcodes share one microprogram.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A microprogrammed control unit addresses its control store with a microprogram counter. Which of the following statements about that addressing and word format are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The mapping ROM lets two machine instructions with different opcodes share one microprogram, by mapping both opcodes to the same start address.", "The microprogram counter is loaded at the start of an instruction straight from the opcode, with no table in between.", "A microcode subroutine call needs a microprogram return-address stack, because one microprogram counter cannot remember where to return.", "The width of a control-store word is the sum of its control field, its condition-select field and its next-address field."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The control store is addressed by the microprogram counter, whose value comes from the mapping ROM at the start of an instruction and from the next-address field or the incrementer afterwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: the mapping ROM is a table, so two opcodes may point at the same entry and share the routine.</li><li>Option B is false: the opcode is not a control-store address; the mapping ROM stands between them, which is exactly what option A relies on.</li><li>Option C is true: with a single counter the return point must be saved somewhere, so microcoded calls use a small stack.</li><li>Option D is true: the three fields partition the word, so their widths add.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the opcode can address the control store directly, which would also make the sharing in option A impossible.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Two machines with the same clock period implement the same instruction set. Machine A uses horizontal microcode: 4 microinstructions per machine instruction, one clock each. Machine B uses vertical microcode: 2 microinstructions per machine instruction, but each microinstruction needs 1 clock plus 2 clocks of control decoding that cannot overlap. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["Machine A is faster: it needs 4 clocks against B's 6, so A's control path is 1.5 times faster.", "Machine B is faster because it uses fewer microinstructions.", "Both need 4 clocks per machine instruction.", "Machine B is faster: 2 clocks against A's 4."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Vertical microcode shortens the control word but lengthens the sequence: each microinstruction pays for decoding, so the comparison must be made in clocks per machine instruction, not in microinstruction count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Machine A: 4 microinstructions × 1 clock = 4 clocks per machine instruction.</li><li>Machine B: each microinstruction costs 1 + 2 = 3 clocks, and there are 2 of them, so 2 × 3 = 6 clocks.</li><li>Compare 4 with 6: A is faster, and the ratio is 6 / 4 = 1.5.</li><li>Option B counts microinstructions and ignores the decoding cost, which is the whole point of the trade-off.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Machine A is faster, 4 clocks against 6 — a factor of 1.5 (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Comparing the number of microinstructions (4 against 2) and concluding that B wins. Clocks per machine instruction decide speed, and vertical microcode pays extra clocks for decoding.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A conditional microinstruction carries a condition-select field that picks one of the machine flags. Which statement describes the sequencing correctly?", "options": ["The microprogram counter is loaded from the next-address field when the selected flag is true, and incremented when it is false.", "The microprogram counter is always loaded from the next-address field, whether the flag is true or false.", "The flag value is added to the next-address field to form the branch target.", "The flag is written into the control store so that a later microinstruction can test it."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A conditional branch in microcode multiplexes the microprogram counter's input: the next-address field when the tested flag is true, the incrementer when it is false.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The condition-select field feeds a multiplexer that picks one flag.</li><li>That flag drives a second multiplexer on the microprogram counter's input.</li><li>Flag true → the counter loads the next-address field, so control jumps.</li><li>Flag false → the counter takes the incrementer, so control falls through to the following microinstruction.</li><li>Option B removes the branch entirely; option C describes arithmetic that the datapath does not perform; option D confuses the flag with stored control.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">The microprogram counter is loaded from the next-address field when the flag is true and incremented when it is false (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing a conditional branch always jumps. The condition chooses between the next-address field and the increment path, so a false condition simply continues in sequence.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The function-select inputs of an ALU inside a CPU datapath are driven by", "options": ["the data bus", "the status register", "the program counter", "the control unit"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The ALU is a slave of the control unit: which operation it performs is decided by control signals, not by the data it is given.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The ALU operation is part of the instruction being executed, so it is decoded by the control unit.</li><li>The control unit places the operation code on the ALU's function-select inputs.</li><li>The data bus carries operands, the status register receives flags, and the program counter holds an address — none of them selects an operation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the control unit (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the opcode travels on the data bus into the ALU. The opcode is decoded into control signals; only the operands travel on the bus.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a single-bus datapath whose ALU has A and B input registers, which sequence executes ADD R1, R2, that is R1 ← R1 + R2?", "options": ["R1 → A; R2 → B; the ALU forms A + B; the result → R1", "R2 → A; R1 → B; the ALU forms A − B; the result → R1", "R1 → A; the ALU takes R2 straight from the bus in the same clock; the result → R1", "R2 → A; R1 → B; the result → R2"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A single bus carries one value per clock, so two operands cannot reach the ALU together; each must be latched into its own input register first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Clock 1: R1 is gated onto the bus and latched into A.</li><li>Clock 2: R2 is gated onto the bus and latched into B.</li><li>The ALU adds A and B, and the sum is gated onto the bus and latched into R1.</li><li>Option C is impossible on a single bus, and options B and D compute the wrong function or write the wrong destination.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">R1 → A; R2 → B; the ALU forms A + B; the result → R1 (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Wanting to fetch both operands in one clock. On a single bus that would require the bus to carry two values at once; that is exactly the cost a two-bus or three-bus datapath removes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about datapath structure and timing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The critical path through the datapath sets the minimum clock period.", "Inserting a pipeline register inside the datapath shortens the critical path but adds latency.", "A register file with only one read port can still supply both ALU operands in the same clock cycle.", "In a single-bus datapath, both ALU operands cannot be transferred in the same clock cycle."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period must exceed the longest combinational delay between two registers, and bus and port counts bound how many values can move per clock.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: the clock must be slow enough for the slowest register-to-register path to settle, so that path fixes the period.</li><li>Option B is true: splitting the path shortens each stage but the result now needs an extra cycle to cross the added register.</li><li>Option C is false: one read port delivers one value per cycle, so the second operand must wait.</li><li>Option D is true: a single bus carries one value at a time, so the operands are transferred in separate clocks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing a read port with a register file's capacity. A 32-register file with one read port can still deliver only one register's contents per cycle.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A 64-bit adder is built from 4-bit carry-lookahead blocks connected by ripple carry between blocks. Each block produces its carry-out 5 ns after its carry-in arrives, and each of its sum bits 2 ns after the block's carry-in arrives. What is the delay in nanoseconds from the arrival of the carry-in of block 1 to the final sum bit, bit 63?", "options": [], "answer_display": "77", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Lookahead removes the carry ripple inside a block but the carry still ripples between blocks, so the total delay is the sum of the inter-block carries plus the last block's sum delay.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>64 bits in 4-bit blocks gives 16 blocks.</li><li>The carry-in of block 1 arrives at t = 0; block k's carry-in arrives at (k − 1) × 5 ns.</li><li>Block 16's carry-in therefore arrives at 15 × 5 = 75 ns — fifteen block-to-block carries, not sixteen, because block 1's carry-in is the adder's own.</li><li>Bits 60–63 are block 16's sum bits, which appear 2 ns after that carry-in: 75 + 2 = 77 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">77</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using 16 carries and answering 16 × 5 + 2 = 82, or forgetting the final sum delay and answering 75. The carry-in of the first block is free; only the 15 carries between blocks cost time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which statement about the status flags produced by an n-bit ALU adder is TRUE?", "options": ["The carry flag and the overflow flag are equal for every addition.", "The zero flag can be derived from the carry flag alone.", "The overflow flag for signed addition is the XOR of the carry into and the carry out of the sign-bit position.", "The sign flag is the carry-out of the most significant bit."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Overflow means the signed result left the representable range, which happens exactly when the carry into the sign bit differs from the carry out of it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let c<sub>n−1</sub> be the carry into the sign position and c<sub>n</sub> the carry out of it.</li><li>Overflow = c<sub>n−1</sub> ⊕ c<sub>n</sub>: for example 0111 + 0001 gives c<sub>n−1</sub> = 1, c<sub>n</sub> = 0, so the sum 1000 is the wrong sign.</li><li>The carry flag is simply c<sub>n</sub>, the unsigned carry out, so the two flags are different quantities (option A is false).</li><li>The zero flag is the NOR of all result bits, and the sign flag is the most significant bit of the result, not the carry out of it (options B and D are false).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Overflow is the XOR of the carries into and out of the sign bit (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Equating carry with overflow. Unsigned addition overflows when there is a carry out; signed addition overflows when the carries into and out of the sign bit disagree.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A datapath path runs register → ALU → multiplexer → register. A register has a clk-to-Q delay of 2 ns and a setup time of 1 ns; the ALU takes 7 ns and the multiplexer 1.5 ns. Which statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The minimum clock period is 11.5 ns.", "Doubling the ALU delay to 14 ns exactly halves the maximum clock frequency.", "The maximum clock frequency is about 86.96 MHz.", "Inserting a pipeline register between the ALU and the multiplexer makes the minimum period 10 ns."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period must cover clk-to-Q of the source register, the combinational logic, and the setup time of the destination register.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Path delay = 2 + 7 + 1.5 + 1 = 11.5 ns, so option A is true.</li><li>Maximum frequency = 1 / 11.5 ns = 86.96 MHz, so option C is true.</li><li>With a register after the ALU, stage 1 = 2 + 7 + 1 = 10 ns and stage 2 = 2 + 1.5 + 1 = 4.5 ns; the period is the larger, 10 ns, so option D is true.</li><li>With a 14 ns ALU the path is 2 + 14 + 1.5 + 1 = 18.5 ns and the frequency is 54.05 MHz, a factor of 0.62 rather than 0.5, so option B is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Forgetting either the clk-to-Q delay or the setup time when adding up the path, or assuming frequency is inversely proportional to one component's delay. Only the whole path sets the period.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Compared with a single-bus datapath, what is the main advantage of a three-bus datapath?", "options": ["It needs fewer registers.", "Both ALU operands can be transferred in one clock, so an ALU instruction needs fewer clock cycles.", "It cannot perform a memory read, which simplifies the control.", "Its buses are wider, which shortens the ALU delay."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bus count bounds how many values can move per clock: with separate source buses both operands reach the ALU together, and a third bus returns the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On a single bus each operand needs its own clock to reach the ALU input registers, so an ADD takes three transfers.</li><li>With three buses the two source registers drive two buses at once and the result leaves on the third, so the operation completes in one clock.</li><li>Register count and memory capability are unaffected, and bus width is a design choice independent of bus count, so options A, C and D are wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Both ALU operands can be transferred in one clock, so fewer clock cycles are needed (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Thinking more buses make each transfer faster. They do not; they allow more transfers to happen concurrently, which cuts the number of cycles.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following is NOT carried on the control bus?", "options": ["the memory read and write strobes", "the address of the operand being fetched", "the interrupt request line", "the bus grant line"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A system bus is split by function: addresses travel on the address bus, data on the data bus, and timing and command signals on the control bus.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read and write strobes tell memory what to do, so they are control signals.</li><li>Interrupt requests and bus grants are requests between devices and the processor, so they are control signals too.</li><li>The address of the operand is carried on the address bus, which exists precisely to carry addresses.</li><li>Hence option B is the one that does not belong to the control bus.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the address of the operand being fetched (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Lumping every 'extra' wire into the control bus. Addresses and data have their own dedicated buses; control carries commands, timing and arbitration.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The number of distinct memory locations a processor can address directly is determined by", "options": ["the width of the data bus", "the width of the address bus", "the clock frequency of the bus", "the number of bus masters"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An address bus of n lines can carry 2<sup>n</sup> distinct patterns, so its width fixes the size of the address space.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each address line carries one bit of the address.</li><li>n lines therefore carry 2<sup>n</sup> distinct addresses, and that is the directly addressable space.</li><li>Data-bus width fixes how much is moved per transfer, not how many locations exist; clock frequency fixes transfer rate; the number of masters affects arbitration.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">the width of the address bus (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing address space with word size. A 32-bit data bus says nothing about how many locations exist; a 32-bit address bus says there are 2<sup>32</sup> of them.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A processor has a 24-bit address bus. What is the maximum number of distinct byte addresses it can generate?", "options": [], "answer_display": "16777216", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each address line doubles the number of distinguishable addresses, so an n-line address bus addresses 2<sup>n</sup> locations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The bus has 24 lines, so 2<sup>24</sup> patterns are possible.</li><li>2<sup>24</sup> = 16 777 216.</li><li>That is 16 MiB of byte-addressable memory.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">16777216</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Computing 24 × 2<sup>10</sup> = 24 576 or 2<sup>24</sup> / 1024 = 16 384. The address space is a power of two, not a multiple of the width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A synchronous bus has a 64-bit data path and a 100 MHz clock. Each non-burst transfer needs one clock for the address phase and one clock for the data phase. What is the effective bandwidth in MB/s, taking 1 MB = 106 bytes?", "options": [], "answer_display": "400", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective bandwidth is the bytes moved per transfer divided by the clocks a whole transfer occupies, times the clock rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One transfer moves 64 bits = 8 bytes.</li><li>One transfer occupies 1 address clock + 1 data clock = 2 clocks.</li><li>So the bus moves 8 / 2 = 4 bytes per clock.</li><li>At 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> clocks per second: 4 × 100 × 10<sup>6</sup> = 400 × 10<sup>6</sup> bytes/s = 400 MB/s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">400</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 800 MB/s by dividing the data-path width by the clock alone. The address phase is bus time too, and it halves the useful rate here.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An asynchronous bus uses full handshaking: the master raises Request, the slave answers Acknowledge, data is transferred, then Request and Acknowledge are withdrawn. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The transfer rate is fixed by a clock, so a slow slave must insert wait states.", "Full handshaking cannot detect the absence of a slave device.", "The master must withdraw Request before placing data on the bus.", "Each transfer takes as long as the two devices' response delays require, so a slow slave simply stretches the transfer and no wait-state logic is needed."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Handshaking replaces a shared clock with a request/acknowledge dialogue, so the transfer time adapts to the devices taking part.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>No common clock exists; the master waits for the slave's Acknowledge before completing the cycle.</li><li>A slow slave delays its Acknowledge, and the cycle stretches by exactly that much.</li><li>There is therefore nothing for wait-state logic to do: the protocol already accommodates any speed.</li><li>Option A describes a synchronous bus. Option B is false because a missing slave never acknowledges, which the master detects as a timeout or an error. Option C reverses the protocol: data is placed before Request is withdrawn.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Each transfer takes as long as the devices' response delays require, so no wait states are needed (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Carrying synchronous thinking into an asynchronous design. Wait states are a synchronous-bus device; an asynchronous bus absorbs the delay in the handshake itself.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A synchronous bus moves one 64-bit word per data phase. Which of the following statements about how the address phase, bursts and wait states affect throughput are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In a burst transfer one address phase serves several data phases, so the effective bandwidth approaches the data-path limit.", "In a non-burst transfer every word repeats the address phase, so the useful fraction of bus time is the data clocks divided by the data plus address clocks.", "A wait state adds a clock to a transfer, so it lowers the effective bandwidth without changing the data-path width.", "Because the address phase and the data phase use different groups of wires, they can always overlap in a non-burst transfer."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective bandwidth is bytes per transfer divided by the clocks a whole transfer occupies, so any clock spent not carrying data lowers it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option A is true: amortising one address phase over many data phases leaves almost every clock carrying data.</li><li>Option B is true: with one data clock and one address clock the useful fraction is 1/2, which is why the non-burst rate is half the data-path limit.</li><li>Option C is true: a wait state is an extra clock in the denominator, so the rate falls while the width is untouched.</li><li>Option D is false: a non-burst transfer uses the address wires and then the data wires, one after the other, because the slave cannot know which word is wanted until the address has been received.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming separate wire groups mean concurrent phases. The dependency, not the wiring, forces the order: the address must arrive before the data can be selected.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A direct-mapped cache holds 4 KB of data and uses 16-byte blocks. Main-memory block number 1500 maps to which cache line?", "options": ["220", "232", "108", "44"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A direct-mapped cache has one line per set, so the line number is the memory block number reduced modulo the number of lines. Only the low bits of the block number select a line; the remaining high bits become the tag.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of lines = cache size / block size = 4096 B / 16 B = 256.</li><li>So the line index is <code class=\"mm\">block mod 256</code>, and 256 = 2<sup>8</sup>, so the low 8 bits of the block number select the line.</li><li>1500 = 5 &times; 256 + 220, so 1500 mod 256 = 220.</li><li>Block 1500 therefore occupies line 220, and its tag is 5 (the quotient).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 220 &mdash; line 220.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The usual slip is to take the byte address modulo the number of lines instead of the block number, or to use 128 lines by forgetting that a 4 KB cache with 16-byte blocks holds 256 blocks. Both give a line that is nowhere near 220.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Line = block number mod (cache size / block size); the quotient is the tag.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 32-bit byte-addressed machine has a 16 KB four-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. How many bits are required for the tag?", "options": ["19", "21", "20", "12"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A physical address splits into three fields: tag, index (set number) and block offset. The offset is <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub>(block size)</code>, the index is <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub>(number of sets)</code>, and the tag takes whatever is left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Block offset = log<sub>2</sub> 32 = 5 bits.</li><li>Number of blocks = 16 KB / 32 B = 512; with 4 ways, number of sets = 512 / 4 = 128, so index = log<sub>2</sub> 128 = 7 bits.</li><li>Tag = 32 &minus; 5 &minus; 7 = 20 bits.</li><li>Check the set count from capacity directly: 16 384 / (32 &times; 4) = 128 sets, which agrees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The common error is to divide the capacity by the block size and stop there, treating 512 blocks as 512 sets; that gives a 9-bit index and a tag of 18. Associativity divides the set count, so the index shrinks by <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub>(ways)</code> and the tag grows by the same two bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> tag = address bits &minus; log<sub>2</sub>(block size) &minus; log<sub>2</sub>(capacity / (block size &times; ways)).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 2-way set-associative cache has 64 sets. A program accesses memory blocks 0, 64, 0, 64, 0, 64, 0, 64 in that order, starting with an empty cache. How many of these eight accesses are hits?", "options": ["2", "6", "4", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a set-associative cache the block number selects the set, <code class=\"mm\">set = block mod (number of sets)</code>, and any of the ways in that set may hold the block. Associativity decides how many blocks that share a set can be resident at once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>0 mod 64 = 0 and 64 mod 64 = 0, so both blocks map to set 0.</li><li>A 2-way set holds two blocks, so blocks 0 and 64 can be resident together.</li><li>Access 1 (block 0) misses and fills way 1; access 2 (block 64) misses and fills way 2.</li><li>Accesses 3 to 8 find the requested block already in the set, so each of the six is a hit.</li><li>Hits = 6, misses = 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6 hits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A frequent answer is 2 or 0 because the reader assumes the two blocks evict each other. That is exactly what a direct-mapped cache does here &mdash; both blocks map to line 0 and every access misses, giving 0 hits &mdash; but the second way removes the conflict, which is the point of associativity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two blocks that share a set conflict only when the number of blocks competing exceeds the number of ways.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A fully associative cache has 128 lines. How many tag comparisons must the cache perform in parallel to decide whether a block is present?", "options": ["1", "7", "32", "128"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A fully associative cache has a single set, so there is no index field at all: the address is only a tag plus a block offset. Presence of a block is therefore decided by comparing the requested tag against the tag of every line simultaneously.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of sets = 1, so the index needs log<sub>2</sub> 1 = 0 bits.</li><li>Every one of the 128 lines can hold the block, so all 128 lines must be searched.</li><li>The search is done in parallel by 128 tag comparators, and a hit is the OR of their outputs.</li><li>That parallel comparator bank is why fully associative caches are built small &mdash; the cost grows linearly with the number of lines.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 128 comparators.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 7 is the trap: it is log<sub>2</sub> 128, the number of index bits a set-associative cache would need, not the number of comparisons. In a fully associative cache there is no index, so nothing selects one line before the comparison.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fully associative means 0 index bits and one comparator per line.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache is changed from direct-mapped to 4-way set-associative with the same total capacity and the same block size. Which one of the following is a direct consequence?", "options": ["Conflict misses can fall, while the number of capacity misses is unchanged.", "Capacity misses fall, because four blocks can now share one set.", "Compulsory misses fall, because the first access to a block can be served from a sibling way.", "Hit time falls, because fewer tag comparisons are needed per access."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Misses are classified by cause. Compulsory misses are the first reference to a block and depend only on the reference stream. Capacity misses are the blocks a cache of that size cannot hold however it is organised. Conflict misses are blocks evicted only because too many of them map to the same set. Associativity attacks the third class alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Total capacity and block size are fixed, so the number of blocks the cache can hold is unchanged and capacity misses cannot change.</li><li>Each block is still seen for the first time once, so compulsory misses cannot change either.</li><li>With 4 ways, up to four blocks that share a set can be resident, so blocks that a direct-mapped cache would have evicted on a conflict now survive: conflict misses can fall.</li><li>Comparing four tags in parallel is slower than comparing one, so hit time rises slightly &mdash; it does not fall.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; conflict misses can fall, capacity misses are unchanged.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the standard error: associativity does not create space, it only re-allocates the freedom to place a block. Option (D) inverts the real cost &mdash; associativity buys miss rate at the price of hit time and comparator hardware.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Associativity trades hit time for conflict misses; it does not touch capacity or compulsory misses.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 32 KB four-way set-associative cache uses 64-byte blocks. The byte address 0x00002A40 is presented to it. Which set does the address map to?", "options": ["169", "64", "41", "19"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The set is selected by the block number, not by the byte address. Convert the address to a block number by dropping the offset bits, then reduce modulo the number of sets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Block offset = log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits, so the block number is the address shifted right by 6.</li><li>0x00002A40 = 10 816 in decimal; 10 816 / 64 = 169, so the block number is 169.</li><li>Number of sets = 32 768 / (64 &times; 4) = 128.</li><li>Set = 169 mod 128 = 41.</li><li>Equivalently: the index field is bits 6 to 12 of the address, and 10 816 = 0b10_1010_0100_0000 gives index bits 1010101 = 85? &mdash; no: bits 6..12 are 0b0101001 = 41, which agrees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 41 &mdash; set 41.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 169 is the block number, which is the answer only if you forget to reduce modulo the set count. 64 is the byte address reduced modulo 128, which mixes the offset into the index. Associativity is what makes the set count 128 rather than 512; using 512 sets would leave the block number unchanged at 169.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> set = (address / block size) mod (capacity / (block size &times; ways)).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle, a miss rate of 4% and a miss penalty of 50 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["2.0 cycles", "3.0 cycles", "3.5 cycles", "5.0 cycles"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Average memory access time is the hit time plus the expected extra cost of a miss: <code class=\"mm\">AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss rate = 0.04, miss penalty = 50 cycles.</li><li>Average miss cost = 0.04 &times; 50 = 2 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 1 + 2 = 3 cycles.</li><li>Read as a weighted average: 96% of accesses cost 1 cycle and 4% cost 1 + 50 = 51 cycles, giving 0.96 &times; 1 + 0.04 &times; 51 = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.0 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 2.0 cycles is the average miss cost alone; the hit time must be added. 5.0 cycles comes from multiplying the hit time by the miss rate instead of using the hit rate, which double-counts the miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty; the weighted-average form gives the same number.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "An L1 cache has a hit time of 1 cycle and a local miss rate of 5%. Its misses go to an L2 cache with a hit time of 10 cycles and a local miss rate of 20%; L2 misses go to main memory, which costs 100 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["1.5 cycles", "2.0 cycles", "2.3 cycles", "2.5 cycles"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a two-level hierarchy the L1 miss penalty is itself an average access time for the lower level. Local miss rates multiply down the hierarchy; the cost of an L1 miss is <code class=\"mm\">L2 hit time + (L2 local miss rate &times; memory cost)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost of an L1 miss = L2 hit time + L2 local miss rate &times; memory cost = 10 + 0.20 &times; 100 = 10 + 20 = 30 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = L1 hit time + L1 local miss rate &times; (cost of an L1 miss) = 1 + 0.05 &times; 30.</li><li>0.05 &times; 30 = 1.5, so AMAT = 2.5 cycles.</li><li>The global miss rate to memory is 0.05 &times; 0.20 = 1%, so 1% of accesses cost 1 + 30 + 100 = 131 cycles; the weighted average 0.99 &times; 1 + 0.01 &times; 131 = 2.3 &mdash; which is not AMAT, because an access that hits in L2 also pays the 30 cycles. Using the local form as above is the correct bookkeeping.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.5 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 2.3 cycles is the trap: it applies the global 1% rate to the full three-level path and forgets that the 5% of accesses that miss L1 but hit L2 also pay for the L2 access. Never mix a global miss rate with a local penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT<sub>L1</sub> = t<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>1</sub> &times; (t<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> &times; t<sub>mem</sub>), with local miss rates.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A processor has a base CPI of 2.0 for a perfect cache. Each instruction makes 1.5 memory accesses on average, the cache miss rate is 3% and the miss penalty is 80 cycles. What is the CPI including memory stalls?", "options": ["5.6", "3.6", "2.6", "4.4"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Memory stalls are counted per instruction, not per access: <code class=\"mm\">stall cycles per instruction = memory accesses per instruction &times; miss rate &times; miss penalty</code>, and this is added to the base CPI.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Misses per instruction = 1.5 &times; 0.03 = 0.045.</li><li>Stall cycles per instruction = 0.045 &times; 80 = 3.6.</li><li>CPI = base CPI + stall CPI = 2.0 + 3.6 = 5.6.</li><li>Sanity check: about 4.5 instructions in every 100 each lose 80 cycles, which is 3.6 cycles per instruction on average.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 3.6 is the stall component alone and omits the base CPI; 2.6 comes from forgetting that each instruction makes 1.5 accesses and using 0.03 &times; 80 = 2.4 with a slip. The number of memory accesses per instruction is the factor most often dropped.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CPI = CPI<sub>base</sub> + (accesses per instruction) &times; miss rate &times; miss penalty.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 4-way set-associative L1 has a hit time of 2 cycles and a local miss rate of 3%. Its L2 is unified, with a hit time of 12 cycles and a local miss rate of 25%; main memory costs 120 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["3.02 cycles", "3.14 cycles", "3.26 cycles", "4.26 cycles"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nest the levels: the L1 miss penalty is the average time to service a miss at L2, which is the L2 hit time plus the L2 local miss rate times the memory cost.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost of an L1 miss = 12 + 0.25 &times; 120 = 12 + 30 = 42 cycles.</li><li>L1 miss contribution = 0.03 &times; 42 = 1.26 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 2 + 1.26 = 3.26 cycles.</li><li>Cross-check with the global miss rate: 0.03 &times; 0.25 = 0.75% of accesses go to memory and cost 2 + 42 + 120 = 164 cycles, and 2.25% miss L1 but hit L2 and cost 44 cycles: 0.97 &times; 2 + 0.0225 &times; 44 + 0.0075 &times; 164 = 1.94 + 0.99 + 1.23 = 4.16 &mdash; this over-counts because a memory access already includes the L2 time; the nested form is the correct one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.26 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 3.02 comes from dropping the associativity penalty and starting from 1 cycle. 4.26 comes from applying the 3% rate to memory cost alone (0.03 &times; 120 = 3.6) and ignoring that most L1 misses are served by L2. The L2 local miss rate of 25% applies only to the 3% of accesses that reach L2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT = t<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>1</sub>(t<sub>2</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> t<sub>mem</sub>).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A fully associative cache with 3 lines is initially empty and uses LRU replacement. The reference string is 1, 2, 3, 1, 4. How many hits occur?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LRU evicts the block whose most recent use is furthest in the past, so every hit refreshes a block's recency and pushes it to the most-recently-used end.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1 &rarr; miss, cache {1}; LRU order 1.</li><li>2 &rarr; miss, cache {1, 2}; order 1, 2.</li><li>3 &rarr; miss, cache {1, 2, 3}; order 1, 2, 3.</li><li>1 &rarr; hit; order becomes 2, 3, 1.</li><li>4 &rarr; miss, victim is 2 (least recently used); cache {3, 1, 4}.</li><li>Hits = 1, misses = 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 hit.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 0 hits comes from evicting 1 on the fourth access, which is what FIFO would do (it evicts the oldest arrival, 1). LRU keeps 1 because it was just used &mdash; the whole difference between the two policies is whether recency or arrival order decides.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> On a hit, LRU moves the block to the MRU end; FIFO ignores hits entirely.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache with 3 frames uses LRU replacement. For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, how many hits occur?", "options": ["5", "4", "3", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Run LRU frame by frame, keeping the frames ordered from least to most recently used and evicting from the left on a miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1 M: {1}.</li><li>2 M: {1,2}. 3 M: {1,2,3}.</li><li>4 M: evict 1, {2,3,4}. 1 M: evict 2, {3,4,1}. 2 M: evict 3, {4,1,2}.</li><li>5 M: evict 4, {1,2,5}.</li><li>1 H: {2,5,1}. 2 H: {5,1,2}.</li><li>3 M: evict 5, {1,2,3}. 4 M: evict 1, {2,3,4}. 5 M: evict 2, {3,4,5}.</li><li>Misses = 10, hits = 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 hits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 5 is the optimal (MIN) hit count for this string and 4 is what LRU achieves with four frames; neither is the 3-frame LRU result. Reading the string left to right and refreshing recency on every hit is what produces 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For this string the hit counts are LRU 2, FIFO 3 and optimal 5 with three frames.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, a cache using FIFO replacement produces 9 misses with 3 frames. How many misses does it produce with 4 frames?", "options": ["10", "9", "8", "11"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FIFO evicts the block that has been in the cache longest, regardless of use. It is not a stack algorithm, so a larger cache does not guarantee a subset relation between the two contents, and the miss count can rise &mdash; this is Belady's anomaly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With 4 frames: 1 M {1}, 2 M {1,2}, 3 M {1,2,3}, 4 M {1,2,3,4}.</li><li>1 H, 2 H.</li><li>5 M, evict 1: {2,3,4,5}. 1 M, evict 2: {3,4,5,1}. 2 M, evict 3: {4,5,1,2}.</li><li>3 M, evict 4: {5,1,2,3}. 4 M, evict 5: {1,2,3,4}. 5 M, evict 1: {2,3,4,5}.</li><li>Misses = 10, hits = 2 &mdash; one more miss than with three frames.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 misses.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 8 or 9 assumes the miss count must fall as the cache grows. That monotonicity holds for stack algorithms such as LRU and optimal, not for FIFO. The anomaly is real and this string is the standard witness.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Belady's anomaly: FIFO with more frames can miss more; LRU and MIN cannot.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following statements about write policies is correct?", "options": ["A write-back cache needs no dirty bit, because every store is sent to main memory immediately.", "A write-through cache cannot use write-allocate, because the block is already on its way to memory.", "A write-back cache sets a dirty bit on a store, so a block is written to memory only if it has been modified.", "A write-back cache sends every store to main memory and leaves the cached copy unchanged."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The write policy decides when main memory is updated. Write-through updates memory on every store and keeps the cache clean; write-back updates memory only when a modified block is evicted, which requires one extra state bit per line, the dirty bit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A) describes write-through, not write-back, and its reason is backwards: write-back exists precisely so that memory is not written on every store.</li><li>Write-through and write-allocate are independent choices; a write-through write-allocate cache is a common design.</li><li>Write-back marks the line dirty on the first store to it and writes the block back on eviction, so an unmodified block never costs a memory write.</li><li>Option (D) again describes write-through while keeping the cache stale, which is simply a wrong cache.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The frequent confusion is to think the dirty bit is an optimisation that can be dropped. Without it the cache cannot tell a clean block from a modified one and must write every evicted block back, which turns write-back into the worst of both policies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dirty bit = one bit per line that licenses write-back on eviction.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache uses write-back with write-allocate. A loop executes 800 one-word stores to addresses that all lie in the same 64-byte block. The block is not in the cache when the loop starts, and it is evicted exactly once, after the loop ends. How many main-memory write operations does the cache perform?", "options": ["800", "1", "0", "100"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-allocate fetches the block on the first write miss; write-back then absorbs all later stores in the cache and writes the block to memory once, on eviction, provided it is dirty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>First store: miss. Write-allocate brings the block in (one memory read) and applies the store, marking the line dirty.</li><li>The remaining 799 stores hit the line in the cache; each updates the cached copy and leaves the dirty bit set.</li><li>On eviction the dirty line is written back once.</li><li>Main-memory writes = 1. (Memory reads = 1, so total block transfers = 2.)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 write.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 800 is the write-through answer, and it is the number to compare against: the same loop under write-through with no write-allocate would issue 800 memory writes. 0 ignores the write-back at eviction, which would leave memory stale forever.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-back converts N stores to one block into 1 write-back, at the price of a dirty bit and a possible stale memory copy.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache mapping are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A fully associative cache has no index field, so a lookup compares the tag against every line.", "In a direct-mapped cache each memory block can occupy exactly one line.", "For a fixed capacity and block size, a 4-way set-associative cache has four times as many sets as a direct-mapped cache.", "Two blocks that map to different lines in a direct-mapped cache must also map to different sets in a 2-way cache of the same total size."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Mapping is decided by how the address bits are split. Direct-mapped: no choice of line. Fully associative: no index, so the whole cache is one set. Set-associative: the index picks a set and the ways give the choice within it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: with one set there is nothing to index, so every line's tag is compared in parallel.</li><li>(B) is true: the index is a function of the block number, so a block has one and only one home line.</li><li>(C) is false: associativity divides the block count into sets. With 512 blocks, direct-mapped has 512 sets and 4-way has 128 sets &mdash; a quarter, not four times.</li><li>(D) is false: a 2-way cache of the same size has half as many sets, so blocks that differed by the direct-mapped line count can now collide. Blocks i and i + 256 in a 512-line direct-mapped cache are in different lines but share a set in a 2-way cache with 256 sets.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the direction error &mdash; more ways means fewer sets for a fixed capacity. Option (D) is the associativity trade-off in disguise: halving the set count is exactly what creates the conflict that the second way then resolves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle, a miss rate of 5% and a miss penalty of 40 cycles, so its AMAT is 3 cycles. Which of the following changes, each applied on its own, would reduce the AMAT below 3 cycles? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Halving the miss rate to 2.5%.", "Halving the miss penalty to 20 cycles.", "Halving the hit time to 0.5 cycle, while the miss rate rises to 6%.", "Raising the miss penalty to 50 cycles."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. Any change must be evaluated in that formula, because a change in one factor can be cancelled by the way it moves another.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Baseline: 1 + 0.05 &times; 40 = 3 cycles.</li><li>(A) 1 + 0.025 &times; 40 = 1 + 1 = 2 cycles &mdash; reduced.</li><li>(B) 1 + 0.05 &times; 20 = 1 + 1 = 2 cycles &mdash; reduced.</li><li>(C) 0.5 + 0.06 &times; 40 = 0.5 + 2.4 = 2.9 cycles &mdash; reduced, but only marginally.</li><li>(D) 1 + 0.05 &times; 50 = 3.5 cycles &mdash; increased, so it does not qualify.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the one usually missed: halving the hit time while the miss rate worsens still helps here (2.9 against 3), but the margin is thin and it would reverse if the miss rate rose past 6.25%. Option (D) is included so that 'any change in the penalty helps' is not assumed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A change helps only if the new AMAT, computed in full, is smaller.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache replacement are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["LRU is a stack algorithm and therefore never exhibits Belady's anomaly.", "FIFO can exhibit Belady's anomaly: a larger cache may produce more misses on the same reference string.", "Optimal (MIN) replacement can be implemented with a fixed, small amount of per-line state.", "For a fixed reference string, LRU with n+1 frames never has more misses than LRU with n frames."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A replacement policy is a stack algorithm when the contents of a cache of size n are always a subset of the contents of a cache of size n+1 for the same reference string. Stack algorithms have monotone miss counts; non-stack algorithms need not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the LRU stack property makes the LRU contents at n frames a subset of those at n+1 frames, so misses can only fall as frames are added.</li><li>(B) is true: FIFO is not a stack algorithm. The standard witness string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 gives 9 misses with 3 frames and 10 with 4.</li><li>(C) is false: optimal replacement needs the future reference string, which the hardware cannot know; it is a bound used for evaluation, not an implementation.</li><li>(D) is true and is exactly the stack property stated as a monotonicity claim.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the trap: optimal replacement is easy to compute offline and impossible online. Confusing 'computable in a simulation' with 'implementable in hardware' is the standard slip, and it is why real caches approximate LRU with clock or pseudo-LRU rather than trying to be optimal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Stack algorithms (LRU, MIN) are monotone in cache size; FIFO is not.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about write policies are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["With write-allocate, a store that misses first fetches the block into the cache and then applies the store.", "A write-through cache with no write-allocate issues one main-memory write for every store that misses.", "A block that is resident and clean in a write-back cache may hold data newer than main memory.", "Write-back generally reduces main-memory traffic when a workload rewrites the same block many times."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent decisions make up a write policy. The update policy says when memory is written (write-through or write-back); the allocation policy says whether a write miss brings the block in (write-allocate or no-write-allocate). Write-back also needs a dirty bit to know which clean blocks may be discarded silently.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: write-allocate brings the block in on a write miss, which is what makes the next store to the same block a hit.</li><li>(B) is true: with no write-allocate the store is not cached, so it is passed straight to memory &mdash; one write per missing store.</li><li>(C) is false: a clean block is by definition identical to memory, so it cannot be newer. Only a dirty block can differ from memory, and that is precisely what the dirty bit records.</li><li>(D) is true: repeated stores to one block collapse into a single write-back at eviction, whereas write-through issues one write per store.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) reverses the meaning of clean: 'clean' means the cached copy equals the memory copy, so memory is never stale for a clean block. The same sentence with 'dirty' in place of 'clean' would be true.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-through/back is about when memory is written; write-allocate/no-write-allocate is about whether a write miss fetches the block.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about block size and locality are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A larger block exploits spatial locality, because neighbouring words are fetched along with the requested word.", "Increasing the block size always reduces the miss rate.", "Temporal locality means that a block used recently is likely to be used again soon.", "For a fixed memory bandwidth, a larger block increases the miss penalty, because more bytes must be transferred."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Block size trades three quantities against each other: the number of blocks a cache of fixed capacity can hold, the fraction of a fetched block that is actually used, and the time to move a block from memory.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: fetching 64 bytes when 4 are requested is a bet on spatial locality, and it pays off when the neighbours are used.</li><li>(B) is false: as blocks grow, the number of blocks a fixed-capacity cache can hold falls, and blocks fetched for one word may be evicted before their neighbours are used. The miss rate falls and then rises, so there is an optimum block size.</li><li>(C) is true: that is the definition of temporal locality, and it is what makes a cache of any size useful.</li><li>(D) is true: the miss penalty is latency plus transfer time, and transfer time grows with the number of bytes moved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the trap: 'more is better' is false because block size steals capacity. The usual optimum on real machines is a few tens of bytes, and past it the miss rate climbs again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Miss rate against block size is U-shaped, not monotone.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 256 KB cache uses 64-byte blocks and is 8-way set-associative. How many sets does the cache have?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "512", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The set count is the block count divided by the associativity: <code class=\"mm\">sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of blocks = 256 KB / 64 B = 262 144 / 64 = 4096.</li><li>Number of sets = 4096 / 8 = 512.</li><li>Equivalently 262 144 / (64 &times; 8) = 262 144 / 512 = 512.</li><li>As a check, the index field is log<sub>2</sub> 512 = 9 bits and the offset is log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits, leaving 32 &minus; 15 = 17 tag bits on a 32-bit machine.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 512 sets.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 4096 is the number of blocks, not sets; 8-way associativity divides it. The other slip is to use 1024 for 1 KB and divide by 4, which comes from a 256 Kb reading of the capacity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> sets = blocks / ways; blocks = capacity / block size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle, a miss rate of 2.5% and a miss penalty of 80 cycles. What is the average memory access time, in cycles?(Enter a number.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. The miss rate must be used as a fraction, so 2.5% is 0.025.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss rate = 0.025, miss penalty = 80 cycles.</li><li>Average miss cost = 0.025 &times; 80 = 2 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 1 + 2 = 3 cycles.</li><li>Weighted-average check: 97.5% of accesses cost 1 cycle and 2.5% cost 81, giving 0.975 + 2.025 = 3.0.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 2.5% as 2.5 instead of 0.025 gives 201 cycles, and reading it as 0.25 gives 21 cycles. The other common slip is to drop the hit time and answer 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Convert every percentage to a fraction before multiplying.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A fully associative cache with 3 frames starts empty. For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, what is the minimum number of misses that any replacement policy can achieve?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The minimum over all policies is achieved by optimal (MIN, Belady) replacement: on a miss, evict the block whose next use is furthest in the future, or one that is never used again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1 M {1}; 2 M {1,2}; 3 M {1,2,3}.</li><li>4 M: 1 is used next at position 5, 2 at 6, 3 at 10, so evict 3 &mdash; {1,2,4}.</li><li>1 H, 2 H.</li><li>5 M: 1 next at 8, 2 at 9, 4 at 11, so evict 4 &mdash; {1,2,5}.</li><li>1 H, 2 H.</li><li>3 M: 1 next at 8, 2 at 9, 5 at 12 &mdash; all future uses are ahead, so evict the furthest, 5 &mdash; {1,2,3}.</li><li>4 M: 1 and 2 are never used again, so evict 1 &mdash; {2,3,4}.</li><li>5 M: 2 is never used again &mdash; evict 2, {3,4,5}.</li><li>Misses = 7, hits = 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7 misses.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 10 is the LRU count for three frames and 9 the FIFO count; both are achievable by real policies but neither is the minimum. Reporting a policy's result as the minimum is the standard error here &mdash; MIN is an offline bound that no online policy can match in general.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Optimal replacement gives the lower bound on misses; LRU and FIFO sit above it.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A write-back, write-allocate cache serves a program that performs 1000 four-byte stores, all falling in 16 distinct 64-byte blocks that are rewritten many times. None of the 16 blocks is in the cache at the start, and all 16 are evicted at the end. How many block write-backs to main memory occur?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-back writes a block to memory when the block is evicted and only if it is dirty. Every block that received at least one store is dirty, so the number of write-backs equals the number of distinct dirty blocks that are evicted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The 1000 stores touch 16 distinct blocks, so at most 16 blocks become dirty.</li><li>Each block receives at least one store (the stores are spread over all 16), so all 16 are dirty.</li><li>A dirty block is written back once, at eviction, no matter how many times it was modified in the cache.</li><li>Write-backs = 16.</li><li>For contrast, a write-through cache with no write-allocate would issue 1000 memory writes, one per store.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 block write-backs.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 1000 is the write-through answer. Answering 1000 / 16 = 63 assumes a write-back per store rather than per block. The whole benefit of write-back is that the write-back count depends on the number of distinct dirty blocks, not on the number of stores.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-backs = number of distinct dirty blocks evicted.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A 32-bit byte-addressed machine has a 16 KB four-way set-associative write-back cache with 64-byte blocks. Each line stores its data, one tag, one valid bit and one dirty bit. How many bits of storage does the whole cache need, including data, tags, valid bits and dirty bits?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "136704", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The storage of a cache is per line: the data array holds block size &times; 8 bits, and the tag array holds the tag width plus one valid bit and, for write-back, one dirty bit. The total is lines &times; bits per line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of lines = 16 384 / 64 = 256.</li><li>Number of sets = 256 / 4 = 64, so the index is log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits.</li><li>Block offset = log<sub>2</sub> 64 = 6 bits.</li><li>Tag width = 32 &minus; 6 &minus; 6 = 20 bits.</li><li>Bits per line = 64 &times; 8 data + 20 tag + 1 valid + 1 dirty = 512 + 22 = 534.</li><li>Total = 256 &times; 534 = 136 704 bits, which is 16.68 KB of storage for 16 KB of data.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 136704 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 131 072 is the data alone (16 KB). Forgetting the dirty bit gives 256 &times; 533 = 136 448; forgetting both bits gives 136 192; computing the tag as 32 &minus; 6 &minus; 8 (using 256 sets as if the cache were direct-mapped) gives 18 tag bits and 256 &times; 532 = 136 192. The associativity is what sets the index at 6 bits, not 8.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> total bits = lines &times; (block size &times; 8 + tag + valid + dirty).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 64 KB direct-mapped cache uses 32-byte blocks on a 32-bit byte-addressed machine. How many bits of the address form the cache index?", "options": ["10", "12", "11", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The index field selects one line, so its width is the base-2 logarithm of the number of lines. The number of lines is the cache capacity divided by the block size, and the offset field is the logarithm of the block size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of lines = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048.</li><li>Index bits = log<sub>2</sub>(2048) = <b>11</b>.</li><li>Offset bits = log<sub>2</sub>(32) = 5, so the tag takes the remaining 32 &minus; 11 &minus; 5 = 16 bits.</li><li>Check the split: 16 tag + 11 index + 5 offset = 32 bits, which accounts for the whole address.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 index bits</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5 by reading the block size as the index, or answering 12 by dividing the capacity by 32 twice. The index counts lines, not bytes and not blocks of memory.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the three field widths in the order offset, index, tag, and check that they add up to the address width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 16-way set-associative cache has 512 sets and 32-byte blocks. What is the data capacity of the cache?", "options": ["256 KB", "16 KB", "128 KB", "512 KB"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A set-associative cache stores one block per way, so the data capacity is (number of sets) &times; (ways) &times; (block size). Associativity adds blocks per set without changing the set count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Data per set = 16 ways &times; 32 B = 512 B.</li><li>Total data = 512 sets &times; 512 B = 262144 B.</li><li>262144 B / 1024 = <b>256 KB</b>.</li><li>Check the index field: log<sub>2</sub>(512) = 9 index bits and log<sub>2</sub>(32) = 5 offset bits, so the tag is 32 &minus; 14 = 18 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 256 KB</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the associativity gives 512 &times; 32 B = 16 KB, which is the capacity of a direct-mapped cache with the same 512 lines. Every way holds its own block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Capacity = sets &times; ways &times; block size. In a direct-mapped cache the number of ways is 1, which is why the formula collapses to lines &times; block size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 32 KB direct-mapped cache with 16-byte blocks serves a machine with a 32-bit byte address and 4 GB of physical memory. How many distinct main-memory blocks map to any one cache line?", "options": ["65536", "262144", "2048", "131072"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a direct-mapped cache each line is claimed by every memory block whose index bits equal that line's number. The count is therefore (total memory blocks) / (number of lines), and the index bits are the only address bits a line ignores.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Lines in the cache = 32768 / 16 = 2048 = 2<sup>11</sup>.</li><li>Total memory blocks = 2<sup>32</sup> / 16 = 2<sup>28</sup> = 268435456.</li><li>Blocks per line = 2<sup>28</sup> / 2<sup>11</sup> = 2<sup>17</sup> = <b>131072</b>.</li><li>Cross-check with the tag: 17 tag bits, and 2<sup>17</sup> distinct tag values can share one line.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 131072 blocks per line</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 2048, the number of lines, or 65536 by using 2<sup>16</sup>. The tag width, not the line count, gives the number of blocks competing for a line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every line has 2<sup>tag bits</sup> claimants. Compute the tag width once and the answer follows.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 2-way set-associative cache has 256 sets and 16-byte blocks on a 32-bit byte-addressed machine. Which bits of the address form the set index?", "options": ["bits 3 to 10", "bits 4 to 11", "bits 5 to 12", "bits 12 to 19"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The address is cut from the least significant end: the offset occupies the lowest bits, the index the next bits, and the tag the highest. The offset width comes from the block size alone, and the index width from the set count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Offset = log<sub>2</sub>(16) = 4 bits, occupying bits 0 to 3.</li><li>Index = log<sub>2</sub>(256) = 8 bits, occupying the next eight positions, bits 4 to 11.</li><li>Tag = 32 &minus; 4 &minus; 8 = 20 bits, occupying bits 12 to 31.</li><li>The associativity does not enter the split: 2 ways change the tag width, not the index width.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: bits 4 to 11</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Starting the index at bit 0 ignores the offset, and using 3 offset bits (option A) is the rule for 8-byte blocks. Associativity is also often wrongly subtracted from the index width.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix offset first, then index, then give the rest to the tag. Only the block size and the set count matter.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 2 cycles, a miss rate of 5% and a miss penalty of 60 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["5 cycles", "4 cycles", "3 cycles", "8 cycles"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT is the hit time plus the expected extra cost of a miss: AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. The miss penalty is counted from the moment the miss is detected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss contribution = 0.05 &times; 60 = 3 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 2 + 3 = <b>5 cycles</b>.</li><li>Sanity check: a 5% miss rate can at most add 0.05 &times; 60 = 3 cycles, so 5 is the only option above the 2-cycle hit time by the right amount.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying the hit time by the miss rate, or adding the whole 60 cycles. Only the miss fraction pays the penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. Every performance question in this unit starts from that line.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 2 GHz processor has a base CPI of 1.2, makes 1.2 memory accesses per instruction, has a cache miss rate of 2% and a miss penalty of 100 cycles. How long does a program of 10<sup>9</sup> instructions take?", "options": ["0.9 s", "2.4 s", "1.8 s", "3.6 s"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Memory stalls enter the CPI equation as accesses per instruction &times; miss rate &times; miss penalty, and execution time is total cycles divided by clock rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stall cycles per instruction = 1.2 &times; 0.02 &times; 100 = 2.4.</li><li>CPI including stalls = 1.2 + 2.4 = 3.6.</li><li>Total cycles = 3.6 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> = 3.6 &times; 10<sup>9</sup>.</li><li>Time = 3.6 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> / (2 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Hz) = <b>1.8 s</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.8 s</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using one memory access per instruction gives 1.2 + 0.02 &times; 100 = 3.2 and 1.6 s, and forgetting the base CPI gives 1.2 s. Both slip through if the formula is applied from memory instead of from the definition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Execution time = (base CPI + stalls per instruction) &times; instruction count / clock rate. Keep the accesses-per-instruction factor.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 1 cycle and a miss penalty of 100 cycles. What is the largest miss rate for which the average memory access time does not exceed 3 cycles?", "options": ["3%", "2%", "1.5%", "5%"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty solved for the miss rate rather than evaluated with it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Set 1 + m &times; 100 &le; 3.</li><li>m &times; 100 &le; 2, so m &le; 0.02.</li><li>The largest admissible miss rate is therefore <b>2%</b>.</li><li>Check: 1 + 0.02 &times; 100 = 3 cycles exactly, and 3% would give 4 cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2%</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the budget as 3% because the target is 3 cycles. The 3 cycles include the 1-cycle hit time, so only 2 cycles of penalty are available.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Budget questions: subtract the hit time first, then divide by the penalty.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "An L1 cache has a local miss rate of 6%. Every L1 miss is sent to an L2 whose local miss rate is 20%. What percentage of all L1 accesses reach main memory?", "options": ["1.2%", "6%", "20%", "26%"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The global miss rate of a level is the fraction of all references that miss at that level, and in a two-level hierarchy it is the product of the local miss rates along the path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An L1 access reaches L2 only on the 6% of accesses that miss in L1.</li><li>Of those, 20% miss in L2 and go to memory: 0.06 &times; 0.20 = 0.012.</li><li>So <b>1.2%</b> of all L1 accesses reach main memory.</li><li>Equivalently, the global L2 miss rate is 1.2% while the local L2 miss rate stays 20%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.2%</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the two rates (26%) or quoting 20%. Local rates multiply; only global rates add up along a path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Local miss rate is per level, global miss rate is per original reference. Convert before comparing levels.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "An L1 cache has a hit time of 1 cycle and a local miss rate of 4%. Its L2 has a hit time of 8 cycles and a local miss rate of 30%; a main-memory access costs 150 cycles. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["3.30 cycles", "2.12 cycles", "3.12 cycles", "7.12 cycles"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a hierarchy the levels nest: the cost of an L1 miss is the L2 hit time plus the L2 miss rate times the cost below it. Multiplying the outer rate by that whole inner cost gives the AMAT.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost of one L1 miss = 8 + 0.30 &times; 150 = 8 + 45 = 53 cycles.</li><li>L1 miss contribution = 0.04 &times; 53 = 2.12 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 1 + 2.12 = <b>3.12 cycles</b>.</li><li>Check the alternative route: 0.04 &times; 8 = 0.32 and 0.04 &times; 0.30 &times; 150 = 1.8, and 1 + 0.32 + 1.8 = 3.12.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.12 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 150 as the whole L1 miss penalty gives 1 + 0.04 &times; 153 = 7.12 and ignores the L2 hit entirely. Dropping the L1 hit time gives 2.12.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Nest the levels from the bottom up: inner cost first, then multiply by the outer miss rate, then add the outer hit time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A fully associative cache with 4 frames starts empty and uses LRU replacement. The reference string is 2, 4, 6, 2, 4, 8, 2, 4, 6, 8. How many hits occur?", "options": ["4", "5", "3", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under LRU the victim is the least recently used frame, so the frame contents must be kept in order of last use. A fully associative cache has no index, so any frame may hold any block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>2, 4, 6 all miss and fill the four frames partially: {2, 4, 6}.</li><li>2 hits, then 4 hits; the frames become {6, 2, 4} in LRU order.</li><li>8 misses and replaces 6, the least recently used: {2, 4, 8}.</li><li>2 hits, 4 hits, 6 hits and 8 hits follow, since nothing is evicted once the four blocks 2, 4, 6, 8 all fit.</li><li>Hits = positions 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10 = <b>6</b> hits against 4 misses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6 hits</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the cache as having three frames gives 4 hits; the fourth frame is what lets 2, 4, 6 and 8 coexist after the last miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the frame contents after every reference. LRU answers are only as good as the bookkeeping.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache with 3 frames uses FIFO replacement. For the reference string 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5, 8, 9, 7, 9, how many misses occur?", "options": ["12", "14", "10", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FIFO evicts the block that has been resident longest, regardless of how often or how recently it was used. A hit does not change the queue order, which is what separates FIFO from LRU.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>3, 1, 4 miss and fill the cache; order of arrival is 3, 1, 4.</li><li>1 hits (no reordering under FIFO). 5 misses and evicts 3: queue 1, 4, 5.</li><li>9 misses, evicting 1: queue 4, 5, 9. Then 2 misses (evict 4), 6 misses (evict 5), 5 misses (evict 9), 3 misses (evict 2): queue 6, 5, 3.</li><li>5 hits. 8 misses and evicts 6: queue 5, 3, 8. Then 9 misses (evict 5), 7 misses (evict 3), and 9 hits.</li><li>Misses = 12, hits = 3, which accounts for all 15 references.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12 misses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reordering on a hit, which turns the trace into LRU and gives a smaller count; or evicting the least recently used block instead of the oldest arrival.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> FIFO keeps an arrival queue and ignores hits. If you find yourself moving a block on a hit, you are tracing LRU.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "For the reference string 2, 3, 2, 1, 5, 2, 4, 5, 3, 2, 5, 2 with 3 frames, LRU and optimal (MIN) replacement give different miss counts. Which pair is correct, written as (LRU, optimal)?", "options": ["(6, 7)", "(7, 6)", "(7, 7)", "(6, 6)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LRU looks backwards at the last use; optimal looks forwards at the next use. Because optimal has strictly more information, its miss count can never exceed LRU's on the same string and frame count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>LRU trace: 2 M, 3 M, 2 H, 1 M, 5 M, 2 H, 4 M, 5 H, 3 M, 2 M, 5 H, 2 H.</li><li>That gives <b>7</b> misses and 5 hits for LRU.</li><li>Optimal trace: on the miss for 4 the victim is 1 (never used again), and on the miss for 3 the victim is 4: misses fall to <b>6</b>.</li><li>So the pair is (7, 6), and optimal is better by exactly one replacement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (7, 6)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the order to (6, 7) by assuming LRU is the better policy, or answering (6, 6) by tracing optimal with LRU's victim choice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Optimal is a bound, not an algorithm. Any online policy's miss count is at least the optimal one for the same string.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through cache with no write-allocate serves a loop of 400 stores, each to a different word that is not in the cache. How many main-memory write operations does the loop cause?", "options": ["0", "100", "400", "800"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-through sends every store to memory immediately. No-write-allocate means a store that misses does not fetch the block, so a miss costs a write but no read.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each of the 400 stores misses, because none of the words is resident.</li><li>No-write-allocate suppresses the fetch, so no read is issued.</li><li>Write-through issues one memory write per store, so there are <b>400</b> writes.</li><li>Total memory transactions = 400 writes + 0 reads.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 400 memory writes</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 0 by confusing no-write-allocate with no-write-through, or 800 by charging a fill read for each miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two independent choices: allocate or not (does a store miss fetch?) and through or back (does a store reach memory now?). Here it is no-allocate plus through.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through cache with write-allocate serves 2000 stores of 4 bytes, all to 8 distinct 64-byte blocks that are not resident when the loop begins. Counting reads and writes together, how many main-memory accesses does the loop cause?", "options": ["2000", "8", "16", "2008"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-allocate turns the first store to a block into a read-modify-write: the block is fetched, and then every store in it writes through. The fetch happens once per block, not once per store.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Eight blocks are fetched, so there are 8 memory reads.</li><li>All 2000 stores write through, so there are 2000 memory writes.</li><li>Total accesses = 8 + 2000 = <b>2008</b>.</li><li>For contrast, write-back with write-allocate would issue 8 reads and 8 write-backs, that is 16 accesses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2008 memory accesses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2000 forgets the eight fills, and 16 is the write-back answer to the same workload. The policy, not the workload, decides which number applies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count fills once per block and writes once per store for write-through, or once per block for write-back.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through cache has no write buffer, so every store waits 20 cycles for main memory. Stores are 12% of all instructions and every other instruction takes exactly 1 cycle. What is the effective CPI?", "options": ["1.12", "3.4", "2.4", "4.0"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Without a write buffer the processor stalls for the full memory write on every store, so the store cost enters the CPI as (fraction of stores) &times; (write time).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stall cycles per instruction = 0.12 &times; 20 = 2.4.</li><li>Base CPI for the remaining instructions = 1.0.</li><li>Effective CPI = 1.0 + 2.4 = <b>3.4</b>.</li><li>With a four-entry write buffer the processor would continue until the buffer filled, so the stall would fall to roughly one store in twenty rather than every store.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1.12 by adding 12% to the CPI as a fraction rather than as cycles, or 2.4 by dropping the base CPI. A store stalls the whole pipeline for 20 cycles here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Stalls per instruction = frequency &times; cost per occurrence. That one line covers caches, stores, branches and interrupts.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache address mapping are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["For a fixed capacity and block size, doubling the associativity halves the number of sets and adds one bit to the tag.", "The offset field width depends only on the block size.", "A fully associative cache has no index field, so every line's tag is compared in parallel.", "Two addresses that differ only in their tag bits always map to different sets."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The address split is determined by three independent quantities: the block size fixes the offset, the number of sets fixes the index, and whatever remains is the tag. Associativity and capacity together fix the number of sets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways), so doubling ways halves the sets, removing one index bit and adding it to the tag.</li><li>(B) True. The offset addresses a byte inside a block, so it is log<sub>2</sub>(block size) whatever the associativity or the capacity.</li><li>(C) True. With one set there is nothing to index; the tag must be compared against every line, which is why a fully associative lookup costs one comparator per line.</li><li>(D) False. The index bits decide the set. Two addresses that differ only in the tag bits agree on the index and therefore land in the <i>same</i> set, where they compete for the ways.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) is the trap: tag bits identify a block within a set, not a set, so differing tags mean the same set, which is exactly how conflicts arise.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Index selects the set, tag identifies the block inside it, offset selects the byte. Map every claim about mapping onto those three roles.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache performance are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["The global miss rate of a two-level hierarchy is the product of the two local miss rates.", "Memory stall cycles per instruction equal memory accesses per instruction times miss rate times miss penalty.", "Increasing the cache capacity always lowers the average memory access time.", "If the hit time rises by one cycle while the miss rate halves with the miss penalty unchanged at 20 cycles, the AMAT must fall."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT is a sum of two terms, the hit time and the expected miss cost, and improvements to one term can be cancelled by damage to the other. That is the whole content of the capacity and associativity trade-offs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Only the fraction that misses at L1 reaches L2, so the fraction reaching memory is the product of the local rates.</li><li>(B) True. That product is the definition of stall cycles per instruction, and it is the standard bridge from miss rate to CPI.</li><li>(C) False. A larger cache usually has a longer hit time because of the wider tag check and the longer access path, and that raises the first term for every access, hit or miss.</li><li>(D) False. Original AMAT = 1 + 20m and new AMAT = 2 + 10m. The new value is smaller only when m &gt; 0.1; for a 5% miss rate it is larger (2.5 against 2.0).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) feels true because the miss rate halved, but a one-cycle hit-time penalty is paid by every access while the miss saving is paid only by the misses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Compare AMAT terms, not miss rates. A change that touches the hit time affects 100% of accesses.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache replacement are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["LRU is a stack algorithm and therefore never exhibits Belady's anomaly.", "Optimal replacement needs knowledge of future references, so it cannot be implemented online.", "FIFO is a stack algorithm.", "For a fixed reference string and frame count, LRU can never have fewer misses than optimal replacement."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A stack algorithm has the property that the set of blocks held with n frames is always a subset of the set held with n+1 frames, which forbids Belady's anomaly. LRU has that property because its ordering does not depend on the frame count; FIFO's does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. LRU's victim choice depends only on the past reference order, so adding a frame can only add blocks to the resident set.</li><li>(B) True. Optimal picks the block whose next use is furthest away, which requires the future of the reference stream; it is a bound used for comparison.</li><li>(C) False. FIFO's resident set depends on the frame count, so a larger cache can hold a worse set; the classic 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 string misses 9 times with 3 frames and 10 times with 4.</li><li>(D) True. Optimal minimises misses over all policies, so no other policy can beat it on the same string and frame count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (C) is the standard error: FIFO looks like a stack algorithm because it is simple, but the anomaly on the classic string proves it is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Only stack algorithms are immune to Belady's anomaly. LRU and optimal are stack algorithms; FIFO and random are not.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about write policies are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A write-back cache may hold data that is newer than the corresponding main-memory contents.", "A write-through cache needs no dirty bit, because main memory is always up to date.", "With no write-allocate, a store that misses does not fetch the block into the cache.", "A write buffer removes every store stall, however long the loop of stores runs."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The dirty bit exists only to answer one question at replacement time: is the cached copy the newest one? Write-through never leaves a stale memory word, so the question never arises.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. A store to a resident line sets the dirty bit and memory keeps the old value until the line is evicted.</li><li>(B) True. Every store has already reached memory, so eviction needs no write-back and no dirty state.</li><li>(C) True. No-write-allocate writes around the cache, which is the usual partner of write-through because the block would not be reused soon.</li><li>(D) False. A buffer absorbs bursts, but if stores arrive faster than memory drains it, it fills and the processor stalls again; its benefit depends on the drain rate.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) is the classic overstatement. A write buffer smooths bursts; it cannot exceed the memory bandwidth over a long run.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask what each mechanism is for: dirty bit for eviction, allocate for reuse, buffer for burst absorption. None of them removes a bandwidth limit.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about write-back caches are TRUE? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Replacing a dirty block costs a write-back in addition to the fill that caused the replacement.", "In a write-back cache a read miss that evicts a dirty block needs two memory transactions.", "Write-back reduces memory traffic when a block is rewritten many times before it is evicted.", "For a read-only workload, write-through generates more main-memory write traffic than write-back."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A write-back cache defers memory writes until eviction, so its traffic is counted in dirty blocks rather than in stores. The price is paid on the replacement path, where a dirty victim must be written before the new block is filled.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The victim's data must be saved before its frame is reused, so the replacement carries an extra write.</li><li>(B) True. The dirty victim is written out and the requested block is read in, which is why write-back caches often need a write buffer on the eviction path.</li><li>(C) True. Many stores to one block produce a single write-back, whereas write-through would produce one memory write per store.</li><li>(D) False. A read-only workload never dirties a line, so write-through issues no writes either; both policies generate zero write traffic and the same read traffic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) sounds like a general law but fails on the degenerate case: with no writes, there is nothing for either policy to write.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write-back trades write traffic for a more expensive miss path. Say which quantity is being compared before claiming one policy is better.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 512 KB 8-way set-associative cache uses 64-byte blocks. How many sets does the cache contain?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1024", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways). Associativity divides the line count into sets rather than adding capacity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Capacity in bytes = 512 &times; 1024 = 524288.</li><li>Block size &times; ways = 64 &times; 8 = 512 bytes per set.</li><li>Sets = 524288 / 512 = <b>1024</b>.</li><li>Check the index width: log<sub>2</sub>(1024) = 10 bits, and the offset is log<sub>2</sub>(64) = 6 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1024 sets</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing only by the block size gives 8192, the number of lines, and that would be the set count of a direct-mapped cache of the same size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sets = capacity / (block size &times; ways). Lines = sets &times; ways.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 4 cycles, a miss rate of 3% and a miss penalty of 120 cycles. What is the average memory access time in cycles?(Enter a number; decimals are allowed.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7.6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty, with the penalty measured from miss detection to the arrival of the block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss contribution = 0.03 &times; 120 = 3.6 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 4 + 3.6 = <b>7.6 cycles</b>.</li><li>Sanity check: 7.6 lies between the 4-cycle hit time and the 124-cycle all-miss time, as it must.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.6 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding the 3.6 to 4 or dropping the hit time gives 3.6, which is below the hit time itself and therefore impossible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The answer must lie between the hit time and the hit time plus the penalty. Use that interval as a free check.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A cache with 3 frames uses LRU replacement and starts empty. For the reference string 4, 1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, how many misses occur?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every first reference to a block misses, and every reference to a block that has been evicted misses again. Tracking the last-use order is what makes LRU decidable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>4, 1, 2 miss and fill the cache; order of last use is 4, 1, 2.</li><li>4 hits and becomes most recent: order 1, 2, 4.</li><li>5 misses, evicting 1: order 2, 4, 5. Then 3 misses, evicting 2: order 4, 5, 3.</li><li>4 hits: order 5, 3, 4. Then 1 misses, evicting 5: order 3, 4, 1.</li><li>2 misses, evicting 3: order 4, 1, 2. Finally 3 misses, evicting 4.</li><li>Misses = 8 (three cold misses plus five replacement misses) and hits = 3, accounting for all 11 references.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 misses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 9 by evicting the most recently used block at one step, or 7 by forgetting that the final 3 was evicted earlier.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three cold misses are unavoidable with three distinct blocks; every further miss is a replacement you must justify.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A write-through L1 forwards every store to an L2 that is write-back with 64-byte blocks. A program performs 5000 four-byte stores into a 4 KB region; all 64 blocks of the region stay resident in L2 for the whole loop and are evicted only after it ends. How many 64-byte blocks does L2 write to main memory in total?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "64", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-back traffic is counted in dirty blocks that leave the cache, not in stores that arrive. An L2 that stays resident accumulates dirty bits and writes a block only when that block is evicted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The region is 4 KB, so it spans 4096 / 64 = 64 blocks.</li><li>All 64 blocks stay resident for the whole loop, so nothing is written back during it: the write-back count inside the loop is 0.</li><li>Every block receives stores, so every block is dirty when the loop ends.</li><li>The final evictions write all <b>64</b> blocks to memory, that is 64 &times; 64 = 4096 bytes.</li><li>For contrast, a write-through L2 would have issued 5000 &times; 4 = 20000 bytes of writes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 64 blocks</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5000 by counting stores instead of blocks, or 16 by assuming a 16-byte write-back granularity. A dirty block is written back whole, once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a write-back cache, count distinct dirty blocks. For a write-through cache, count stores. The two are different denominators.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 3-frame cache starts empty. For the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 6, what is the minimum number of misses that any replacement policy can achieve?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The minimum is the optimal (MIN) count: on every miss, evict the resident block whose next use is furthest in the future, or one that is never used again. No policy can do better, because a miss is unavoidable whenever the requested block is absent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1, 2, 3 are cold misses: 3 misses, frames {1, 2, 3}.</li><li>4 misses. Next uses are 1 at position 6, 2 at position 5 and 3 at position 12, so 3 is furthest away and is evicted: 4 misses, frames {1, 2, 4}.</li><li>2 and 1 hit. 5 misses and evicts 4, which is never used again: 5 misses, frames {1, 2, 5}.</li><li>6 misses and evicts 5, also never used again: 6 misses, frames {1, 2, 6}.</li><li>2, 1 and 2 hit. 3 misses and evicts 1, whose next use is at position 17 against 13 for 6 and 16 for 2: 7 misses, frames {2, 6, 3}.</li><li>7 misses and evicts 2, whose next use is at position 16 against 14 for 6 and 15 for 3: 8 misses, frames {6, 3, 7}.</li><li>6 and 3 hit. 2 misses and evicts 7, never used again: 9 misses, frames {6, 3, 2}.</li><li>1 misses and evicts 6, whose next use is at position 20 against 18 for 2 and 19 for 3: 10 misses, frames {3, 2, 1}.</li><li>2 and 3 hit. The final 6 misses, and any frame may go: 11 misses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 misses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting the LRU count of 15, which is a property of LRU rather than a lower bound, or forgetting that the last reference to 6 is a miss because 6 was evicted at position 14.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Optimal is a bound. Any implementable policy, including LRU and FIFO, can only do worse on the same string.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following is TRUE of SRAM but NOT of DRAM?", "options": ["Each bit is stored as charge on a capacitor.", "It holds its contents without periodic refresh as long as power is applied.", "It offers higher storage density per unit area.", "It is the technology normally used for main memory."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An SRAM cell is a bistable latch of about six transistors, so the stored bit is held by active feedback and stays valid while power is on. A DRAM cell is one transistor plus one capacitor, and the charge leaks away.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An SRAM cell keeps its value through the feedback loop of its cross-coupled inverters, so no refresh is needed while power is applied.</li><li>A DRAM cell stores the bit as charge, which leaks, so every row must be re-read and re-written within the retention interval.</li><li>The capacitor cell is far smaller than the six-transistor cell, so DRAM is denser and cheaper per bit, and it is what main memory is built from.</li><li>SRAM is faster and needs no refresh, which is why it is used for cache.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: It needs no periodic refresh while powered — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A describes DRAM, not SRAM. Option C is the DRAM advantage: the capacitor cell is denser, which is exactly why main memory is DRAM.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A DRAM chip is organised as 1M &times; 8 bits, where 1M = 2<sup>20</sup>. How many address lines does the chip need?", "options": ["8", "20", "23", "28"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The address lines must be able to name every storage location. The second factor of the organisation is the data width, which affects the data pins, not the address pins.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The chip holds 2<sup>20</sup> locations, since 1M = 2<sup>20</sup>.</li><li>Naming 2<sup>20</sup> locations requires 20 address lines.</li><li>The &times; 8 refers to 8 data bits read or written at a time, so it sets the number of data pins.</li><li>Hence the chip needs 20 address lines and 8 data lines.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20 address lines — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the two numbers to get 28, or answering 8 because the word 'bits' appears. Address width comes from the location count only.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A 64 MB main memory is built from 4M &times; 4-bit DRAM chips, where 1M = 2<sup>20</sup>. How many chips are required?", "options": ["8", "16", "32", "64"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of chips is the total number of bits needed divided by the number of bits one chip supplies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One chip holds 2<sup>22</sup> &times; 4 bits = 2<sup>24</sup> bits = 16 Mbit = 2 MB.</li><li>The memory to be built is 64 MB.</li><li>Chips = 64 MB / 2 MB = 32.</li><li>Cross-check by the other route: 64 MB = 2<sup>29</sup> bits, and 2<sup>29</sup> / 2<sup>24</sup> = 2<sup>5</sup> = 32.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 32 chips — option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing 64 MB by 4M to get 16 and forgetting that each chip supplies only 4 bits per location, so chips must also be grouped to make a byte.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A DRAM array has 4096 rows and is refreshed by refreshing one row per refresh cycle. Every cell must be refreshed within 64 ms. How long may a single refresh cycle last?", "options": ["1.95 &micro;s", "15.625 &micro;s", "64 &micro;s", "256 &micro;s"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Refreshing one row per cycle means the whole array is covered in (rows &times; cycle time). Setting that equal to the retention interval fixes the cycle time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All 4096 rows must be visited within 64 ms.</li><li>Time per row = 64 ms / 4096.</li><li>64 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> / 4096 = 15.625 &times; 10<sup>&minus;6</sup> s.</li><li>So one refresh cycle may last 15.625 &micro;s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15.625 &micro;s — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 64 &micro;s by treating the retention interval as the per-row interval. The interval must be divided among all the rows.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A system uses 32-bit logical addresses and 4 KB pages. How many bits form the page offset, and how many entries does a single-level page table need?", "options": ["12 bits and 2<sup>20</sup> entries", "12 bits and 2<sup>12</sup> entries", "20 bits and 2<sup>12</sup> entries", "10 bits and 2<sup>22</sup> entries"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The offset selects a byte inside a page, so its width is log<sub>2</sub>(page size). Whatever remains of the logical address is the page number, and a single-level table has one entry per page.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Page size 4 KB = 2<sup>12</sup> bytes, so the offset is 12 bits.</li><li>The page number takes the remaining 32 &minus; 12 = 20 bits.</li><li>A page number of 20 bits names 2<sup>20</sup> = 1,048,576 pages.</li><li>A single-level page table therefore needs 2<sup>20</sup> entries, one per page.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12 offset bits and 2<sup>20</sup> table entries — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the two widths, or assuming the table has one entry per offset value. The offset addresses bytes inside a page and never indexes the page table.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB has 64 entries and is 4-way set associative. How many sets does it have, and how many bits of the page number select the set?", "options": ["16 sets and 4 bits", "16 sets and 6 bits", "64 sets and 6 bits", "4 sets and 2 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a set-associative structure the entries are partitioned into sets of equal size: sets = entries / ways. The set index width is the logarithm of the set count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Sets = 64 entries / 4 ways = 16 sets.</li><li>16 = 2<sup>4</sup>, so 4 bits of the page number select the set.</li><li>The remaining high-order bits of the page number form the tag compared against the 4 ways.</li><li>Cross-check: 16 sets &times; 4 ways &times; 1 entry = 64 entries.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 sets and a 4-bit set index — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing by the wrong factor, or quoting 6 bits because 64 = 2<sup>6</sup>. The 6 bits index entries, not sets; associativity removes them.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "What does a single TLB entry hold?", "options": ["The full contents of one page frame.", "A page-number to frame-number mapping together with protection and valid bits.", "The complete page table of the running process.", "The physical address of the page-table base register."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The TLB is a small, fast, fully or set-associative cache of page-table entries. Each entry caches one translation, not the data and not the table.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A translation maps a virtual page number to a physical frame number.</li><li>The entry stores that page number as its tag, the frame number as its data, and control bits: valid, dirty, reference and protection.</li><li>The page data itself lives in the frame in main memory, not in the TLB.</li><li>The page-table base register is a separate CPU register, and the page table is far larger than a TLB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A page-number to frame-number mapping with protection and valid bits — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the TLB with a data cache. The TLB caches translations; a data cache caches the contents of frames.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB is accessed in 10 ns and main memory in 80 ns. The TLB hit ratio is 90%. On a miss the page-table entry is read from memory and then the data word is fetched. The effective access time is", "options": ["90 ns", "98 ns", "106 ns", "170 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective access time is the probability-weighted sum of the hit path and the miss path, and both paths include the TLB lookup.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = TLB access + one memory access = 10 + 80 = 90 ns.</li><li>Miss path = TLB access + page-table read + data read = 10 + 80 + 80 = 170 ns.</li><li>EAT = 0.90 &times; 90 + 0.10 &times; 170.</li><li>0.90 &times; 90 = 81 and 0.10 &times; 170 = 17, so EAT = 98 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 98 ns — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A is the hit path alone, quoted as if every access hit. Option D is the miss path alone. The TLB time must appear in both terms.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A system uses two-level paging. The TLB is accessed in 2 ns and main memory in 100 ns, and the TLB hit ratio is 95%. On a TLB miss both page-table levels are read from memory and then the data word is fetched. The effective access time is", "options": ["102 ns", "112 ns", "117 ns", "302 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each level of paging adds one memory access to the miss path only. The hit path stays at TLB access plus one memory access.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = 2 + 100 = 102 ns.</li><li>Miss path = 2 + 100 + 100 + 100 = 302 ns, that is the TLB, level 1, level 2 and the data word.</li><li>EAT = 0.95 &times; 102 + 0.05 &times; 302.</li><li>0.95 &times; 102 = 96.9 and 0.05 &times; 302 = 15.1, so EAT = 112 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 112 ns — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C comes from charging three memory accesses on the hit path as well, and option D is the miss path used as if it were the average.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "In a segmented memory system a logical address is the pair (s, d). What does d denote?", "options": ["The offset within segment s.", "The segment number.", "The frame number in physical memory.", "The page index inside the segment."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Segmentation divides the logical address space into variable-length named segments. The first component names the segment; the second gives the displacement inside it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>s is an index into the segment table, which yields the base address and the limit of that segment.</li><li>d is the displacement from the base of the segment.</li><li>The hardware checks d &lt; limit; if the check fails a trap is raised.</li><li>The physical address is base(s) + d.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: The offset within segment s — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading d as a page index assumes paging, where the second component really is a page number. In segmentation the second component is a byte displacement inside a variable-length segment.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A process has the segment table below. Which logical address causes a trap?<br/>Segment 0: base 219, limit 600 &middot; Segment 1: base 2300, limit 14 &middot; Segment 2: base 90, limit 100", "options": ["(0, 430)", "(1, 13)", "(2, 500)", "(2, 99)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The hardware compares the offset with the segment limit before forming the physical address. The offset must satisfy 0 &le; d &lt; limit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(0, 430): 430 &lt; 600, valid, and maps to 219 + 430 = 649.</li><li>(1, 13): 13 &lt; 14, valid, and maps to 2300 + 13 = 2313.</li><li>(2, 500): 500 &gt; 100, so the offset is outside the segment and the access traps.</li><li>(2, 99): 99 &lt; 100, valid, and maps to 90 + 99 = 189.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (2, 500) traps — option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the base instead of the limit for the check, or forgetting that the bound is strict: an offset exactly equal to the limit is already outside a segment of that length.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A single-level page table maps a 32-bit logical address space with 4 KB pages, and each page-table entry is 4 bytes. How large is the page table?", "options": ["4 KB", "1 MB", "4 MB", "2<sup>32</sup> bytes"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Page-table size is the number of entries times the size of one entry, and the number of entries is the number of virtual pages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Pages = 2<sup>32</sup> / 2<sup>12</sup> = 2<sup>20</sup>.</li><li>Entries = 2<sup>20</sup>, one per page.</li><li>Size = 2<sup>20</sup> &times; 4 bytes = 2<sup>22</sup> bytes.</li><li>2<sup>22</sup> bytes = 4 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 MB — option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option D forgets that the table has one entry per page, not one per byte of address space. Option A confuses the page size with the table size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "For the same system (32-bit logical addresses, 4 KB pages, 4-byte entries), two-level paging is used so that each page table fits in exactly one page. How many bits of the page number form the outer (first-level) index?", "options": ["8", "10", "12", "20"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A page table that fits in one page holds (page size / entry size) entries, and each level therefore consumes exactly log<sub>2</sub> of that many bits of the page number.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One page holds 4096 / 4 = 1024 entries.</li><li>1024 = 2<sup>10</sup>, so each level consumes 10 bits.</li><li>The page number is 32 &minus; 12 = 20 bits.</li><li>Two levels of 10 bits each account for exactly the 20 bits, so the outer index is 10 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 bits — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Splitting the 20 bits unevenly, or taking 12 bits because that is the offset width. The offset is not part of the page number and is never indexed by a table.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "With a TLB of access time t, a main-memory access time m and a TLB hit ratio h, and with a single-level page table held in memory, the effective access time is", "options": ["h &times; t + (1 &minus; h) &times; m", "h &times; (t + m) + (1 &minus; h) &times; (t + 2m)", "t + (1 &minus; h) &times; m", "m / h"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every access pays the TLB lookup; only a miss pays the extra page-table read. The two paths are averaged by their probabilities.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A hit costs t + m: the TLB lookup and then the data access.</li><li>A miss costs t + 2m: the TLB lookup, the page-table read and then the data access.</li><li>Weighting by h and 1 &minus; h gives EAT = h(t + m) + (1 &minus; h)(t + 2m).</li><li>Check the endpoints: h = 1 gives t + m and h = 0 gives t + 2m.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: EAT = h(t + m) + (1 &minus; h)(t + 2m) — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A drops the TLB time from both paths, which is the classic slip. The TLB is consulted on every access, hit or miss.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following is a disadvantage of segmentation compared with paging?", "options": ["It hides the logical structure of the program from the programmer.", "Because segments have variable length, main memory suffers from external fragmentation.", "It makes sharing of code between processes impossible.", "It provides no protection mechanism."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Segments are allocated as contiguous variable-length blocks, so the free space between them is broken into pieces that may be individually too small.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A segment must occupy one contiguous run of physical memory.</li><li>As segments are created and destroyed, the free memory is fragmented into variable-size holes.</li><li>A new segment may fail to fit even though the total free space is large enough: external fragmentation.</li><li>Paging avoids this because every frame is the same size.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: External fragmentation caused by variable-length segments — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The other three options describe the opposite of what segmentation does. Segmentation exposes logical structure, supports sharing through segment-table entries and carries protection bits.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about SRAM and DRAM are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An SRAM cell is a bistable latch of about six transistors and needs no refresh.", "A DRAM cell stores a bit as charge on a capacitor and must be refreshed periodically.", "DRAM is faster than SRAM but less dense.", "SRAM is used for cache memory while DRAM is used for main memory."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two technologies trade density against speed and refresh cost, and that trade is what fixes their places in the memory hierarchy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The cross-coupled inverter pair holds the bit actively, so no refresh is required.</li><li>(B) True. The capacitor leaks, so each row is read and rewritten within the retention interval.</li><li>(C) False. It is the other way round: SRAM is faster and less dense, DRAM is denser and slower.</li><li>(D) True. Cache needs the speed of SRAM, main memory needs the density and cost of DRAM.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C reverses the trade-off. Density and speed pull in opposite directions here, and DRAM wins only on density.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about TLBs are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A TLB hit avoids one or more page-table memory accesses.", "A TLB is usually fully associative or set associative because its hit time matters.", "A TLB must be flushed, or its entries tagged with an address-space identifier, on a context switch.", "Doubling the number of TLB entries halves the number of page faults."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The TLB caches translations, so it shortens the address-translation path. It never changes which pages are resident.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. A hit supplies the frame number directly, so the page-table walk is skipped.</li><li>(B) True. A fully or set-associative search in parallel keeps the hit time low despite the tag comparison against several entries.</li><li>(C) True. Entries are per-process mappings, so a switch either invalidates them or requires an address-space identifier in the tag.</li><li>(D) False. A page fault means the page is not in memory; a larger TLB only reduces translation misses, not page faults.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option D confuses a TLB miss with a page fault. A TLB miss usually costs a page-table walk; a page fault costs a disk or flash transfer.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about effective access time (EAT) in a paged system are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["With a single-level page table in memory, EAT = h(t + m) + (1 &minus; h)(t + 2m), where t is the TLB time, m the memory time and h the hit ratio.", "EAT is the probability-weighted average of the hit path and the miss path.", "Because the page-fault service time is bounded, it can be added as a fixed constant.", "With a two-level page table the miss path reads two page-table entries and then the data word."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> EAT is an expectation over the paths an access can take, so each path must be charged in full and weighted by its probability.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. That expression is the standard one-level formula.</li><li>(B) True. That is the definition of an expectation over the two outcomes.</li><li>(C) False. A page-fault service time is dominated by a device access and varies with the device and the queue; it is modelled as a large, not a small fixed, penalty and it is not a constant of the machine.</li><li>(D) True. Each level costs one memory read, and the data word costs one more.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C treats a fault as a fixed overhead like a cache miss. Fault handling involves a trap to the operating system and a device transfer, and its cost is neither small nor constant.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about segmented memory are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A logical address is a (segment number, offset) pair, and the offset is checked against the segment limit.", "Two processes can share a segment by pointing their segment-table entries at the same base with matching protection.", "Because segments have variable length, main memory suffers from external fragmentation.", "The segment number is translated to a frame number through a page table."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Segmentation is a logical, variable-length division of the address space; the segment table supplies a base and a limit per segment, and that is all the hardware needs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The limit check is what makes segmentation a protection mechanism as well as an addressing scheme.</li><li>(B) True. Sharing needs only that both segment tables name the same base with compatible permissions.</li><li>(C) True. Variable-length contiguous allocation leaves holes that may be too small individually.</li><li>(D) False. A segment table maps a segment number to a base and a limit. Frame numbers belong to paging.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option D mixes the two schemes. Segments are located by a base and bounded by a limit; only pages are located by a frame number.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A paged system has a TLB access time of 10 ns, a memory access time of 100 ns and a single-level page table in memory. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["With a 100% TLB hit ratio the effective access time is 110 ns.", "With a 0% TLB hit ratio the effective access time is 210 ns.", "Raising the hit ratio from 90% to 95% reduces the effective access time by 5 ns.", "Doubling the memory access time doubles the effective access time at every hit ratio."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> EAT = h(t + m) + (1 &minus; h)(t + 2m) = t + m + (1 &minus; h)m. In this form the TLB time and one memory access are paid unconditionally, and only the page-table read is proportional to the miss rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>h = 1: EAT = 10 + 100 = 110 ns. Statement A is true.</li><li>h = 0: EAT = 10 + 2 &times; 100 = 210 ns. Statement B is true.</li><li>h = 0.90: EAT = 10 + 100 + 0.10 &times; 100 = 120 ns. h = 0.95: EAT = 10 + 100 + 0.05 &times; 100 = 115 ns. The drop is 5 ns, so statement C is true.</li><li>Doubling m changes the h = 1 case from 110 to 210 ns, not to 220 ns, because the TLB time does not double. Statement D is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement D looks like a scaling law but fails because one component of EAT, the TLB access, is independent of memory time. Only the terms containing m scale.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A DRAM chip has 10 row-address pins and 10 column-address pins, and the row and column addresses are multiplexed onto the same pins. How many addressable locations does the chip have?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1048576", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Address multiplexing halves the pin count without reducing the address space: the row address is latched first, then the column address, and together they name one cell.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The row address contributes 10 bits and the column address contributes 10 bits.</li><li>Together they form a 20-bit address.</li><li>2<sup>20</sup> = 1,048,576.</li><li>So the chip has 1,048,576 addressable locations, that is 1M.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1048576</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1024, the number of locations on one address, or 20, the number of address bits. The two 10-bit halves combine into a 20-bit address.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB has 128 entries and is 8-way set associative. How many sets does it have?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a set-associative array the entries are divided equally among the sets, so the set count is the entry count divided by the associativity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Entries = 128 and ways = 8.</li><li>Sets = 128 / 8 = 16.</li><li>Cross-check: 16 sets &times; 8 ways = 128 entries.</li><li>The set index therefore takes log<sub>2</sub> 16 = 4 bits of the page number.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 128, the entry count, or 8, the associativity. The set count is the quotient of the two.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A machine has 2 GB of physical memory and a page size of 4 KB. How many frames does physical memory contain?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "524288", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A frame is one page-sized slot in physical memory, so the frame count is physical memory size divided by page size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>2 GB = 2 &times; 2<sup>30</sup> = 2<sup>31</sup> bytes.</li><li>4 KB = 2<sup>12</sup> bytes.</li><li>Frames = 2<sup>31</sup> / 2<sup>12</sup> = 2<sup>19</sup>.</li><li>2<sup>19</sup> = 524,288.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 524288</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2<sup>31</sup>, the number of bytes, or 2<sup>21</sup> by using 1 KB = 1000. Both GB and KB here are powers of two.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB is accessed in 20 ns and main memory in 100 ns, and the page table is single-level and held in memory. The measured effective access time is 150 ns. What is the TLB hit ratio, in percent?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "70", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> EAT = h(t + m) + (1 &minus; h)(t + 2m). Given EAT, solve the linear equation for h.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = 20 + 100 = 120 ns. Miss path = 20 + 100 + 100 = 220 ns.</li><li>120h + 220(1 &minus; h) = 150.</li><li>220 &minus; 100h = 150.</li><li>100h = 70, so h = 0.70, that is 70%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 70</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Setting the miss path to 2m = 200 and forgetting the TLB time, which gives 200 &minus; 80h = 150 and hence 62.5%. The TLB is read on the miss path too.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A system uses segmentation with paging. Each logical address consists of an 8-bit segment number and a 24-bit offset, pages are 4 KB, and each page-table entry is 4 bytes. What is the maximum size, in KB, of one segment's page table?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With segmentation plus paging, the segment's offset is itself divided into a page number and a page offset, so the number of page-table entries for a segment is (maximum offset size) / (page size).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The maximum segment size is 2<sup>24</sup> bytes, since the offset is 24 bits.</li><li>Pages per segment = 2<sup>24</sup> / 2<sup>12</sup> = 2<sup>12</sup> = 4096.</li><li>Page-table size = 4096 entries &times; 4 bytes = 16,384 bytes.</li><li>16,384 bytes = 16 KB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the 8-bit segment number instead of the 24-bit offset, which gives 256 entries and 1 KB. The page table is per segment, so it is sized by the segment, not by the number of segments.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A byte-addressable main memory has 24 address lines. What is the capacity of the memory?", "options": ["8 MB", "16 MB", "32 MB", "64 MB"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a byte-addressable memory each address names one byte, so the number of distinct addresses is the number of bytes, and <code class=\"mm\">n</code> address lines give 2<sup>n</sup> addresses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>24 address lines give 2<sup>24</sup> = 16,777,216 distinct addresses.</li><li>Each address names one byte, so the memory holds 16,777,216 bytes.</li><li>16,777,216 B = 2<sup>24</sup> B = 2<sup>4</sup> &times; 2<sup>20</sup> B = 16 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 MB &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by the word width when the memory is byte-addressable, or reading 24 lines as 24 bits of storage. Address width fixes the number of locations; the data width, which is not given here, would fix the bytes per location.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A paged system uses a page size of 8 KB. How many bits of the logical address form the page offset?", "options": ["12 bits", "13 bits", "14 bits", "16 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The offset must be able to name every byte inside one page, so its width is <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub>(page size in bytes)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>8 KB = 8 &times; 2<sup>10</sup> bytes = 2<sup>13</sup> bytes.</li><li>Naming 2<sup>13</sup> byte positions needs 13 bits.</li><li>The remaining high-order bits of the logical address form the page number.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 13 bits &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 8 KB as 8 &times; 1024 = 8192 and then taking <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub> 8 = 3</code>, or answering 10 because the page size is 'in KB'. The page size must be converted to bytes first.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB is accessed in 5 ns and main memory in 95 ns. The TLB hit ratio is 100% and the page table is single-level and held in memory. What is the effective access time?", "options": ["95 ns", "100 ns", "105 ns", "190 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective access time is the probability-weighted cost of the paths a reference can take. With a hit ratio of 1 only the hit path is ever taken, but that path still pays the TLB lookup.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = TLB lookup + one memory access = 5 + 95 = 100 ns.</li><li>Hit ratio 1.00 means the miss path has probability 0, so it contributes nothing.</li><li>EAT = 1.00 &times; 100 = 100 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 100 ns &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 95 ns by dropping the TLB time, or 190 ns by charging a page-table read on every reference. The TLB time is paid on every access; only the page-table read is conditional on a miss.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A logical address consists of an 8-bit segment number and a 16-bit offset. What is the maximum number of segments a process can have?", "options": ["8", "128", "256", "65536"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In segmentation the segment number indexes the segment table, so the number of segments is fixed by the width of the segment number alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The segment number is 8 bits wide.</li><li>8 bits name 2<sup>8</sup> = 256 distinct segments.</li><li>The 16-bit offset fixes the maximum size of one segment at 2<sup>16</sup> = 64 KB; it does not affect the count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 256 &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 65536 from the offset width, or 8 because the field is 8 bits. The two fields answer two different questions: how many segments, and how large each may be.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "In a DRAM array, one refresh operation restores the charge in", "options": ["only the cell that was written most recently.", "every cell of the row being refreshed.", "every cell of the column being refreshed.", "exactly one cell."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DRAM array is organised as rows of cells that share a word line and a sense amplifier, so a row is the natural unit of a read-and-restore operation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The word line of the selected row turns on every cell transistor in that row.</li><li>The sense amplifiers read the whole row at once and drive the values back into the cells.</li><li>One refresh operation therefore restores all cells of one row; covering the array takes as many refresh operations as there are rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Every cell of the row being refreshed &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing refresh is per cell, which would need billions of operations, or per column, which does not match the array's wiring. Row-by-row refresh is what makes the refresh interval equal to retention time divided by the number of rows.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB is accessed in 10 ns, main memory in 90 ns, and the page table is single-level and in memory. If every reference misses the TLB, what is the effective access time?", "options": ["90 ns", "100 ns", "190 ns", "200 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a hit ratio of 0 every reference takes the miss path, and that path pays the TLB lookup, the page-table read and the data read.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>TLB lookup = 10 ns, paid on every reference.</li><li>Page-table entry read from memory = 90 ns.</li><li>Data word read from memory = 90 ns.</li><li>EAT = 10 + 90 + 90 = 190 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 190 ns &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 100 ns by forgetting the page-table read, or 200 ns by charging a second TLB lookup on the miss path. A miss costs one extra memory access, not an extra TLB access.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "In a segmented system, after the limit check succeeds, the physical address is formed as", "options": ["base + offset", "limit + offset", "segment number &times; page size + offset", "base &minus; offset"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A segment-table entry holds the physical base of the segment and its length. The offset is a displacement from that base, and the limit exists only to validate the offset.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The segment number indexes the segment table and yields (base, limit).</li><li>The hardware checks offset &lt; limit; a failure traps.</li><li>On success the physical address is base + offset.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: base + offset &mdash; option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the limit, which is a length and not an address, or importing the paging formula segment &times; page size, which assumes equal-size segments and a frame number.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A memory is built from four banks with low-order interleaving, so consecutive addresses fall in different banks. The main benefit is that", "options": ["the refresh overhead of the array is halved.", "successive words of a block can be supplied by different banks in parallel or in a pipeline, raising sustained bandwidth.", "address multiplexing is no longer required.", "each bank can hold twice as many words."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Interleaving spreads consecutive addresses across independent banks so that their access cycles can overlap; it changes when words become available, not how much is stored or how often refresh runs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With low-order interleaving address <code class=\"mm\">a</code> lives in bank <code class=\"mm\">a mod 4</code>.</li><li>Words <code class=\"mm\">a, a+1, a+2, a+3</code> therefore sit in four different banks and can be fetched concurrently or pipelined.</li><li>Capacity, refresh period and the multiplexing of row and column addresses are all unchanged by interleaving.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Successive words come from different banks and can be fetched in parallel &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting interleaving to save capacity or refresh time. It buys bandwidth for sequential access, and it can even make some strides slower by concentrating them on one bank.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A DRAM array has 4096 rows. Every row must be refreshed once within 64 ms, and one refresh operation occupies the memory for 90 ns. What percentage of memory time is spent on refresh?", "options": ["0.06%", "0.58%", "5.8%", "11.5%"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Refresh overhead is the total time the array spends refreshing during one retention interval, divided by that interval.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One full refresh pass costs 4096 &times; 90 ns = 368,640 ns = 368.64 &micro;s.</li><li>The pass must be completed within 64 ms = 64,000 &micro;s.</li><li>Fraction = 368.64 / 64,000 = 0.00576.</li><li>0.00576 = 0.576%, which rounds to 0.58%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0.58% &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing one refresh time by the interval (90 ns / 64 ms = 0.00014%) and forgetting that every row needs its own operation, or leaving the answer as a fraction instead of a percentage.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A machine has 1 GB of physical memory, a page size of 4 KB and 4-byte page-table entries. An inverted page table keeps one entry per frame. How many entries does it have, and how large is the table?", "options": ["2<sup>18</sup> entries, 1 MB", "2<sup>20</sup> entries, 4 MB", "2<sup>18</sup> entries, 256 KB", "2<sup>30</sup> entries, 4 GB"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An inverted page table is indexed by frame number rather than by virtual page number, so its size is fixed by physical memory and the page size, and it is independent of how large the virtual address space is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frames = 1 GB / 4 KB = 2<sup>30</sup> / 2<sup>12</sup> = 2<sup>18</sup> = 262,144.</li><li>One entry per frame gives 262,144 entries.</li><li>Table size = 262,144 &times; 4 B = 1,048,576 B = 1 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2<sup>18</sup> entries and 1 MB &mdash; option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Sizing it like a normal page table, one entry per virtual page (2<sup>20</sup> entries here), or forgetting that the entry holds the virtual page number as its tag and so is larger than a frame number alone.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A system has 36-bit physical addresses and a page size of 8 KB. How many bits of a page-table entry are needed to hold the frame number?", "options": ["20 bits", "23 bits", "24 bits", "36 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The frame number is the physical page number, so its width is the physical address width minus the page-offset width.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Page size 8 KB = 2<sup>13</sup> bytes, so the offset is 13 bits.</li><li>The frame number therefore takes 36 &minus; 13 = 23 bits.</li><li>Check: 2<sup>23</sup> frames &times; 2<sup>13</sup> bytes = 2<sup>36</sup> bytes of physical memory.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 23 bits &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 24 bits from 36 &minus; 12, which assumes 4 KB pages, or answering 36 because the physical address is 36 bits wide. The offset never appears in a page-table entry.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A paged system uses 4 KB pages. For the 32-bit logical address <code class=\"mm\">0x00003A7C</code>, what are the page number and the page offset?", "options": ["page 3, offset 2684", "page 0, offset 14972", "page 3, offset 1276", "page 58, offset 2684"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With 4 KB pages the low 12 bits of the logical address are the offset and the remaining high bits are the page number.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>4 KB = 2<sup>12</sup> bytes, so the offset is the low 12 bits of <code class=\"mm\">0x00003A7C</code>.</li><li>Low 12 bits = <code class=\"mm\">0xA7C</code> = 10&times;256 + 7&times;16 + 12 = 2684.</li><li>The remaining high bits are <code class=\"mm\">0x3</code>, so the page number is 3.</li><li>Check: 3 &times; 4096 + 2684 = 12,288 + 2684 = 14,972 = <code class=\"mm\">0x3A7C</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: page 3, offset 2684 &mdash; option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Taking the whole <code class=\"mm\">0x3A7C</code> as the offset, or splitting on a hex-digit boundary instead of a bit boundary: 12 bits is three hex digits, not four.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A byte-addressable 1 MB memory is built from 256K &times; 8-bit DRAM chips, where 1K = 2<sup>10</sup>. How many chips are needed, and how many address lines feed the chip-select decoder?", "options": ["4 chips, 2 lines", "4 chips, 18 lines", "8 chips, 3 lines", "2 chips, 1 line"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each chip supplies a fixed number of addressable bytes; the remaining high-order address bits select which chip answers, and a decoder with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> inputs selects one of 2<sup>k</sup> chips.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1 MB = 2<sup>20</sup> bytes, so the system has 20 address lines.</li><li>One chip is 256K &times; 8 bits = 2<sup>18</sup> bytes, so it consumes the low 18 address lines.</li><li>Chips needed = 2<sup>20</sup> / 2<sup>18</sup> = 4.</li><li>High-order lines left for selection = 20 &minus; 18 = 2, and 2<sup>2</sup> = 4 chips, so a 2-to-4 decoder is enough.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 chips and 2 decoder lines &mdash; option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Sending all 20 address lines into every chip, or feeding the decoder 4 lines because there are 4 chips. A decoder needs <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub></code> of the chip count, and the chips themselves take only the lines below that.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A machine has 48-bit virtual addresses, a page size of 4 KB and 8-byte page-table entries. Every page table must fit exactly inside one page. How many levels of paging are needed?", "options": ["2 levels", "3 levels", "4 levels", "5 levels"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The page number is cut into equal slices, one per level, and each slice must be just wide enough to index a full page of entries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Page size 4 KB = 2<sup>12</sup> bytes, so the offset is 12 bits and the page number is 48 &minus; 12 = 36 bits.</li><li>One page holds 4096 / 8 = 512 entries, and 512 = 2<sup>9</sup>, so each level consumes 9 bits.</li><li>36 / 9 = 4 exactly, so four levels cover the page number with nothing left over.</li><li>Check: 12 + 4&times;9 = 48 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 levels &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing 36 by 10 because a familiar 4-byte entry gives 1024 entries per page; with 8-byte entries the slice is 9 bits, not 10. Rounding up a fractional level is also wrong when the split is exact.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB is accessed in 10 ns, main memory in 100 ns, the hit ratio is 0.8 and the page table is single-level and in memory. On average, how many main-memory accesses does one data reference make?", "options": ["1.0", "1.2", "1.4", "2.0"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A memory access is made for the data in every case, and for the page-table entry only when the TLB misses, so the expected count is a two-outcome average.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On a TLB hit: one memory access, the data word.</li><li>On a TLB miss: two memory accesses, the page-table entry and then the data word.</li><li>Expected accesses = 0.8 &times; 1 + 0.2 &times; 2 = 0.8 + 0.4 = 1.2.</li><li>Cross-check with times: EAT = 10 + 1.2 &times; 100 = 130 ns, which equals 0.8(110) + 0.2(210).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.2 &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2.0 because a miss costs two accesses and assuming every reference misses, or 1.0 because every reference eventually touches memory once. The page-table read is the conditional part.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about DRAM refresh are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Refreshing a row rewrites the charge in every cell of that row.", "A refresh is internally a read of the row followed by a write-back of the sensed values.", "Refresh cycles consume memory-bus bandwidth that ordinary accesses cannot use.", "Once a cell is written, its charge stays unchanged until the next write to that same cell."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DRAM cell is a capacitor that leaks, so the stored charge must be restored periodically; refresh is an internal read-and-restore of one row at a time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The word line activates every cell in the row and the sense amplifiers restore all of them.</li><li>(B) True. There is no separate refresh mechanism; the array performs a row read and writes the sensed values back.</li><li>(C) True. Refresh occupies the array and the bus for its duration, so ordinary traffic waits.</li><li>(D) False. Charge leaks continuously, which is precisely why refresh is needed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating refresh as a write of the original data from outside, or believing it is free because it happens 'in the background'. It is a real read-and-write cycle that steals bandwidth.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about multi-level page tables are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Only the second-level tables for regions that are actually mapped need to exist in memory.", "Every first-level entry must point to an allocated second-level table.", "The number of levels is chosen so that one table fits inside one page.", "A multi-level table needs less memory than a single-level table when the address space is used sparsely."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Multi-level paging exists to stop a process from paying for page-table entries covering address ranges it never uses; the price is more memory references on a TLB miss.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. A second-level table is created only when some page under its first-level entry is mapped.</li><li>(B) False. Unmapped first-level entries hold an invalid bit, not a pointer.</li><li>(C) True. Sizing each table to one page is what makes the walk a fixed number of memory reads.</li><li>(D) True. For a sparse address space the untouched second-level tables simply do not exist, which is the whole point.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the hierarchy must be complete. Sparsity is the reason for the hierarchy: a fully populated address space would save nothing and only add walk cost.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about the choice of page size are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A larger page size reduces the number of page-table entries needed for a fixed logical address space.", "A larger page size increases the internal fragmentation in the last page of a region.", "A larger page size increases the number of TLB entries needed to cover a fixed working set.", "A larger page size reduces the number of TLB misses for a fixed working set."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Page size trades table size and translation coverage against wasted space at the end of regions; every effect below follows from 'fewer, larger pages'.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Entries = address space / page size, so doubling the page halves the entry count.</li><li>(B) True. A region rarely ends on a page boundary, and the larger the page the more of the last page is wasted.</li><li>(C) False. A fixed working set spans fewer pages when pages are larger, so fewer TLB entries cover it.</li><li>(D) True. Fewer pages per working set means fewer distinct translations and therefore fewer TLB misses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the TLB effect: larger pages improve TLB coverage, which is exactly why very large pages are offered for databases and virtual machines. The cost is fragmentation, not coverage.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about a segmentation-with-paging system are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The segment-table entry gives the base of that segment's own page table.", "The offset inside the segment is split into a page number and a page offset.", "A segment may be larger than a page.", "Combining segmentation with paging removes internal fragmentation completely."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Segmentation supplies the logical division and protection; paging supplies fixed-size allocation. The two are layered, with the segment's offset reinterpreted as a page number plus page offset.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Each segment has its own page table, and the segment-table entry points at it.</li><li>(B) True. The offset field is itself divided into page number and page offset.</li><li>(C) True. Segments are variable length and commonly span many pages.</li><li>(D) False. The last page of a segment is partly used, so internal fragmentation remains, though external fragmentation is gone.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the combination is fragmentation-free. Paging removes external fragmentation; the unused tail of a segment's final page is still internal fragmentation.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A system uses two-level paging. The TLB is accessed in 20 ns, main memory in 100 ns, and the TLB hit ratio is 0.98. On a TLB miss both page-table levels are read from memory. Which of the following are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The effective access time is 124 ns.", "With no TLB at all, the effective access time would be 300 ns.", "Raising the hit ratio to 1.00 lowers the effective access time by 4 ns.", "If the memory access time were halved and the TLB time kept at 20 ns, the effective access time would halve."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> EAT is the probability-weighted sum of the hit path and the miss path, and each extra page-table level adds exactly one memory access to the miss path only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = 20 + 100 = 120 ns.</li><li>Miss path = 20 + 100 + 100 + 100 = 320 ns (TLB, level 1, level 2, data).</li><li>(A) EAT = 0.98 &times; 120 + 0.02 &times; 320 = 117.6 + 6.4 = 124 ns. True.</li><li>(B) Without a TLB every reference walks both levels and reads the data: 3 &times; 100 = 300 ns. True.</li><li>(C) At h = 1 the EAT is the hit path, 120 ns, so the drop is 124 &minus; 120 = 4 ns. True.</li><li>(D) With m = 50: hit = 70, miss = 20 + 150 = 170, EAT = 0.98(70) + 0.02(170) = 72 ns, not 62 ns. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming EAT scales with memory time. The TLB term is independent of m, so halving m cannot halve the total; the same error appears whenever one component of a sum is fixed.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A DRAM chip is organised as 4M &times; 16 bits, where 1M = 2<sup>20</sup>. What is the capacity of the chip in MB?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8 MB", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Capacity in bytes is the number of locations times the number of bytes per location; the word width must be converted from bits to bytes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Locations = 4M = 4 &times; 2<sup>20</sup> = 2<sup>22</sup>.</li><li>Each location is 16 bits = 2 bytes.</li><li>Capacity = 2<sup>22</sup> &times; 2 B = 2<sup>23</sup> B = 8 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4, the number of locations in millions, or 16, the width. The product is 2<sup>23</sup> bytes, which is 8 MB, not 8 Mb or 4 MB.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB has 512 entries and the page size is 8 KB. What is the TLB reach in KB, that is, the total memory a fully resident TLB can map?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4096 KB (4 MB)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each TLB entry maps one whole page, so the reach is the number of entries times the page size, assuming the entries are all distinct.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One entry covers one page of 8 KB.</li><li>512 entries cover 512 &times; 8 KB.</li><li>512 &times; 8 = 4096 KB, which is 4 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4096 (KB)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 512, the entry count, or 4096 bytes by forgetting that the page size is in KB. Reach is a product of a count and a size, so the unit comes from the size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A TLB is accessed in 8 ns, main memory in 72 ns, the hit ratio is 0.95, and the page table is single-level and in memory. What is the effective access time in ns?<br/>(Enter the value to one decimal place.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "83.6 ns", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective access time averages the hit path and the miss path, and the TLB time is charged on both.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = 8 + 72 = 80 ns.</li><li>Miss path = 8 + 72 + 72 = 152 ns.</li><li>EAT = 0.95 &times; 80 + 0.05 &times; 152.</li><li>= 76 + 7.6 = 83.6 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 83.6 ns</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Computing 0.95 &times; 80 + 0.05 &times; 144 by omitting the TLB time from the miss path, which gives 83.2, or averaging to 116 ns by using the two memory accesses as the hit path.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A segment table holds segment 0 with base 1000 and limit 500, and segment 1 with base 4000 and limit 200. What physical address does the logical address (1, 150) produce?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4150", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Segmentation validates the offset against the segment limit and then adds the segment base, so the arithmetic is base + offset after a successful check.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The segment number is 1, so the entry is (base 4000, limit 200).</li><li>Check the offset: 150 &lt; 200, so the access is legal.</li><li>Physical address = 4000 + 150 = 4150.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4150</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using segment 0's base because the offset is small, which gives 1000 + 150 = 1150, or adding the limit 200 instead of the base, which gives 350. The segment number chooses the entry; the offset never does.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A machine uses a four-level page table for 64-bit virtual addresses, with 4 KB pages and 8-byte page-table entries. Each table fits exactly in one page. How many bits of the virtual address index the outermost (level-1) table?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "16 bits", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every level consumes the same number of index bits, fixed by how many entries fit in one page; whatever remains after the offset and the lower levels belongs to the outermost level.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Page size 4 KB = 2<sup>12</sup> bytes, so the offset is 12 bits.</li><li>One page holds 4096 / 8 = 512 entries, and 512 = 2<sup>9</sup>, so each level indexes 9 bits.</li><li>Four levels consume 4 &times; 9 = 36 bits.</li><li>Level-1 index = 64 &minus; 12 &minus; 36 = 16 bits.</li><li>Check: 16 + 36 + 12 = 64 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming all levels are equal and answering 9, or forgetting the 12-bit offset and answering 28. The outer level is the remainder, and it is usually the widest.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "In a k-stage instruction pipeline with perfectly balanced stages, no hazards and no register overhead, how many clock cycles are needed to complete n instructions?", "options": ["n + k − 1", "n × k", "n − k + 1", "n"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pipeline trades latency for throughput. The first instruction still has to walk through all <code class=\"mm\">k</code> stages, and only after that does one instruction finish per cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first instruction finishes at the end of cycle <code class=\"mm\">k</code>.</li><li>After that the pipeline is full, so one instruction completes in each of the remaining <code class=\"mm\">n − 1</code> cycles.</li><li>Total = <code class=\"mm\">k + (n − 1) = n + k − 1</code>.</li><li>For example, 10 instructions on 5 stages take 10 + 5 − 1 = 14 cycles, not 50.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: n + k − 1 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> n × k is the time an unpipelined machine needs, and n alone forgets that the pipeline must first fill and finally drain. The k − 1 extra cycles are the fill and drain, and they matter most when n is small.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A classic five-stage pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB) uses one unified memory for both instructions and data. Which structural hazard can arise?", "options": ["Two instructions both need the ALU in the same cycle", "The IF stage of one instruction and the MEM stage of another need the memory in the same cycle", "The ID stage reads the register file while the WB stage writes it", "The PC increment conflicts with the ALU"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A structural hazard is a resource conflict: two instructions in flight want the same hardware in the same cycle. The hazard exists only when the resource cannot serve both requests.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A single memory can answer one request per cycle.</li><li>In a five-stage pipeline, a load or store occupies MEM while the next instruction is in IF, so both want the memory at once.</li><li>The pipeline must therefore serialise them, either by stalling IF or by duplicating the resource.</li><li>The ALU conflict (option A) is avoided because only one instruction sits in EX per cycle in a single-issue pipeline; the register file conflict (option C) is avoided by giving it two read ports and one write port.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the IF and MEM stages contending for one memory (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C is the trap: reading the register file in ID while writing it in WB is legal when the file has separate read and write ports, which is exactly how the classic design avoids a hazard there.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A read-after-write (RAW) data hazard in a pipeline occurs when", "options": ["an instruction reads a register that an earlier instruction has not yet written", "two instructions write the same register", "an instruction writes a register that an earlier instruction has not yet read", "two instructions read the same register in the same cycle"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A data hazard is a dependence carried by a register or memory location. RAW is the true dependence: the reader must see the writer's value, and in a pipeline the reader may reach ID before the writer reaches WB.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The writer is still in EX or MEM when the reader wants the operand, so the register file does not hold the new value yet.</li><li>This is a read after write: the read is the dependent operation.</li><li>Option B is WAW and option C is WAR; both are name-induced and disappear when the pipeline keeps in-order execution with a single write stage.</li><li>Option D creates no hazard at all, since reads do not change state.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a read of a register that an earlier instruction has not yet written (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing RAW with WAW/WAR. Only RAW survives in a classic in-order five-stage pipeline; WAR and WAW appear only once instructions can overtake one another, as in out-of-order execution.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A control hazard arises in a pipeline because", "options": ["the outcome of a branch is not known at the time the following instructions must be fetched", "two instructions need the same functional unit in the same cycle", "a load's data is needed by the very next instruction", "the register file has fewer write ports than the pipeline needs"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The pipeline fetches ahead of execution, so it must decide the next PC before the branch condition has been evaluated. If the guess is wrong, the instructions fetched in the meantime are wrong too.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The branch condition is computed in EX (or later), while IF is fetching <code class=\"mm\">k − 1</code> instructions ahead.</li><li>Until the condition is known the next PC is unknown, so the fetcher must guess.</li><li>A wrong guess means discarding the speculative instructions, which is the branch penalty.</li><li>Option B is structural, option C is a data hazard, and option D is also structural.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the branch outcome is unknown while the fetch unit must already supply the next PC (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a control hazard as a data hazard because both cause stalls. A control hazard is about the program counter; a data hazard is about operand values.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "In a five-stage pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB) a conditional branch is resolved in the EX stage and no prediction is used. How many cycles are lost when the branch is taken?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The branch penalty is the number of stages between the fetch of the branch and the stage that resolves it, because every instruction fetched in that window is speculative.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The branch itself is fetched in IF and its condition is settled at the end of EX, which is two stages later.</li><li>Meanwhile the next two instructions have already been fetched, one into ID and one into IF.</li><li>When the branch is taken, both must be discarded: two cycles are lost.</li><li>The count is the stage distance EX − IF = 2, and it is independent of the pipeline depth only through that distance, not through the total number of stages.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 by counting the stages up to MEM, or 4 by counting the whole pipeline. Only the instructions fetched before resolution are wrong, and that window ends at the stage that resolves the branch.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline runs on a 2 ns clock and executes 100 independent instructions with no hazards and balanced stages. What is the total execution time?", "options": ["200 ns", "208 ns", "210 ns", "104 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Execution time = number of clock cycles × clock period, and the cycle count of a <code class=\"mm\">k</code>-stage pipeline is <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cycles = 100 + 5 − 1 = 104.</li><li>Clock period = 2 ns.</li><li>Time = 104 × 2 = 208 ns.</li><li>A pipelined machine is limited by its slowest stage, so the 2 ns clock is the period of every stage, including the fast ones.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 208 ns</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 200 ns is the trap: it assumes the first instruction completes in cycle 1. The four fill cycles are real time and must be paid once.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following changes removes the structural hazard that a five-stage pipeline with a single memory suffers?", "options": ["Adding forwarding paths from EX to ID", "Using separate instruction and data caches", "Doubling the clock frequency", "Adding a branch target buffer"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A structural hazard is cured by giving the contending stages their own copy of the resource, or by making the resource accept two requests per cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The conflict is between the instruction fetch and the memory data access of a load or store.</li><li>Splitting the memory into an instruction cache and a data cache gives each stage a private port, so both requests proceed in the same cycle.</li><li>Forwarding (option A) treats data hazards, not resource conflicts.</li><li>A faster clock (option C) makes the conflict happen more often, and a branch target buffer (option D) treats control hazards.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: separate instruction and data caches (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reaching for forwarding as a universal fix. Forwarding moves values between stages; it does not create a second memory port.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline with one memory executes 100 instructions, 30 of which are loads or stores. Each memory access in MEM that collides with IF stalls the whole pipeline for one cycle. How many cycles does the program take in total?", "options": ["130", "134", "104", "160"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Total cycles = fill and drain + one cycle per instruction + one stall cycle per conflicting access.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fill and drain cost <code class=\"mm\">k − 1 = 4</code> cycles, so the hazard-free count is 100 + 4 = 104.</li><li>Each of the 30 loads or stores collides once and costs one stall.</li><li>Total = 104 + 30 = 134 cycles.</li><li>The same answer in CPI form: 134 / 100 = 1.34.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 134 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the fill and drain and answering 130, or charging a stall for every instruction instead of only the 30 that touch memory.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "With full forwarding, which of the following instruction pairs still costs at least one stall cycle in a five-stage pipeline?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">add r1, r2, r3</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">sub r4, r1, r5</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">lw r1, 0(r2)</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">add r3, r1, r4</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">add r1, r2, r3</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">sw r1, 0(r4)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">lw r1, 0(r2)</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">sw r1, 0(r3)</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Forwarding moves a value from where it is produced to where it is needed, so a hazard survives only when the producer is too late for the consumer's stage.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An ALU result exists at the end of EX, so a following ALU instruction can take it from the EX/MEM register: option A costs 0 stalls.</li><li>A loaded value exists only at the end of MEM, one stage later than an ALU result. The dependent ALU instruction needs it in EX, so one cycle must be inserted: option B costs 1 stall.</li><li>A store reads its data in the MEM stage, by which time the ALU result has long been available: option C costs 0 stalls.</li><li>For option D the store's data is also read in MEM, after the load's value is available, so no stall is needed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the load followed immediately by a dependent instruction (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every RAW pair costs a stall. Only a load followed immediately by its consumer does, because the load's value appears one stage later than an ALU result.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Three consecutive instructions are add r1, r2, r3; add r4, r1, r5; add r6, r4, r7. With full ALU forwarding, how many stall cycles does the pipeline insert?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An ALU result is ready at the end of EX and can be forwarded to the EX stage of the very next instruction, so a chain of dependent ALU operations needs no stall.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first <code class=\"mm\">add</code> produces <code class=\"mm\">r1</code> at the end of its EX stage.</li><li>The second instruction enters EX in the next cycle and takes <code class=\"mm\">r1</code> from the EX/MEM register.</li><li>The third instruction does the same with <code class=\"mm\">r4</code>.</li><li>No cycle is inserted: the answer is 0.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0 stall cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging one stall per dependence. That is the behaviour without forwarding, where the value is not visible until WB; with forwarding the back-to-back ALU chain is free.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "An unpipelined processor has a critical path that divides into four stages of 3 ns, 2 ns, 4 ns and 2 ns. The four stages are pipelined with zero register overhead and 1000 instructions are run. What is the speedup, to two decimal places?", "options": ["2.74", "2.75", "3.67", "4.00"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup = unpipelined time ÷ pipelined time, where the pipelined clock is set by the <b>slowest</b> stage and the cycle count includes fill and drain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unpipelined time per instruction = 3 + 2 + 4 + 2 = 11 ns, so 1000 instructions take 11000 ns.</li><li>The pipelined clock must be 4 ns, the maximum stage delay.</li><li>Pipelined cycles = 1000 + 4 − 1 = 1003, so time = 1003 × 4 = 4012 ns.</li><li>Speedup = 11000 / 4012 = 2.7418…, which is 2.74 to two decimal places.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.74</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4.00 by assuming the ideal k-fold speedup. Unbalanced stages destroy part of the gain: the clock is stretched to 4 ns while the average stage is only 2.75 ns, and the fill cycles cost a little more.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A six-stage pipeline executes 200 independent instructions with no hazards. If efficiency is defined as speedup ÷ number of stages, what is the efficiency, to one decimal place?", "options": ["97.6%", "95.2%", "100.0%", "92.3%"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup = (n × k) / (n + k − 1); efficiency = speedup / k, which measures how much of the ideal per-stage gain is actually realised.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unpipelined cycles = 200 × 6 = 1200.</li><li>Pipelined cycles = 200 + 6 − 1 = 205.</li><li>Speedup = 1200 / 205 = 5.8537.</li><li>Efficiency = 5.8537 / 6 = 0.97561, that is 97.6%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 97.6%</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming efficiency is always 100% because there are no hazards. The fill and drain cycles alone prevent it: efficiency approaches 1 only as n grows without bound.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline contains a single unpipelined floating-point unit that occupies the EX stage for four cycles and is used by 30% of all instructions. Each extra EX cycle stalls the whole pipeline for one cycle. What is the CPI?", "options": ["1.30", "1.90", "2.20", "2.50"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An unpipelined multi-cycle functional unit is a structural resource: while one instruction holds it, no other instruction can reach EX, so each extra occupancy cycle costs the pipeline one cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A floating-point instruction occupies EX for 4 cycles instead of 1, so it adds 3 stall cycles.</li><li>Its share of the instruction stream is 0.30.</li><li>Added CPI = 0.30 × 3 = 0.90.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.90 = 1.90.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: CPI = 1.90</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging 4 stall cycles instead of 3, which double-counts the one EX cycle the instruction would have used anyway, or forgetting that the stall blocks every instruction, not just other floating-point ones.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline has full forwarding, including load-use forwarding. Consider", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each dependence is settled by asking which stage produces the value and which stage consumes it. A load produces at the end of MEM, an ALU instruction at the end of EX, and a store consumes its data in MEM.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>I1 → I2: <code class=\"mm\">r1</code> comes from a load, and I2 needs it in EX one cycle after I1's MEM. One stall.</li><li>I2 → I3: <code class=\"mm\">r2</code> comes from an ALU operation, and I3 needs it in EX for the address. Forwarding covers it: no stall.</li><li>I3 → I4: <code class=\"mm\">r3</code> comes from a load, so I4 waits one cycle: second stall.</li><li>I4 → I5 and I5 → I6: both are ALU results forwarded to EX, and the store reads <code class=\"mm\">r5</code> in MEM, which is even later. No further stalls. Total 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 stall cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging a stall for every dependent pair (which gives 4 or 5), or charging one for the store because it names <code class=\"mm\">r5</code>. A store reads its data in MEM, so the value is ready long before it is needed.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline executes a program in which 20% of the instructions are conditional branches. Branches are resolved in EX and the processor uses static not-taken prediction; 60% of the branches are actually taken. What is the CPI, to two decimal places?", "options": ["1.00", "1.12", "1.24", "1.40"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With not-taken prediction the penalty is paid only on the taken branches, and each misprediction costs the cycles between fetch and resolution.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fraction of instructions that are taken branches = 0.20 × 0.60 = 0.12.</li><li>Each taken branch costs 2 cycles, the distance from IF to EX.</li><li>Added CPI = 0.12 × 2 = 0.24.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.24 = 1.24.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: CPI = 1.24</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by the branch frequency twice, or charging every branch instead of only the taken ones. Under not-taken prediction a not-taken branch is free.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about an ideal k-stage instruction pipeline are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Its peak throughput is one completed instruction per clock cycle.", "Its ideal speedup over the unpipelined machine is <code class=\"mm\">k</code>.", "The latency of a single instruction is smaller than on the unpipelined machine.", "Filling and draining the pipeline costs <code class=\"mm\">k − 1</code> extra cycles for a program of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> instructions."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pipeline improves throughput, not the latency of one instruction. The ideal model assumes balanced stages, no hazards and no register overhead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Once full, one instruction leaves the pipeline per cycle. True.</li><li>(B) With perfect balance the cycle time is <code class=\"mm\">1/k</code> of the unpipelined critical path, so speedup tends to <code class=\"mm\">k</code>. True.</li><li>(C) A single instruction still traverses all <code class=\"mm\">k</code> stages, and pipeline registers only add delay, so latency is at best equal and usually larger. False.</li><li>(D) The first instruction needs <code class=\"mm\">k</code> cycles and the last drains <code class=\"mm\">k − 1</code> after the n-th issue, giving n + k − 1 cycles. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C is the usual slip: pipelining shortens the time between successive completions, not the time one instruction spends inside.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following situations is a structural hazard in a pipelined processor? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Two instructions need the single memory port in the same cycle.", "Two instructions need the single unpipelined multiplier in the same cycle.", "An instruction reads a register that an earlier instruction has not yet written.", "Two instructions try to write the same register."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A structural hazard is a conflict over hardware, not over data. If the resource cannot serve both requests in the same cycle, one of them must wait.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) One memory port cannot answer an instruction fetch and a data access together. Structural. True.</li><li>(B) An unpipelined multiplier is busy for several cycles, so a second request must wait. Structural. True.</li><li>(C) This is a RAW data hazard: the register value is not ready, the hardware is free. False.</li><li>(D) Two writes to one register form a WAW data hazard in an out-of-order machine; the register file port is not oversubscribed by it in the classic pipeline. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A) and (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling every conflict a structural hazard. Ask whether a piece of hardware is wanted twice in one cycle; if the answer is a stale value instead, it is a data hazard.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about data hazards and forwarding are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Forwarding removes the stall between two dependent ALU instructions.", "A load followed immediately by an instruction that uses its result still needs one stall, because the loaded value is available only after MEM.", "In a classic five-stage in-order pipeline with a single WB stage, WAR and WAW hazards cannot occur.", "Forwarding alone can eliminate every RAW stall."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Forwarding shortens the distance a value must travel, but it cannot make a value appear before it exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The ALU result exists at the end of EX and is forwarded to the next instruction's EX. True.</li><li>(B) A load's data appears at the end of MEM, one stage later than an ALU result, so the consumer waits one cycle even with load-use forwarding. True.</li><li>(C) In-order issue and in-order write mean that no later instruction can write or read before an earlier one, so only RAW arises. True.</li><li>(D) The load-use case and, in a deeper pipeline, any producer that finishes too late still cost a stall. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing forwarding is complete. It handles the ALU-to-ALU case perfectly and the load-use case only after one stall; scheduling the load earlier is what removes that stall.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following reduce the penalty paid when a branch is taken? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Resolving the branch in ID instead of in EX.", "A branch target buffer that supplies the target address in the IF stage.", "Delayed branching, with useful instructions placed in the delay slots.", "Forwarding the branch outcome from EX back to ID."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The penalty equals the number of instructions fetched before the branch is resolved, so anything that shortens that window or fills it with useful work helps.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Moving resolution from EX to ID removes one speculative instruction, cutting the penalty from 2 cycles to 1. True.</li><li>(B) A branch target buffer predicts direction and supplies the target in IF, so the correct path is fetched from the start. True.</li><li>(C) Delay slots are instructions that execute anyway, so they are work rather than waste. True.</li><li>(D) Forwarding moves data values between stages; it does not decide a branch earlier, so the penalty is unchanged. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating forwarding as a cure for control hazards. Forwarding can only deliver a value that already exists; the branch condition still has to be evaluated in some stage.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about branch prediction are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Always-taken prediction beats always-not-taken prediction when most branches are taken.", "A mispredicted branch costs the cycles between fetch and the stage in which the branch is resolved.", "A dynamic predictor uses the recorded history of past branch outcomes.", "A correctly predicted branch removes the need to fetch from the target address."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Prediction converts a control hazard into a bet: the pipeline fetches speculatively and pays only when the bet is wrong.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The expected penalty is the misprediction rate times the penalty, so a predictor that matches the common outcome wins. True.</li><li>(B) The penalty is exactly the number of speculative instructions discarded. True.</li><li>(C) A dynamic predictor indexes a table by branch history or by the branch address and updates it after each outcome. True.</li><li>(D) A correct prediction still requires fetching from the target when the branch is taken; the prediction decides which address to fetch, it does not remove the fetch. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading option D as if prediction were a cache of the target instruction. The instruction must still be fetched, from the predicted address rather than the fall-through one.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline executes 50 instructions with no hazards. Including fill and drain, how many clock cycles does it take?", "options": [], "answer_display": "54", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The cycle count of a <code class=\"mm\">k</code>-stage pipeline is <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>n = 50 and k = 5.</li><li>Cycles = 50 + 5 − 1 = 54.</li><li>Equivalently, 5 cycles to fill and 49 further issues, one per cycle.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 54 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 250 by multiplying instructions by stages, or 50 by assuming the first instruction finishes in cycle 1.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A four-stage pipeline has a balanced stage delay of 5 ns and no register overhead. How much time, in nanoseconds, does it need for 100 independent instructions?", "options": [], "answer_display": "515", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With balanced stages the clock period is the stage delay, and the cycle count is <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Clock period = 5 ns.</li><li>Cycles = 100 + 4 − 1 = 103.</li><li>Time = 103 × 5 = 515 ns.</li><li>Check against the unpipelined figure: 100 × 4 × 5 = 2000 ns, so the speedup is about 3.88.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 515 ns</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 100 cycles and answering 500 ns. The three fill cycles are the reason a short program never reaches the full k-fold speedup.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A program of 1000 instructions contains 15% conditional branches. The processor uses static not-taken prediction, branches are resolved in EX with a 2-cycle penalty, and 50% of the branches are taken. How many extra cycles do branch mispredictions add?", "options": [], "answer_display": "150", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under not-taken prediction only the taken branches are mispredicted, so the extra cycles are (instructions × branch fraction × taken fraction × penalty).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Branches = 1000 × 0.15 = 150.</li><li>Taken branches = 150 × 0.50 = 75.</li><li>Extra cycles = 75 × 2 = 150.</li><li>As a CPI figure that is 150 / 1000 = 0.15 cycles per instruction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 150 extra cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging the penalty to all 150 branches (giving 300) and forgetting that a not-taken branch is predicted correctly for free.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "In a five-stage pipeline with a single unified memory, 25% of the executed instructions are loads or stores, and each one costs exactly one stall cycle because of the IF/MEM conflict. What is the CPI, to two decimal places?", "options": [], "answer_display": "1.25", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CPI = ideal CPI + stall cycles per instruction, where the stalls come from the fraction of instructions that trigger the conflict.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Ideal CPI for a scalar pipeline = 1.</li><li>Stalls per instruction = 0.25 × 1 = 0.25.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.25 = 1.25.</li><li>In cycles: 100 instructions take 100 + 4 + 25 = 129 cycles, and 129 / 100 ≈ 1.29 once fill and drain are amortised; the CPI figure here excludes the one-off fill.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: CPI = 1.25</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying 0.25 by the number of stages, or charging the stall to every instruction. Only the memory-touching instructions collide with IF.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A program executes 500 instructions. Of these, 40% are loads, and 25% of those loads are immediately followed by an instruction that uses the loaded value. With load-use forwarding, how many stall cycles occur in total?", "options": [], "answer_display": "50", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Load-use forwarding still inserts one stall per adjacent load-consumer pair, so the count is the number of such pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Loads = 500 × 0.40 = 200.</li><li>Adjacent consumers = 200 × 0.25 = 50.</li><li>Each such pair costs exactly one stall cycle.</li><li>Total = 50 stall cycles, which is 0.10 cycles per instruction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 50 stall cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 200 by charging a stall to every load. A load whose result is not needed by the next instruction costs nothing.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A k-stage pipeline has clock period T and no hazards. Once the pipeline is full, the number of instructions completed per unit time is", "options": ["k / T", "k / (nT)", "1 / T", "1 / (kT)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In steady state a pipeline retires one instruction per clock cycle, so throughput is the reciprocal of the clock period and does not depend on the depth <code class=\"mm\">k</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The pipeline is full, so every stage holds one instruction.</li><li>In each cycle every instruction moves on by one stage, so exactly one instruction leaves at WB.</li><li>One completion every <code class=\"mm\">T</code> seconds means <code class=\"mm\">1/T</code> instructions per second.</li><li>The depth <code class=\"mm\">k</code> sets how long one instruction takes (latency <code class=\"mm\">kT</code>), not how often instructions finish.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">1 / T</code> instructions per unit time (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option D confuses latency with throughput: <code class=\"mm\">1/(kT)</code> would mean a full pipeline finishes one instruction every <code class=\"mm\">k</code> cycles, which is the unpipelined rate divided by <code class=\"mm\">k</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "In a synchronous pipelined datapath, the clock period is set by", "options": ["the sum of the combinational delays of all stages", "the average of the stage delays", "the delay of the fastest stage", "the delay of the slowest stage plus the pipeline-register overhead"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every stage must finish its work and latch its result inside one clock period, so the period is the worst-case stage path, plus the setup and propagation delay of the inter-stage registers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All stages are driven by the same clock, so they all get the same amount of time.</li><li>A stage that is slower than the period would not finish before its output register latches, so the period must cover the slowest stage.</li><li>The register itself adds delay, so the period is the slowest combinational delay plus the register overhead.</li><li>A balanced design equalises the stage delays to keep that maximum, and therefore the period, as small as possible.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: slowest stage delay plus register overhead (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The sum of all stage delays is the latency of one instruction, not the clock period; using it would throw away the whole point of pipelining.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB) uses one unified memory for instructions and data. Which pair of stages can demand that memory in the same clock cycle?", "options": ["IF and MEM", "ID and EX", "EX and WB", "ID and WB"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A structural hazard is a resource conflict: two instructions in different stages want the same hardware in the same cycle. With one memory, the fetch of a new instruction and the data access of an older load or store collide.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>IF reads the instruction word from memory; MEM reads or writes a data word.</li><li>The two stages run in parallel on different instructions, so both want the memory in the same cycle.</li><li>One of them must wait, costing one cycle for every such collision.</li><li>The cure is a Harvard split into separate instruction and data memories, or an extra memory port.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: IF and MEM (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> ID reads registers and EX uses the ALU, so those pairs need no memory at all; naming them mistakes a data dependence for a resource conflict.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "In a simple in-order five-stage pipeline that holds one instruction per stage, which data hazards cannot arise at all?", "options": ["RAW only", "WAR only", "RAW and WAW", "WAR and WAW"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Hazards are named by the order of the accesses in the original program. RAW (read after write) is a true dependence; WAR and WAW are name dependences that need the accesses to be reordered before they can bite.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In this pipeline every instruction passes through the stages in program order and only one instruction is in each stage.</li><li>A later instruction can never read a register before an earlier instruction writes it, nor write before an earlier instruction reads it, in a way that changes the order of accesses.</li><li>Only RAW survives, because the producer reaches WB while the consumer needs the value earlier, in EX.</li><li>WAR and WAW appear once instructions can overtake each other, as in out-of-order execution.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: WAR and WAW (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Some candidates answer \"RAW only\", which is what remains rather than what is impossible. In-order issue removes the name dependences, it does not remove RAW.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline resolves a conditional branch in the ID stage. It uses no branch prediction and no delay slots. When the branch is taken, how many instructions already in the pipeline are squashed?", "options": ["0", "3", "1", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The branch penalty is the number of stages between instruction fetch and the stage that resolves the branch, because one new instruction is fetched in each of those cycles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In the cycle the branch sits in ID, the next sequential instruction has already been fetched and is sitting in IF.</li><li>Resolution in ID means the outcome is known one stage after fetch, so exactly one wrong instruction exists.</li><li>That instruction is flushed and the target is fetched next, so the penalty is 1 cycle.</li><li>Resolving in EX instead would let two wrong instructions in, giving a penalty of 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 instruction (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the branch itself as squashed, or adding the fetch of the target, gives 2. The branch is not squashed: it is the instruction that decides the target.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "For an ideal k-stage pipeline running a very long program (n → ∞), the speedup over the same datapath without pipelining approaches", "options": ["1", "k", "n", "n / k"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup is unpipelined time divided by pipelined time. With balanced stages and no overhead, unpipelined time is <code class=\"mm\">nk</code> cycles and pipelined time is <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code> cycles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unpipelined: <code class=\"mm\">n</code> instructions at <code class=\"mm\">k</code> cycles each, so <code class=\"mm\">nk</code> cycles.</li><li>Pipelined: <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code> cycles.</li><li>Speedup = <code class=\"mm\">nk / (n + k − 1)</code>, which tends to <code class=\"mm\">k</code> as <code class=\"mm\">n</code> grows.</li><li>The fill and drain cost <code class=\"mm\">k − 1</code> cycles, so a short program never reaches <code class=\"mm\">k</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">k</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">n/k</code> treats the pipeline as finishing k instructions per cycle, which would need k issue slots per cycle, not just k stages.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A datapath is cut into four stages whose combinational delays are 4 ns, 3 ns, 6 ns and 3 ns. Each pipeline register adds 1 ns. How long does it take to complete 20 instructions?", "options": ["140 ns", "184 ns", "161 ns", "207 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period is the slowest stage plus register overhead, and the cycle count is <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code>. Unbalanced stages waste the slack in the fast ones.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Slowest stage = 6 ns, so clock period = 6 + 1 = 7 ns.</li><li>Cycles = <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code> = 20 + 4 − 1 = 23.</li><li>Time = 23 × 7 = 161 ns.</li><li>The unpipelined datapath would need 16 ns per instruction, or 320 ns for the same 20 instructions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 161 ns (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 184 ns comes from using 8 cycles of fill and drain, or from adding the register overhead twice; 140 ns comes from ignoring the registers altogether.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline reads two source registers in ID and writes one destination register in WB. Its register file has two read ports and one write port. Which change would create a structural hazard in a single-issue pipeline?", "options": ["resolving branches in ID instead of EX", "adding forwarding paths from EX/MEM to EX", "splitting the unified memory into separate instruction and data memories", "cutting the register file down to a single read port"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ports are a resource. Two reads in one cycle need two read ports; one write in the same cycle needs a third, independent port.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An instruction in ID reads two source registers in the same cycle, so one read port would have to serve both.</li><li>With a single read port the second operand cannot be fetched, so the instruction stalls: a structural hazard.</li><li>Branch resolution in ID uses no extra register-file port, forwarding only adds bypass paths, and separate memories remove a conflict rather than create one.</li><li>So A, B and C all leave the register file able to serve two reads and one write in a cycle, and only D breaks that.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a register file with a single read port (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C is the reverse of a fix: separate instruction and data memories are the standard cure for a structural hazard, not a cause of one.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Consider lw r1, 0(r2) immediately followed by sw r1, 8(r3) in a five-stage pipeline with full forwarding, including a path from the MEM/WB register to the store-data input. How many stall cycles are needed?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A store needs its data operand at the start of MEM, not at the start of EX. The load has produced its value by the end of its own MEM stage, which is exactly when the store is entering MEM.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cycle 1: lw in IF. Cycle 2: lw in ID, sw in IF. Cycle 3: lw in EX, sw in ID. Cycle 4: lw in MEM, sw in EX. Cycle 5: lw in WB, sw in MEM.</li><li>The store consumes r1 in MEM, which is cycle 5; the load writes r1 at the end of cycle 4 into MEM/WB.</li><li>A bypass from MEM/WB into the store-data input delivers the value in time.</li><li>So no stall is needed, even though the same pair of instructions would cost one stall if the second instruction were an ALU operation reading r1 in EX.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0 stall cycles (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the load-use rule blindly gives 1. That rule applies when the consumer needs the value at the start of EX; a store needs it one stage later.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A program has base CPI 1 with no branches. 25% of its instructions are conditional branches, 40% of those branches are taken, and each taken branch costs 3 cycles. What is the effective CPI?", "options": ["1.10", "1.15", "1.75", "1.30"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under static not-taken prediction the only lost cycles come from taken branches, so the added CPI is branch frequency × taken fraction × penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Extra cycles per instruction = 0.25 × 0.40 × 3.</li><li>0.25 × 0.40 = 0.10 taken branches per instruction.</li><li>0.10 × 3 = 0.30 extra cycles per instruction.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.30 = 1.30.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.30 (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by the branch frequency alone (0.25 × 3 = 0.75) assumes every branch is taken; 1.75 is that error.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline executes 120 independent instructions. Including fill and drain and ignoring register overhead, the speedup over the unpipelined machine with the same clock is closest to", "options": ["4.84", "5.00", "4.00", "4.62"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup = <code class=\"mm\">nk / (n + k − 1)</code>. The fill and drain stop a finite program from reaching the ideal <code class=\"mm\">k</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unpipelined cycles = 120 × 5 = 600.</li><li>Pipelined cycles = 120 + 5 − 1 = 124.</li><li>Speedup = 600 / 124 = 4.8387…</li><li>That is 96.8% of the ideal 5, the shortfall being the 4 cycles of fill and drain.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.84 (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 5.00 is the asymptotic limit; the question fixes n = 120, where the fill and drain are still felt.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A processor has separate instruction and data memories, but the data memory delivers only one word every two processor cycles. Loads and stores make up 35% of the instructions and each needs one data-memory word. With no other hazards, the CPI is", "options": ["1.00", "1.70", "1.35", "1.175"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A slow data memory is a structural limit on MEM. Every access that cannot complete in one cycle adds the extra cycles it occupies, weighted by how often such accesses occur.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A load or store occupies MEM for 2 cycles instead of 1, so it adds 1 cycle.</li><li>Fraction of instructions that are loads or stores = 0.35.</li><li>Extra cycles per instruction = 0.35 × 1 = 0.35.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.35 = 1.35.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.35 (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 1.70 charges 2 extra cycles per access instead of 1, forgetting that the MEM stage already accounts for the first of the two cycles.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline writes the register file in the first half of a cycle and reads it in the second half, and has full forwarding. An instruction that produces a value is followed three instructions later by one that reads it. How many stall cycles does the pair need?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dependence separated by three instructions is the boundary case: the producer reaches WB in the same cycle the consumer reads in ID, so the write-first read-second register file closes the gap with no forwarding and no stall.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Producer in WB at cycle 5; the consumer three instructions later is in ID at cycle 5.</li><li>The write happens in the first half of the cycle and the read in the second half, so the consumer sees the new value.</li><li>No bypass and no stall are needed; a producer only two instructions ahead would be in MEM at cycle 4 and would need the EX/MEM bypass path instead.</li><li>Had the register file read in the first half and written in the second, this distance would cost one stall.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0 stall cycles (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The reflex \"three apart still needs a stall\" comes from pipelines without write-first ordering; with forwarding the two-instruction gap is already covered, and three is well clear.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A branch target buffer supplies both the predicted direction and the target address during IF. When its prediction is correct, the number of cycles lost to the branch is", "options": ["1", "0", "2", "equal to the number of pipeline stages"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A branch costs nothing when the fetch unit never fetches a wrong instruction. A BTB achieves that by predicting at fetch time and giving the target in the same cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Without prediction, fetch continues sequentially until the branch is resolved, and the wrong instructions must be flushed.</li><li>A BTB is indexed at fetch, returns a prediction and a target address in the same cycle, so fetch continues at the target.</li><li>No wrong instruction is ever fetched, so no cycle is lost.</li><li>The remaining cost is the penalty when the prediction is wrong, which is still the full distance to resolution.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0 cycles (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The stage count is the misprediction penalty, not the cost of a correct prediction.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A datapath is pipelined into 5 stages of 2 ns each with 0.4 ns of register overhead per stage. The same logic is re-cut into 10 stages of 1 ns each, keeping the 0.4 ns overhead. The steady-state throughput speedup is closest to", "options": ["1.20", "1.43", "2.00", "1.71"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deeper pipelining shortens the combinational path per stage but the register overhead is paid at every stage, so throughput gains sub-linearly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>5-stage clock = 2 + 0.4 = 2.4 ns, throughput = 1/2.4 per ns.</li><li>10-stage clock = 1 + 0.4 = 1.4 ns, throughput = 1/1.4 per ns.</li><li>Speedup = 2.4 / 1.4 = 1.714…</li><li>The ideal factor of 2 shrinks to about 1.71 because the fixed 0.4 ns overhead is a larger fraction of the shorter stage.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.71 (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 2.00 counts only the halved stage delay and forgets that the overhead is now paid twice as often.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about an ideal k-stage pipeline (balanced stages, no hazards, no register overhead) are TRUE?", "options": ["The first instruction leaves the pipeline after k clock cycles.", "Doubling the number of stages doubles the speedup on a finite program.", "Once the pipeline is full, one instruction completes in every clock cycle.", "In steady state the pipeline holds exactly k instructions."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pipeline raises throughput to one instruction per cycle and leaves the latency of a single instruction at <code class=\"mm\">k</code> cycles. Fill and drain make the speedup on a short program fall short of <code class=\"mm\">k</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: the first instruction must traverse all <code class=\"mm\">k</code> stages.</li><li>(B) False: cycles are <code class=\"mm\">n + k − 1</code>, so a larger <code class=\"mm\">k</code> also lengthens the fill and drain, and any real register overhead grows too.</li><li>(C) True: that is the definition of the steady state.</li><li>(D) True: with one instruction per stage and no stalls, the number in flight equals the number of stages.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option B is the common error: it takes the asymptotic limit <code class=\"mm\">k</code> as if it held for every program length.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following are structural hazards?", "options": ["A load in MEM and an instruction fetch in IF both need the single unified memory.", "In a dual-issue machine, two instructions reach EX in the same cycle but only one ALU exists.", "A conditional branch is resolved in EX while the next sequential instruction is already in IF.", "A multi-cycle divider still occupies EX when the following instruction needs that stage."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A structural hazard is two instructions wanting one hardware resource in the same cycle. A branch that is still being resolved is a control hazard, not a resource conflict.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: one memory, two stages, one cycle.</li><li>(B) True: one ALU, two instructions in EX.</li><li>(C) False: no resource is contended; the problem is that the next instruction may be the wrong one.</li><li>(D) True: the divider holds EX for several cycles, so the next instruction cannot enter it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C feels like \"two things at once\", but the conflict there is over the program counter, not over a functional unit, so it is a control hazard.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline has full forwarding. Which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["An ALU result can be forwarded to the very next instruction's EX input, so an ALU-to-ALU dependence costs no stall.", "A load result is available early enough that a load followed immediately by its consumer needs no stall.", "A store reads its data operand in MEM, so a load followed immediately by a store of the same register needs no stall.", "With forwarding, a chain of dependent ALU instructions costs no stalls, although the latency of one instruction is unchanged."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Forwarding moves a value from the output of a stage to the input of a stage. Whether a stall remains depends on when the producer produces and when the consumer consumes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: the ALU output exists at the end of EX and can be bypassed into the next EX.</li><li>(B) False: a load produces its value at the end of MEM, one cycle too late for a consumer that needs it at the start of EX, so one stall remains.</li><li>(C) True: the store consumes its data in MEM, one stage after EX, so the timing works out.</li><li>(D) True: forwarding removes stalls, not latency; the instruction still passes through all five stages.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option B is the classic trap: \"full forwarding\" is often taken to mean \"no stalls ever\", but the load-use pair is the documented exception.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following reduce the penalty paid when a branch is taken?", "options": ["Resolving the branch in ID rather than in EX.", "Filling the delay slots with instructions that are always useful.", "Adding pipeline stages before the branch is resolved.", "A branch target buffer that supplies the target during fetch."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The penalty is the number of cycles between fetching the branch and knowing its outcome and target. Anything that shortens that gap, or that keeps useful work in it, reduces the penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: moving resolution from EX to ID removes one wrong instruction from the pipeline.</li><li>(B) True: a filled delay slot does useful work instead of being squashed, so the branch effectively costs nothing.</li><li>(C) False: more stages between IF and resolution means more wrong instructions fetched.</li><li>(D) True: predicting at fetch with a target removes the wrong fetch altogether when the prediction is right.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A deeper pipeline is often assumed to be faster; for control hazards it is worse, because the penalty equals the distance from fetch to resolution.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about branch prediction are TRUE?", "options": ["A static always-not-taken predictor pays a penalty only on taken branches.", "A 2-bit saturating-counter predictor changes its prediction only after two consecutive outcomes of the other kind.", "The misprediction penalty is the number of cycles between fetching the branch and resolving it.", "A predictor removes the need for a branch target buffer."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Prediction decides which instructions to fetch; the penalty is paid only when the prediction is wrong, and it is set by how long the pipeline takes to find out.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: predicting not-taken is right on fall-through, so only taken branches flush.</li><li>(B) True: the four states are strongly/weakly taken and strongly/weakly not-taken, and two consecutive opposite outcomes are needed to flip the direction.</li><li>(C) True: that distance is exactly the number of wrongly fetched instructions.</li><li>(D) False: a prediction still has to say where to fetch from, which is the BTB's job.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option D confuses direction prediction with target prediction; a direction-only predictor still needs the target from a BTB or from the decode of the branch.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline has a clock period of 1.5 ns. Once the pipeline is full and running without hazards, how many instructions complete in 30 ns?", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In steady state one instruction completes per clock cycle, so the count is the number of cycles in the given time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cycles in 30 ns = 30 / 1.5 = 20.</li><li>In the steady state each cycle completes exactly one instruction.</li><li>So 20 instructions complete in that window.</li><li>The fill and drain are excluded by the words \"once the pipeline is full\".</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20 instructions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing 30 by the latency of one instruction (5 × 1.5 = 7.5 ns) gives 4, which is the unpipelined rate, not the pipelined one.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline with separate instruction and data memories executes 200 instructions, 25% of which are loads or stores. Each load or store occupies the data memory for one clock cycle. For how many cycles is the data memory busy?", "options": [], "answer_display": "50", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Only loads and stores reach the data memory; instruction fetches go to the instruction memory. Busy cycles = number of memory instructions × cycles each.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Memory instructions = 200 × 0.25 = 50.</li><li>Each occupies the data memory for one cycle.</li><li>Busy cycles = 50 × 1 = 50.</li><li>The other 150 instructions use only the instruction memory, so they add nothing here.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 50 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 200 counts every instruction, including the 150 that never touch the data memory.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A five-stage pipeline has full forwarding, including the load-use path. How many stall cycles does the sequence lw r1,0(r10); add r2,r1,r3; sub r4,r5,r6; lw r7,4(r10); or r8,r7,r9; and r11,r2,r4; lw r12,8(r10); add r13,r12,r12 need?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With forwarding, only a load whose value is needed by the very next instruction forces a stall, because the loaded value appears at the end of MEM but is needed at the start of EX.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>lw r1 → add r2, r1, r3: adjacent load-use pair, 1 stall.</li><li>sub r4, r5, r6 depends on nothing pending: 0 stalls.</li><li>lw r7 → or r8, r7, r9: adjacent load-use pair, 1 stall.</li><li>and r11, r2, r4 reads r2 (three instructions back) and r4 (three instructions back): both forwarded, 0 stalls.</li><li>lw r12 → add r13, r12, r12: adjacent load-use pair, 1 stall.</li><li>Total = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 stall cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 stall cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging a stall to every dependence gives 4 or more; only the three load-use adjacencies cost anything once forwarding is in place.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A branch target buffer predicts 90% of branches correctly. 15% of all instructions are branches, and a misprediction costs 2 cycles. The effective CPI is ______ (give 2 decimal places).", "options": [], "answer_display": "1.03", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The added CPI is branch frequency × misprediction rate × penalty. A predictor changes the rate, not the penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Mispredictions per instruction = 0.15 × 0.10 = 0.015.</li><li>Extra cycles per instruction = 0.015 × 2 = 0.03.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.03 = 1.03.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.03.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the hit rate instead of the miss rate (0.15 × 0.90 × 2 = 0.27) gives 1.27; the penalty is paid only on the 10% that are wrong.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A program of 500 instructions runs on a five-stage pipeline. 40% are loads and 10% are stores, and the single unified memory costs one extra cycle for every load or store. Half of the loads are immediately followed by an instruction that uses the loaded value, and each such pair costs one stall. 20% of the instructions are conditional branches, half of which are taken, and each taken branch costs 3 cycles. Including fill and drain, how many clock cycles does the program take?", "options": [], "answer_display": "1004", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Total cycles = ideal cycles + fill and drain + every stall category, each weighted by how often it occurs. The categories are additive when they are independent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Ideal cycles = 500.</li><li>Fill and drain = k − 1 = 4.</li><li>Memory structural stalls = (0.40 + 0.10) × 500 = 250.</li><li>Load-use stalls = 0.40 × 500 × 0.5 = 100.</li><li>Branch penalty = 0.20 × 500 × 0.5 × 3 = 150.</li><li>Total = 500 + 4 + 250 + 100 + 150 = 1004 cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1004 clock cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the load-use stalls to the load count, or forgetting the 4 fill/drain cycles, gives 1000 or 1008; each category must be counted once, with its own weight.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A processor uses programmed I/O with polling to read one byte at a time from a keyboard. How does it decide that a byte is available?", "options": ["It reads the status register of the keyboard interface repeatedly until the ready bit is set, and then reads the data register", "The keyboard raises an interrupt and the processor branches to a service routine", "The DMA controller signals the processor once for each block of bytes", "The keyboard executes a trap instruction that switches the processor into kernel mode"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Programmed I/O keeps the transfer under direct program control: the processor itself moves each word between the device interface and memory. Because the device is much slower, the program must ask repeatedly whether the device is ready.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The device interface exposes a status register whose ready (or busy) bit is set by the device when it holds a new byte.</li><li>The program reads that status register in a loop. While the bit is clear, the loop simply repeats, so the processor is busy-waiting.</li><li>When the bit is set, the program reads the data register into a processor register and stores it in memory.</li><li>No interrupt, no DMA controller and no privileged trap is involved, which is exactly what distinguishes polling from the other two mechanisms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the status register is polled until the ready bit is set, then the data register is read (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Polling is often confused with interrupt-driven I/O because both check a status bit. In polling the processor initiates every check; in interrupt-driven I/O the device initiates the transfer by interrupting the processor.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "What is the main advantage of interrupt-driven I/O over programmed I/O with polling?", "options": ["It removes the need for a device driver in the operating system", "The processor can execute other work while the device is busy and is disturbed only when the device has data or has finished", "It transfers a whole block without any processor involvement per word", "It guarantees a higher transfer rate from the device"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Polling spends processor cycles asking a question whose answer is usually \"not yet\". An interrupt inverts the control: the device tells the processor when attention is needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With polling, the processor is trapped in a loop for the whole of the device's service time, so its cycles are wasted.</li><li>With interrupts, the processor runs other instructions and the interface raises an interrupt request when the device becomes ready.</li><li>The processor finishes the current instruction, saves its state, and runs the service routine; the transfer itself is still done by the processor, word by word.</li><li>Hence the gain is in processor utilisation, not in device speed and not in the number of transfers per word.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the processor does useful work until the device signals that it is ready (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C describes DMA, and option D confuses a scheduling gain with a bandwidth gain: the device is the same, only the waiting is removed.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A device must be serviced once every 8 µs. Polling its status register costs the processor 100 ns, and the processor has no other work, so it polls continuously. What percentage of processor time is spent polling? (Give your answer to two decimal places.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1.25", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In polling the processor spends a fixed slice of time per poll and repeats the poll at least once every service interval, so the wasted fraction is the cost of one poll divided by the interval between services.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Service interval = <code class=\"mm\">8 &micro;s</code> = <code class=\"mm\">8000 ns</code>.</li><li>Cost of one poll = <code class=\"mm\">100 ns</code>, and the processor polls as often as it can, so it spends <code class=\"mm\">100 ns</code> out of every <code class=\"mm\">8000 ns</code>.</li><li>Fraction = <code class=\"mm\">100 / 8000 = 0.0125</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">1.25%</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.25%.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing by the poll cost instead of the service interval, or forgetting that the poll repeats forever, gives values such as 80% or 8000%; the useful ratio is always (work per poll) / (interval between services).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following statements about interrupts are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A vectored interrupt supplies the starting address of the service routine, so the processor does not have to poll the devices to discover which one interrupted", "A maskable interrupt can be disabled by the processor, and a request raised while it is disabled stays pending until interrupts are enabled again", "An interrupt request raised while interrupts are disabled is always lost", "Interrupt latency includes the time needed to save the program counter and the processor state"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An interrupt is an asynchronous request that diverts the processor to a service routine. The interface carries the identification of the source (vector), a mask bit that lets software defer the request, and a saved context that lets the interrupted program resume.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: in vectored interrupting the device (or the interrupt controller) places a vector, that is the address of the handler, on the bus, so no software polling of devices is needed.</li><li>(B) True: masking gates the request at the interface or in the controller; the pending bit remains set, so the request is serviced when the mask is cleared.</li><li>(C) False: the request is normally latched as pending. It is lost only if the interface does not latch it, which is a design defect rather than the rule.</li><li>(D) True: latency runs from the raising of the request to the first useful instruction of the handler, and the context save is part of it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C is the usual trap: \"disabled\" is read as \"dropped\". Masking delays an interrupt; it does not destroy it, and the handler still runs later.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which statement correctly distinguishes a trap from an interrupt?", "options": ["A trap is raised by an external device, whereas an interrupt is raised by the instruction being executed", "A trap is generated inside the processor by the instruction just executed (for example a system call or a divide-by-zero), whereas an interrupt is raised asynchronously by an external device", "Both are asynchronous, and the only difference between them is the priority level assigned to them", "A trap can never transfer control to an operating-system routine"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Exceptions are classified by their origin. A trap or exception is synchronous: it is caused by the instruction the processor is executing. An interrupt is asynchronous: it is caused by an event outside the instruction stream.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Executing <code class=\"mm\">DIV</code> with a zero divisor raises an exception at a deterministic point in the program, so re-running the same instruction raises it again. That is a trap.</li><li>A system call deliberately executes a trap-type instruction to enter the kernel; again the cause is the instruction itself.</li><li>A disk completion or a timer tick arrives at a moment unrelated to the instruction stream, so it is an interrupt.</li><li>Both normally transfer control to a kernel routine, which is why option D is wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a trap is synchronous and instruction-caused; an interrupt is asynchronous and device-caused (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A simply reverses the two terms. Remembering \"trap = my own instruction did it\" prevents the reversal.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A disk interface produces one 512-byte block every 20 µs. Servicing the completion interrupt costs the processor 2 µs, and that servicing overlaps the transfer of the next block. What percentage of processor time is spent servicing these interrupts?", "options": ["2%", "5%", "10%", "20%"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Interrupt-driven I/O overlaps the transfer with computation, so the processor cost per unit time is the service cost per interrupt divided by the interval at which interrupts arrive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The device delivers one block every <code class=\"mm\">20 &micro;s</code>, so one interrupt arrives every <code class=\"mm\">20 &micro;s</code>.</li><li>Each interrupt costs <code class=\"mm\">2 &micro;s</code> of processor time.</li><li>Fraction = <code class=\"mm\">2 / 20 = 0.10</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">10%</code>.</li><li>The remaining 90% of processor time is available for other work, which is the whole point of interrupting instead of polling.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10% of processor time (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the service cost to the transfer time first and then dividing gives 9.1%, and multiplying the two numbers gives 40 &micro;s; the correct denominator is the interrupt arrival interval.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "During a DMA transfer of a block between a device and main memory, the processor", "options": ["copies every word from the device data register to memory in a software loop", "programs the DMA controller with the memory address, the word count and the direction, and is interrupted once when the block is complete", "polls the device status register once for every word transferred", "is halted for the entire transfer and cannot execute any instruction"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DMA controller is a bus master that moves data between memory and a device without the processor touching each word. The processor is involved only at the two ends of the transfer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Setup: the processor writes the memory starting address, the number of words (or bytes), the direction and the device identity into the controller's registers.</li><li>The controller requests the bus, and each granted cycle moves one word directly between memory and the device.</li><li>When the count reaches zero the controller raises a single interrupt, and the processor resumes responsibility for the buffer.</li><li>Options A and C describe programmed I/O; option D overstates the cost, because the processor keeps executing even while the controller steals cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the processor programs the controller and is interrupted once at the end (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> \"No processor involvement\" is read as \"the processor stops\". In cycle stealing the processor runs throughout, losing only the cycles the controller takes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following statements about DMA transfer modes are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In burst (block) mode the controller holds the bus for the whole block, so the processor cannot use memory until the block is finished", "In cycle-stealing mode the controller requests the bus for one word at a time, so the processor loses one cycle per word but is never locked out for long", "Cycle stealing always completes a block faster than burst mode", "In transparent (hidden) DMA the controller uses only those memory cycles in which the processor does not need the bus"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The modes differ in how long the controller keeps bus ownership, and therefore in how the transfer time is traded against processor slowdown.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: in burst mode the controller is master until the block ends, so processor memory accesses wait for the whole block.</li><li>(B) True: in cycle stealing the controller takes one cycle per word, interleaving with the processor, so the processor is slowed but never blocked for long.</li><li>(C) False: burst mode finishes sooner because it pays the bus-acquisition overhead once, not once per word; cycle stealing is slower but gentler on the processor.</li><li>(D) True: transparent DMA exploits cycles in which the processor does not drive the bus, so the processor sees almost no slowdown.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C inverts the trade-off: cycle stealing exists to protect the processor, not to win on transfer time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A DMA controller working in cycle-stealing mode moves 4 bytes per stolen memory cycle. How many processor cycles does it steal in order to transfer 64 KB? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "16384", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In cycle stealing each transferred word costs the processor exactly one memory cycle, so the number of stolen cycles is the number of words moved.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bytes to move = <code class=\"mm\">64 KB = 64 &times; 1024 = 65536 bytes</code>.</li><li>Words = <code class=\"mm\">65536 / 4 = 16384</code> words, because each cycle moves 4 bytes.</li><li>Each word costs one stolen cycle, so <code class=\"mm\">16384</code> processor cycles are stolen.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16384 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">64 &times; 1000</code> bytes, or counting bytes instead of words, gives 64000 or 65536; the word size must divide the byte count exactly once.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Before a DMA block transfer can begin, the processor must supply the controller with", "options": ["only the device number, because the controller derives everything else from the device", "the memory starting address, the number of words to transfer and the direction of the transfer", "the contents of the block, which it copies into the controller's internal buffer", "the interrupt vector of the device service routine and nothing else"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The DMA controller is a small processor of its own: it needs to know where in memory to start, how much to move, in which direction, and which device to talk to.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The address register is loaded with the first memory address of the buffer, and is incremented as the transfer proceeds.</li><li>The word-count register is loaded with the number of words, and is decremented; when it reaches zero the controller raises its completion interrupt.</li><li>A control field selects read (device to memory) or write (memory to device) and the device address.</li><li>The data itself is never staged in the controller's registers in bulk; it flows word by word on the bus, so option C is wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: memory address, word count and direction (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The interrupt vector is a consequence of the transfer, not an input to it; the processor only needs to know which routine to run when the controller finishes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A 1 MB file is read from a disk in 512-byte blocks. With interrupt-driven I/O each block costs 2 µs of processor time for its completion interrupt. With DMA the processor spends 20 µs programming the controller and is interrupted once when the whole file has arrived. Roughly how many times less processor time does the DMA transfer cost?", "options": ["about 20 times", "about 50 times", "about 200 times", "about 2048 times"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Interrupt-driven I/O pays the interrupt overhead once per block, whereas DMA pays a fixed setup cost once per transfer, so the ratio of costs grows with the number of blocks.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Blocks = <code class=\"mm\">1 MB / 512 B = 1048576 / 512 = 2048</code>.</li><li>Interrupt-driven cost = <code class=\"mm\">2048 &times; 2 &micro;s = 4096 &micro;s</code>.</li><li>DMA cost = <code class=\"mm\">20 &micro;s</code> of setup plus one interrupt, so about <code class=\"mm\">20 &micro;s</code>.</li><li>Ratio = <code class=\"mm\">4096 / 20 = 204.8</code>, that is about <code class=\"mm\">200</code> times.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: about 200 times less processor time (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option D counts the blocks but forgets that the DMA cost is a single setup; option A mistakes the per-block saving (2 &micro;s against 20 &micro;s) for the ratio of the totals.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A DMA controller transfers at 100 MB/s using 4-byte words in cycle-stealing mode. The processor runs at 100 MHz and needs one memory cycle for every processor cycle. While the transfer is in progress, what percentage of processor cycles does the controller steal?", "options": ["12.5%", "25%", "50%", "100%"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each stolen cycle is one memory cycle, so the stolen fraction is the controller's word rate divided by the processor's cycle rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Controller word rate = <code class=\"mm\">100 MB/s / 4 B = 25 &times; 10<sup>6</sup></code> words per second.</li><li>Processor cycle rate = <code class=\"mm\">100 MHz = 100 &times; 10<sup>6</sup></code> cycles per second.</li><li>One word per cycle, so stolen cycles per second = <code class=\"mm\">25 &times; 10<sup>6</sup></code>.</li><li>Fraction = <code class=\"mm\">25 / 100 = 0.25</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">25%</code> of processor cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 25% of processor cycles (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing the byte rate by the cycle rate (100 MB/s against 100 MHz) gives 100%, which silently assumes one byte per cycle; the word size must be carried through the calculation.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A disk rotates at 7200 rpm. Its average rotational latency is about", "options": ["2.08 ms", "4.17 ms", "8.33 ms", "16.7 ms"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rotational latency is the time spent waiting for the required sector to arrive under the head. On average the sector is half a revolution away.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Revolutions per second = <code class=\"mm\">7200 / 60 = 120</code>.</li><li>One revolution = <code class=\"mm\">1 / 120 s = 8.33 ms</code>.</li><li>Average latency = half a revolution = <code class=\"mm\">8.33 / 2 = 4.17 ms</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: about 4.17 ms (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 8.33 ms is the worst case (a full revolution), not the average; using the full revolution doubles every disk access-time estimate.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A disk has an average seek time of 8 ms, rotates at 7200 rpm and transfers data at 100 MB/s. What is the average time to read one 4 KB sector, in milliseconds? (Give your answer to two decimal places.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "12.21", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A random read pays three costs in sequence: seek (moving the head to the cylinder), rotational latency (waiting for the sector) and transfer (streaming the bytes).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Seek = <code class=\"mm\">8 ms</code>.</li><li>Rotation = <code class=\"mm\">60 / 7200 s = 8.333 ms</code>, so average rotational latency = <code class=\"mm\">4.167 ms</code>.</li><li>Transfer = <code class=\"mm\">4096 B / 100 MB/s = 40.96 &micro;s = 0.041 ms</code>.</li><li>Total = <code class=\"mm\">8 + 4.167 + 0.041 = 12.208 ms</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">12.21 ms</code> to two decimal places.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12.21 ms (accept 12.21; the exact value is 12.208 ms).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using a full revolution instead of half gives 16.37 ms, and forgetting the transfer time gives 12.17 ms. For a 4 KB sector the transfer is tiny, but it must still be added.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "On a multi-platter disk, a cylinder is", "options": ["one complete track on the topmost surface", "the set of tracks at the same radius on all surfaces, which can be read without any further seek", "the region swept by one head between two consecutive index marks", "the number of sectors that fit in a single track"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A disk has several recording surfaces on a common spindle, each with its own head on a common arm. The tracks that lie at one radius on all surfaces form a cylinder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Because all heads move together, once the arm is positioned on a cylinder every surface's track at that radius is reachable by electronic head selection.</li><li>So a cylinder can be filled or read with a single seek, which is why file systems try to place related blocks in the same cylinder.</li><li>Option A describes a single track; option C describes one track between index marks; option D is the sectors-per-track count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the same-radius tracks on all surfaces, reachable with one seek (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> \"Cylinder\" is assumed to mean the physical platter stack. It is a logical grouping of tracks at one radius, and that is what makes it useful for placement.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following statements about disk access time are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Seek time dominates the access time of a small random read on a disk whose average seek is several milliseconds", "Transfer time is proportional to the number of sectors transferred and inversely proportional to the transfer rate", "Rotational latency is independent of the rotation speed", "Doubling the rotation speed halves the average rotational latency"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Access time = seek + rotational latency + transfer. Each term scales with a different physical quantity, and their relative weight decides which optimisation matters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: for a single sector the transfer is tens of microseconds while the seek is milliseconds, so the seek dominates.</li><li>(B) True: transfer time = (sectors &times; bytes per sector) / transfer rate, so it grows with the count and falls as the rate rises.</li><li>(C) False: rotational latency is half a revolution, which is inversely proportional to the rotation speed.</li><li>(D) True: latency = <code class=\"mm\">30 / rpm</code> seconds, so doubling the rpm halves it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C is tempting because latency is a constant for a given disk, but that constant is set by the speed; changing the speed changes the latency.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A disk has 300 sectors of 512 bytes per track and rotates at 10 000 rpm. What is the sustained transfer rate of one head?", "options": ["12.8 MB/s", "25.6 MB/s", "51.2 MB/s", "102.4 MB/s"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> While the head stays on one track, the bytes stream past it at (bytes per track) &times; (revolutions per second). That product is the sustained transfer rate of a single head.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bytes per track = <code class=\"mm\">300 &times; 512 = 153600 B</code>.</li><li>Revolutions per second = <code class=\"mm\">10000 / 60 = 166.67</code>.</li><li>Rate = <code class=\"mm\">153600 &times; 166.67 &asymp; 25.6 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> B/s</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">25.6 MB/s</code>.</li><li>Multiply by the number of surfaces only if the question asks for the rate of the whole drive with parallel heads.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 25.6 MB/s (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C doubles the answer by using 600 sectors or by counting two surfaces, and option A halves it by using 5000 rpm; the rate belongs to one head on one track.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A disk rotates at 6000 rpm. One complete revolution takes", "options": ["5 ms", "10 ms", "16.7 ms", "20 ms"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rotation speed in revolutions per minute converts to a period by inverting: period = 60 / rpm seconds.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Revolutions per second = <code class=\"mm\">6000 / 60 = 100</code>.</li><li>Period = <code class=\"mm\">1 / 100 s = 0.01 s = 10 ms</code>.</li><li>Average rotational latency is half of that, <code class=\"mm\">5 ms</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 ms for a full revolution (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A is the average latency, not the revolution time; the question asks for one full turn.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following statements about disk recording and organisation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In zoned-bit recording the number of sectors per track increases towards the outer edge of the platter", "With constant angular velocity every track holds exactly the same number of sectors, even when zoned recording is used", "A cylinder can be read without any further seek once its first track is reached, because all its tracks lie at the same radius", "Sector interleaving spaces logically consecutive sectors apart so that the controller can keep up with the rotation"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A disk keeps constant angular velocity, so an outer track passes the head faster in linear terms; recording more sectors there uses that extra length. Interleaving and cylinder grouping are layout tricks that hide timing effects.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: zoned recording groups tracks into bands and raises the sector count per track from the inner to the outer band.</li><li>(B) False: that is precisely what zoned recording abandons; a uniform sector count per track wastes the outer bands.</li><li>(C) True: all heads sit on one arm, so switching surfaces inside a cylinder needs no mechanical movement.</li><li>(D) True: interleaving gives the controller time to move a sector before the next logical one arrives, so it raises effective throughput.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option B is the common slip: zoned recording is often described as \"constant angular velocity with varying sectors\", and the \"varying\" part is exactly what makes B false.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which RAID level stripes data in blocks across all the disks and keeps parity blocks distributed over all the disks, so that a single disk failure can be survived?", "options": ["RAID 0", "RAID 1", "RAID 4", "RAID 5"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RAID levels differ in two decisions: how data is spread over disks (striping) and how redundancy is provided (mirroring or parity).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>RAID 0 stripes but keeps no redundancy, so any failure loses data.</li><li>RAID 1 mirrors, so capacity is halved and there is no parity.</li><li>RAID 4 keeps block striping with a single dedicated parity disk.</li><li>RAID 5 keeps the parity but distributes it block by block over all disks, which removes RAID 4's parity-disk bottleneck and still survives one failure.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: RAID 5 (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> RAID 4 and RAID 5 both use parity, so the distinguishing word in the question is \"distributed\"; a dedicated parity disk is RAID 4.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A RAID 6 array is built from 8 disks of 2 TB each. What is its usable capacity in TB? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RAID 6 stores two independent parity blocks per stripe, so two disks' worth of capacity are spent on redundancy whatever the array size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Raw capacity = <code class=\"mm\">8 &times; 2 TB = 16 TB</code>.</li><li>Redundancy = 2 disks = <code class=\"mm\">4 TB</code>.</li><li>Usable = <code class=\"mm\">16 &minus; 4 = 12 TB</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">(n &minus; 2) / n = 6 / 8 = 75%</code> of the raw capacity.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12 TB.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the RAID 5 rule and subtracting one disk gives 14 TB; RAID 6 always costs two.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following statements about RAID levels are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["RAID 0 gives no redundancy, so one failed disk loses the whole array", "RAID 1 halves the usable capacity, because every block is stored twice", "RAID 5 survives two simultaneous disk failures", "RAID 10 combines mirroring with striping"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each level is a trade-off between capacity, performance and the number of failures it can survive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: RAID 0 has striping only, so the failure of one disk destroys the stripe and therefore the array.</li><li>(B) True: mirroring writes each block twice, so half the raw capacity is usable.</li><li>(C) False: RAID 5 holds one parity block per stripe and can reconstruct from a single loss; a second loss before rebuild destroys the stripe. Two-failure tolerance is RAID 6.</li><li>(D) True: RAID 10 mirrors within pairs and stripes across the pairs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C is the standard confusion between RAID 5 (one parity, one failure) and RAID 6 (two parities, two failures).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "The main advantage of RAID 5 over RAID 4 is that", "options": ["it needs no parity at all", "the parity blocks are distributed across all disks, so the dedicated parity disk of RAID 4 stops being a write bottleneck", "it can survive two disk failures instead of one", "it mirrors every block instead of striping it"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both RAID 4 and RAID 5 use block striping with one parity block per stripe. The difference is where the parity block of each stripe lives.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In RAID 4 every stripe writes its parity to the same disk, so every write touches that disk and it becomes a serialisation point.</li><li>In RAID 5 the parity block rotates from disk to disk across stripes, so writes to different stripes hit different disks and proceed in parallel.</li><li>Both tolerate exactly one failure and both pay the same capacity cost of one disk.</li><li>So the gain is write concurrency, not redundancy or capacity.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: distributed parity removes RAID 4's parity-disk write bottleneck (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option C is attributed to RAID 5 by candidates who remember \"RAID 5 is more reliable\"; the extra failure tolerance is RAID 6's property.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A RAID 5 array is built from 5 disks, each with a mean time to failure of 100 000 hours. Treating the failures as independent, what is the approximate mean time to the first disk failure in the array, in hours? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "20000", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> If each of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> disks fails independently at rate <code class=\"mm\">1 / MTTF</code>, the array fails at the sum of the rates, so the array MTTF is <code class=\"mm\">MTTF / n</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Per-disk failure rate = <code class=\"mm\">1 / 100000</code> failures per hour.</li><li>Array failure rate = <code class=\"mm\">5 / 100000</code> failures per hour.</li><li>Mean time to the first failure = the reciprocal = <code class=\"mm\">100000 / 5 = 20000</code> hours.</li><li>With 5 disks the array fails five times sooner than one disk, which is why large arrays rely on parity and scrubbing rather than on disk reliability alone.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20000 hours.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by 5 gives 500 000 hours, which would describe a series system that survives only while every disk survives; the first failure is the event of interest here.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following arrays keeps exactly 75% of its raw capacity usable?", "options": ["RAID 5 built from 4 disks", "RAID 6 built from 5 disks", "RAID 1 built from 4 disks", "RAID 5 built from 6 disks"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The usable fraction follows from the redundancy scheme: RAID 5 keeps <code class=\"mm\">(n &minus; 1) / n</code>, RAID 6 keeps <code class=\"mm\">(n &minus; 2) / n</code>, and RAID 1 keeps <code class=\"mm\">1 / 2</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>RAID 5 with 4 disks: <code class=\"mm\">(4 &minus; 1) / 4 = 0.75</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">75%</code>.</li><li>RAID 6 with 5 disks: <code class=\"mm\">(5 &minus; 2) / 5 = 0.60</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">60%</code>.</li><li>RAID 1 with 4 disks: mirroring keeps <code class=\"mm\">50%</code> whatever the disk count.</li><li>RAID 5 with 6 disks: <code class=\"mm\">(6 &minus; 1) / 6 &asymp; 0.833</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">83.3%</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: RAID 5 built from 4 disks (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> \"RAID 5 gives 75%\" is remembered as a fixed number, but the fraction rises with the disk count; only the 4-disk case is exactly 75%.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Four devices share one interrupt request line using a daisy chain, in which the processor's acknowledge signal passes from device to device. If two devices raise the request line at the same instant, which one is serviced?", "options": ["The device electrically closest to the processor in the chain", "The device that has been waiting longest", "The device electrically farthest from the processor in the chain", "Neither; the processor rejects both requests as ambiguous"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A daisy chain is a hardware priority scheme. The devices share one request line, so the processor cannot tell them apart; the acknowledge is passed serially along the chain instead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Any device needing service asserts the common request line, so the processor learns only that <em>some</em> device wants attention.</li><li>The processor asserts the acknowledge.</li><li>The acknowledge propagates along the chain; the first requesting device in that physical order blocks further propagation and places its vector on the data bus.</li><li>Devices farther along the chain never receive the acknowledge for this request, so they keep their request asserted and wait.</li><li>Priority is therefore fixed by wiring position: closest to the processor means highest priority.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the device closest to the processor in the chain (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the chain as a queue, so that arrival order or longest waiting decides. Daisy chaining gives <em>static</em> priority by wiring; fairness needs a rotating-priority scheme or a software queue.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A device handler is executing when a second interrupt of higher priority arrives and interrupts are currently enabled. What happens?", "options": ["The second request is discarded because a handler is already running", "The processor completes the current handler and only then services the second request", "The processor suspends the current handler, saves its context, services the higher-priority request, and then resumes the interrupted handler", "The two handlers execute concurrently on the same core"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Interrupts may nest. A higher-priority request that arrives with interrupts enabled pre-empts the handler in progress, which is resumed later.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The processor saves the program counter and status word of the running handler.</li><li>It vectors to the higher-priority service routine, which runs to completion (unless it too is pre-empted).</li><li>On return it restores the saved context, so the lower-priority handler continues from exactly where it was suspended.</li><li>Only if interrupts were disabled inside the handler would the request stay pending until they are enabled again.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the higher-priority request pre-empts the running handler, which resumes afterwards (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the second request is lost or that the two handlers run in parallel. A single core executes one at a time; nesting is suspension and resumption, and priority decides only who may interrupt whom.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A device must be serviced once every <code class=\"mm\">25 &micro;s</code>. Its interrupt handler takes <code class=\"mm\">6 &micro;s</code> and the context save and restore around it costs another <code class=\"mm\">2 &micro;s</code>. What percentage of the processor's time remains available for other work?", "options": ["24%", "32%", "68%", "76%"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The processor cost of one interrupt is the handler plus the context save and restore. Its share of the processor is that cost divided by the interval between interrupts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost per service = 6 + 2 = 8 &micro;s.</li><li>Interval between services = 25 &micro;s.</li><li>Fraction consumed = 8 / 25 = 0.32, that is 32%.</li><li>Remaining = 100 &minus; 32 = 68%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 68% of the processor remains available (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Leaving out the context switch: 6 / 25 = 24%, which suggests 76% is free (option D). The save and restore are part of the interrupt cost, not free overhead.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In a daisy chain of four devices the acknowledge signal must pass through all four devices before it reaches the last one, and each device adds <code class=\"mm\">150 ns</code> of delay. The processor's own entry overhead is <code class=\"mm\">2 &micro;s</code>. What is the interrupt latency seen by the last device in the chain, in microseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2.6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a daisy chain the acknowledge is delayed by every device it traverses, so the latency of a device grows with its distance from the processor.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Processor entry overhead = 2 &micro;s.</li><li>Delay through four devices = 4 &times; 0.15 &micro;s = 0.6 &micro;s.</li><li>Latency for the last device = 2 + 0.6 = 2.6 &micro;s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.6 microseconds.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging only the three devices ahead of it, or adding the propagation delay to the handler time. The question asks for entry latency, and the acknowledge must traverse all four devices to reach the last one.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about programmed I/O and interrupt-driven I/O are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In programmed I/O the processor itself moves every word between the device and memory", "Interrupt-driven I/O still moves each word under program control, but the program is entered only when the device is ready", "Interrupt-driven I/O lets a device write directly into memory without a processor instruction per word", "The processor takes an interrupt only at an instruction boundary, never in the middle of an instruction"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Programmed I/O and interrupt-driven I/O are both <em>program-controlled</em>: the instruction sequence that moves a word is the same. They differ only in when that sequence is entered.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A is the definition of programmed I/O — the processor executes the load and store for each word.</li><li>B is the definition of interrupt-driven I/O: the same per-word instructions, entered when the device signals instead of when a polling loop happens to look.</li><li>C describes DMA, where the controller drives the bus and the processor issues no per-word instruction at all.</li><li>D holds because an interrupt is sampled between instructions: the instruction in progress is completed (or its state is preserved) before control transfers.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Equating interrupt-driven I/O with DMA. An interrupt removes the <em>waiting</em>, not the per-word work; removing the per-word work is what DMA does.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "programmed-io-interrupts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A device produces one byte every <code class=\"mm\">5 &micro;s</code>. Servicing each byte by interrupt costs <code class=\"mm\">4 &micro;s</code> of processor time, including the handler and the context switch. A program needs 40% of the processor for its own computation. Which statement is correct?", "options": ["Interrupt-driven I/O leaves the program 60% of the processor, so it is sufficient", "Interrupt-driven I/O consumes 80% of the processor, so the program's 40% cannot be met and a block-transfer mechanism such as DMA is needed", "Interrupt-driven I/O consumes 20% of the processor, so it is sufficient", "The fraction of the processor consumed does not depend on how often the device interrupts"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The interrupt load is (cost per interrupt) / (interval between interrupts). If that load exceeds the complement of what the program needs, the arrangement cannot work.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Interrupts per second = 1 / (5 &micro;s) = 200 000.</li><li>Processor time per second = 200 000 &times; 4 &micro;s = 800 000 &micro;s = 0.8 s.</li><li>Fraction consumed = 80%, leaving 20%.</li><li>The program needs 40%, so 20% is not enough.</li><li>A block transfer (DMA) removes the per-byte interrupt: the device interrupts once per block instead of once per byte.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the interrupt scheme consumes 80% and fails; DMA is needed (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the numbers as &ldquo;4 out of every 5 microseconds is spent, so 1 is left&rdquo; and calling that 60%. The leftover is 1 &micro;s in every 5, which is 20%.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following is NOT a register that a DMA controller needs in order to move a block?", "options": ["A memory address register", "A word count register", "The program counter of the running process", "A control and status register holding the direction of transfer and the mode"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DMA controller is a small bus master with its own register set describing the transfer. The processor's program counter is not part of it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The address register holds where in memory the next word is read or written.</li><li>The word count register holds how many words remain; it is decremented as the block moves.</li><li>The control and status register holds the direction (read or write), the mode (burst, cycle stealing, transparent) and the done flag.</li><li>The program counter sequences the processor's own instruction stream and has nothing to do with a transfer the controller runs by itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the program counter of the running process is not a DMA register (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the controller must know which instruction is executing. It does not: it is an independent bus master, programmed once and then left to run.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "How does a DMA controller obtain the memory bus when it is ready to move a word?", "options": ["It asserts a bus-request line and waits for the processor to return a bus-grant, because both are masters of the same bus", "It writes to a special memory location that the processor polls", "It raises an interrupt, and the handler copies the word", "It drives the address bus alone, so no arbitration is needed"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DMA controller and the processor are two bus masters sharing one bus, so access must be arbitrated in hardware.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The controller asserts the bus-request (hold) line.</li><li>The processor finishes the bus cycle in progress, releases the bus and asserts bus-grant (hold-acknowledge).</li><li>The controller drives address and data, completes the cycle, then releases the bus.</li><li>An interrupt-based handover would cost far more than the single memory cycle the transfer needs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the bus-request / bus-grant handshake (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the controller can drive the bus whenever it likes. Without arbitration its cycle would collide with a processor memory cycle and both would be corrupted.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In cycle-stealing DMA the controller takes one memory cycle per word and returns the bus immediately. Why is the bus released after every word rather than held for the whole block?", "options": ["Because the controller cannot generate consecutive addresses", "Because a memory cycle is longer than a processor cycle, so holding the bus would corrupt data", "Because the controller must be reprogrammed before every word", "So that the processor can still execute instructions between the stolen cycles, which keeps its slowdown proportional to the fraction of cycles stolen rather than to the whole block"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cycle stealing and burst mode differ in how long the controller keeps the bus, and therefore in how much the processor is slowed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In cycle stealing the controller requests the bus, moves one word, and releases it.</li><li>The processor executes between stolen cycles, so it is slowed by the fraction of cycles taken rather than being stopped.</li><li>Holding the bus for the whole block — burst mode — would lock the processor out for the entire transfer.</li><li>The trade-off is speed for the block against interference with the processor; the controller generates consecutive addresses internally either way.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: releasing the bus lets the processor interleave its own work, so slowdown is proportional to cycles stolen (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the release protects data integrity or is forced by addressing. Neither is true: burst mode holds the bus for a whole block without corrupting anything.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about DMA are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A DMA write into memory can leave a write-back cache holding a stale copy, so the cache must be flushed or invalidated", "A DMA controller becomes a bus master and competes with the processor for memory cycles", "Scatter-gather DMA uses a chain of descriptors, so several non-contiguous blocks are moved with one setup", "Once DMA is used, no interrupt is needed for the transfer"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> DMA adds a second bus master that moves data without the processor, which has consequences for caches, for memory bandwidth and for how completion is reported.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A is the cache-coherence problem: a write-back cache may still hold a line the controller has just overwritten, so the affected lines are invalidated (or the cache flushed) around the transfer.</li><li>B follows from arbitration: memory cycles used by the controller are cycles the processor cannot use.</li><li>C is scatter-gather: a descriptor chain lets one programming step move several blocks that are not contiguous.</li><li>D is false. The controller signals completion, normally with an interrupt, and the processor must still learn that the block has arrived.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing DMA is completely interrupt-free. It reduces the interrupt rate from one per word to one per block; the completion interrupt remains.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Interrupt-driven I/O costs <code class=\"mm\">5 &micro;s</code> of processor time per byte transferred. DMA costs a one-time setup of <code class=\"mm\">100 &micro;s</code> and then <code class=\"mm\">1 &micro;s</code> of processor time per byte for the memory cycles it steals. At what transfer size do the two schemes cost the same total processor time?", "options": ["20 bytes", "25 bytes", "100 bytes", "125 bytes"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Total processor time is a per-byte cost for interrupt-driven I/O, and a fixed setup plus a smaller per-byte cost for DMA. The two are equal where the lines cross.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Interrupt-driven cost for <code class=\"mm\">n</code> bytes = 5n &micro;s.</li><li>DMA cost for <code class=\"mm\">n</code> bytes = 100 + n &micro;s.</li><li>Set them equal: 5n = 100 + n, so 4n = 100 and n = 25.</li><li>Above 25 bytes DMA is cheaper; below it the fixed setup dominates and interrupts win.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 25 bytes (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Comparing only the per-byte rates (5 against 1) and concluding DMA always wins. The fixed setup is what makes interrupts the better choice for short transfers.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "dma-controller", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A DMA controller moves a 4 KB block using 4 bytes per memory cycle, and each memory cycle takes <code class=\"mm\">100 ns</code>. In burst mode it takes one cycle per 4 bytes without releasing the bus. How long does the transfer take, in microseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "102.4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a block (burst) transfer the controller keeps the bus and moves one word per memory cycle, so the time is the number of words times the cycle time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Words in 4 KB = 4096 / 4 = 1024.</li><li>Cycles = 1024, one per word.</li><li>Time = 1024 &times; 100 ns = 102 400 ns.</li><li>102 400 ns = 102.4 &micro;s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 102.4 microseconds.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by 4096, treating each byte as needing its own cycle, which gives 409.6 &micro;s. The cycle count follows the number of <em>words</em>, not bytes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Storing the blocks of a file in the same cylinder reduces which component of the access time?", "options": ["Seek time", "Rotational latency", "Transfer time", "Controller overhead"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Access time = seek + rotational latency + transfer. A cylinder is the set of tracks at one radius, so moving between them needs no radial motion of the head.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Seek time is the time to move the head to the required track, that is, to the required radius.</li><li>All tracks of one cylinder share a radius; switching between them is an electronic head selection, not a mechanical seek.</li><li>Rotational latency and transfer time depend on rotation and on the number of sectors, not on the radius.</li><li>So the component removed by cylinder locality is seek time.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: seek time (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a cylinder also removes rotational latency. The platters must still rotate until the required sector passes under the head.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A disk has 4 platters with both surfaces usable, 1000 cylinders and 200 sectors per track. How many sectors does the disk hold?", "options": ["200 000", "400 000", "800 000", "1 600 000"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Capacity in sectors = surfaces &times; tracks per surface &times; sectors per track, and the number of tracks per surface equals the number of cylinders.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>4 platters with both surfaces usable gives 4 &times; 2 = 8 surfaces.</li><li>Tracks per surface = cylinders = 1000.</li><li>Sectors = 8 &times; 1000 &times; 200 = 1 600 000.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 600 000 sectors (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting one surface per platter, which gives 4 &times; 1000 &times; 200 = 800 000 (option C), or forgetting the cylinders altogether.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about disk geometry are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A sector is the smallest addressable unit of a disk", "A track is one complete circle on one surface", "A disk's transfer rate is independent of how fast it rotates", "The number of tracks on each surface equals the number of cylinders"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Disk geometry is built from a small vocabulary — surface, track, cylinder, sector — and each term has a precise meaning that the performance formulas depend on.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A sector is the unit the controller addresses and the unit that carries its own checksum, so it is the smallest addressable unit — A is true.</li><li>A track is one complete circle written at one radius on one surface — B is true.</li><li>The transfer rate is (bytes per track) &times; (revolutions per second), so it is proportional to the rotation speed — C is false.</li><li>Cylinders are counted once per radius and there is one track per surface at each radius, so tracks per surface = cylinders — D is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Supposing more platters make a single head faster. Extra platters add capacity and allow parallel head reads; the rate of one head is set by rotation and by sectors per track.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A disk rotates at 10 000 rpm and has 500 sectors of 512 bytes per track. What is the transfer time for one sector?", "options": ["6 &micro;s", "12 &micro;s", "24 &micro;s", "60 &micro;s"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> One complete track passes under the head in one revolution, so the time to read one sector is the revolution time divided by the number of sectors on the track.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Revolution time = 60 / 10 000 s = 6 ms.</li><li>One track = 500 sectors, read in one revolution.</li><li>Transfer time per sector = 6 ms / 500 = 0.012 ms = 12 &micro;s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12 &micro;s (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting the whole revolution (6 ms, which is 6000 &micro;s, not 6 &micro;s) or dividing by 1000 rpm instead of 10 000.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A disk's access time is modelled as seek plus rotational latency plus transfer. Which of the following statements about those three components are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Average rotational latency is half the time for one complete revolution", "Transfer time for a block is the block size divided by the transfer rate, so it does not depend on where on the track the block begins", "A disk's average seek time is the arithmetic mean of its minimum and maximum seek times", "Seek time for a short seek is often dominated by head settling time rather than by pure acceleration"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Access time has three components, and each is estimated by its own rule; mixing the rules is the usual source of error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If the target sector is equally likely to be anywhere in a revolution, the expected wait is half a revolution — A is true.</li><li>Transfer time = bytes / transfer rate; the wait for the block to come round is counted as rotational latency, not as transfer — B is true.</li><li>Average seek time is not (min + max) / 2. It is a measured or modelled figure, commonly taken as about one third of a full stroke, because short seeks are far more common — C is false.</li><li>For short seeks the head must accelerate, travel and then settle, and the settling dominates — D is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Taking the midpoint of the minimum and maximum seek as the average. Real seek profiles are non-linear, and short seeks dominate a typical workload.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A disk rotates at 7200 rpm, has an average seek time of <code class=\"mm\">9 ms</code> and 400 sectors of 512 bytes per track. A program reads 8 KB that lies entirely within one track. What is the average time for this read, in milliseconds?", "options": ["9.5 ms", "12.5 ms", "13.5 ms", "17.7 ms"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Access time = seek + average rotational latency + transfer. The transfer part is proportional to the number of sectors taken from the track.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Sectors needed = 8192 / 512 = 16.</li><li>Revolution time = 60 / 7200 s = 8.333 ms, so average rotational latency = 4.167 ms.</li><li>Transfer of 16 sectors = (16 / 400) &times; 8.333 ms = 0.333 ms.</li><li>Total = 9 + 4.167 + 0.333 = 13.5 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 13.5 ms (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging a full revolution for rotational latency, 9 + 8.333 + 0.333 = 17.67 ms (option D). The <em>average</em> wait is half a revolution.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "secondary-storage-disks", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A disk rotates at 15 000 rpm. What is its average rotational latency, in milliseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Average rotational latency is the expected wait for the target sector to reach the head, which is half of one revolution.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Revolution time = 60 / 15 000 s = 0.004 s = 4 ms.</li><li>Average rotational latency = 4 / 2 = 2 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 milliseconds.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 4, the full revolution, or 0.25 from a decimal slip. Only the half-revolution is the average wait.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "How many simultaneous disk failures can a RAID 6 array survive?", "options": ["None", "Two", "One", "As many as there are disks in the array"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RAID 6 stores two independent parity blocks in every stripe, so the array can reconstruct two lost blocks from the survivors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One parity block lets any single lost block in the stripe be recomputed from the others.</li><li>Two independent parities let two lost blocks be recovered, whichever two they are.</li><li>A third loss exceeds what the redundancy can reconstruct, so the array is lost.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: two failures (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding that RAID 6 tolerates any number of failures because it holds two parities. Two is the limit, and only while the array has not already lost more than that.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which RAID level gives the highest read throughput with no redundancy at all?", "options": ["RAID 0", "RAID 1", "RAID 5", "RAID 6"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RAID 0 stripes blocks across all the disks and keeps no parity and no mirror, so every disk carries only data and all of them serve reads in parallel.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every disk holds only data, so the whole raw capacity is usable.</li><li>Reads of different blocks go to different disks at the same time, which gives the highest throughput of the levels listed.</li><li>No redundant block exists, so a single disk failure loses the whole array.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: RAID 0 (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Ranking RAID 1 highest because a read may be served from either copy. Its usable capacity is halved, and it is not the no-redundancy level the question asks for.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about RAID are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["RAID 4 keeps all parity on one dedicated disk, so every write must update that disk", "RAID 6 uses two independent parity blocks, which is why it survives two failures", "A RAID 5 array of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> equal disks has usable capacity <code class=\"mm\">(n &minus; 1)</code> times the size of one disk", "A RAID 0 array of two 1 TB disks has 1 TB of usable capacity"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each RAID level is defined by where its redundancy sits, and that placement fixes both the capacity arithmetic and the write path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>RAID 4 stripes data but puts all parity on one disk; every write touches that disk, so it serialises writes — A is true.</li><li>RAID 6 holds two independent parities per stripe, which is exactly what allows two failures — B is true.</li><li>RAID 5 spreads one disk's worth of parity over all n disks, leaving (n &minus; 1) disks' worth of data — C is true.</li><li>RAID 0 keeps no redundancy, so capacities add: two 1 TB disks give 2 TB — D is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying RAID 5's (n &minus; 1)/n to RAID 0. RAID 0 loses nothing to redundancy, and everything to a single failure.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A RAID 5 array is built from 6 disks of 4 TB each. What is its usable capacity, in TB?", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RAID 5 spends one disk's worth of capacity on parity, distributed across all the disks, so usable capacity is (n &minus; 1) disks' worth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>n = 6 disks.</li><li>Data-bearing capacity = (6 &minus; 1) = 5 disks' worth.</li><li>5 &times; 4 TB = 20 TB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20 TB.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 24 TB, the raw total, or 16 TB, which is RAID 6's (n &minus; 2) rule.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A RAID 10 array is built from 6 disks of 2 TB each. Which statement is true?", "options": ["Usable capacity is 12 TB, because striping adds the capacity of all the disks", "Usable capacity is 10 TB, because one disk's worth is lost to parity", "Usable capacity is 6 TB, but the array cannot survive any failure", "Usable capacity is 6 TB, and the array survives one failure in each mirrored pair, so up to three failures in different pairs"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RAID 10 mirrors inside each pair and stripes across the pairs, so capacity halves and survivability is per pair.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Six disks form three mirrored pairs.</li><li>Each pair stores one disk's worth of data, so usable capacity = 3 &times; 2 TB = 6 TB.</li><li>A pair survives one failure, because its mirror holds a copy.</li><li>Failures in different pairs are independent, so up to three can be survived — but two failures inside the same pair lose that pair, and with it the array.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6 TB usable, surviving one failure per pair (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating RAID 10 as parity-based and applying (n &minus; 1)/n to get 10 TB, or assuming that any second failure is fatal. Neither matches mirror-then-stripe.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U6-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "io-interfaces", "subtopic": "raid-levels", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A RAID 5 array of 5 disks is built from drives whose mean time to failure is <code class=\"mm\">100 000 hours</code> each. One disk has just failed and a rebuild that takes 10 hours has begun. Treating the remaining failures as independent and exponential, what is the probability, in percent, that a second disk fails before the rebuild finishes? (Give your answer to two decimal places.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "0.04", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> After one failure, the disks that can destroy the array are the n &minus; 1 survivors. Their combined failure rate is (n &minus; 1) / MTTF, and for a short window the probability of a failure inside it is approximately rate &times; window.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Remaining disks = 5 &minus; 1 = 4.</li><li>Combined failure rate = 4 / 100 000 = 4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> per hour.</li><li>Window = 10 hours.</li><li>Probability &asymp; 4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> &times; 10 = 4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>.</li><li>As a percentage: 4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> &times; 100 = 0.04%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0.04 percent.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting all five disks instead of the four survivors, which gives 0.05%, or stopping at 0.0004 and calling that the percentage. The question asks for percent.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q01", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A processor uses 32-bit instructions. Every instruction has an 8-bit opcode and two register specifiers of 5 bits each; all the remaining bits form the immediate operand. The width of the immediate field is", "options": ["10 bits", "12 bits", "14 bits", "16 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An instruction format is a budget of bits: the opcode, the register specifiers and the operand field must together fit exactly into one instruction word, so every field is obtained by subtracting the others from the word length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Word length = 32 bits.</li><li>Opcode = 8 bits; register specifiers = 5 + 5 = 10 bits.</li><li>Immediate = 32 − 8 − 10 = 14 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 14 bits</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Subtracting only one register field (giving 19) or forgetting the opcode. A format is only valid if the fields add up to the word length exactly, so the check 8 + 5 + 5 + 14 = 32 is part of the answer.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q02", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A 12-bit machine uses an expanding opcode. The first four bits are the opcode: the twelve values `0000`–`1011` are 2-address instructions with two 4-bit register fields, the two values `1100` and `1101` are 1-address instructions with one 4-bit register field, and the two values `1110` and `1111` open a second 4-bit opcode field for 0-address instructions. How many 0-address instructions can this encoding provide?", "options": ["16", "24", "32", "64"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In an expanding (variable-width) opcode the instruction length stays fixed, but one primary opcode value is spent as an escape: it opens a further opcode field, and each escape value multiplies the number of codes available at the next level.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The twelve 2-address opcodes and the two 1-address opcodes use 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 bits, so they fit the word exactly.</li><li>Two primary values, `1110` and `1111`, are escapes rather than instructions.</li><li>Each escape opens 4 more opcode bits, that is 2⁴ = 16 codes, and the two escapes are independent.</li><li>Total 0-address instructions = 2 × 16 = 32.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 32</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the whole second-level space as 16 (one escape only) or answering 64 by assuming the remaining 4 bits of the 2-address format are also available. Each escape is worth exactly 2⁴ codes, and only two escapes exist.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q03", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "An instruction set must provide 100 distinct operation codes. What is the minimum number of bits in the opcode field?", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An n-bit opcode can name 2ⁿ distinct operations, so the opcode width is the smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ number of operations required.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>2⁶ = 64 < 100, so six bits are not enough.</li><li>2⁷ = 128 ≥ 100, so seven bits suffice (28 codes stay unused).</li><li>The width is therefore ⌈log₂ 100⌉ = 7.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing 6 after noticing that 64 is the largest power of two below 100, or writing 100 as if the opcode stored a count rather than a code.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q04", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A 16-bit instruction has a 4-bit opcode, two register specifier fields of equal width, and a 4-bit immediate. Each register specifier occupies", "options": ["3 bits", "4 bits", "5 bits", "6 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Equal-width register fields split whatever bit budget is left after the opcode and the immediate, so the arithmetic is (word length − opcode − immediate) / 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bits left for the two register fields = 16 − 4 − 4 = 8.</li><li>The two fields are equal, so each is 8 / 2 = 4 bits.</li><li>Check: 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16. Four bits address 2⁴ = 16 registers.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 4 bits</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing the whole word by two, or assuming a register field must be 5 bits because most textbook machines have 32 registers. The width is fixed by this format's budget, not by a convention.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q05", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about expanding (variable-width) opcodes are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The instruction length stays fixed while the number of opcode bits varies from one instruction class to another.", "Spending one primary opcode value as an escape reduces the number of instruction classes that the primary field alone can encode by one.", "An expanding opcode can encode more distinct instructions than the same number of bits used as a single fixed opcode field.", "The decoder must first determine the instruction length before it can identify the opcode."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Expanding opcodes trade opcode space for operand space at a fixed instruction length: short opcodes keep long address fields, and rare instructions pay with an escape code.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition: the word is 12 bits (or 16, or 32) for every class; only the split between opcode bits and operand bits changes.</li><li>(B) is true by construction — the escape value is no longer an instruction, so the primary field loses exactly one class in exchange for a whole second-level space.</li><li>(C) is true: 12 two-address classes plus 2 one-address classes plus 2 escapes × 16 = 32 zero-address classes give 46 classes here, against 16 with a plain 4-bit opcode.</li><li>(D) is false: with a fixed instruction length the decoder already knows how many bits the word has; it is the *field split* that varies, not the length.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing an expanding opcode with a variable-length instruction set (as in x86). Expanding opcodes keep a fixed word; the price of extra codes is paid in operand bits, never in instruction length.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q06", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "An 8-bit instruction set uses a 2-bit opcode. The values `00`, `01` and `10` are 1-address instructions whose remaining 6 bits are an address. The value `11` is an escape that opens a 6-bit opcode field for 0-address instructions. What is the largest number of 0-address instructions this encoding can provide?", "options": [], "answer_display": "64", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An escape opcode releases the operand bits of the shorter formats for use as opcode bits, so the size of the escaped space is 2 raised to the number of released bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The escape value `11` leaves 6 bits free in the 8-bit word.</li><li>Those 6 bits become a 6-bit opcode field: 2⁶ = 64 distinct 0-address instructions.</li><li>The three 1-address classes are unaffected, so the maximum is 64.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 64</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 63 by reserving one of the 64 codes for a further escape that the question does not ask for, or answering 4 by counting opcode values instead of codes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q07", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A machine has a 6-bit opcode. The value `000000` is reserved as an escape that opens a second 6-bit opcode field, and every other primary value is an ordinary instruction. How many ordinary instructions does the primary opcode space hold?", "options": ["32", "63", "64", "127"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Reserving one opcode value for an escape removes exactly one instruction from the primary space; the remaining values are ordinary instructions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A 6-bit field has 2⁶ = 64 values.</li><li>One value, `000000`, is not an instruction but an escape.</li><li>Ordinary instructions = 64 − 1 = 63.</li><li>The escape in turn opens 2⁶ = 64 further codes, which is where the remaining instructions live.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 63</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 64 by forgetting the escape, or 127 by assuming the escaped codes join the primary space. The two spaces are separate, and only the primary space is asked about.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q08", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "An instruction at address 1000 has address field 500 and uses indirect addressing. Memory[500] = 620 and Memory[620] = 7. The effective address of the operand is", "options": ["500", "620", "7", "1000"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In indirect addressing the address field points to a memory word that holds the address of the operand, so the effective address is the *contents* of the location named by the address field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Direct addressing would make the effective address 500.</li><li>Indirect addressing adds one level: EA = Memory[500] = 620.</li><li>The operand itself is Memory[620] = 7, which is the operand, not its address.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 620</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 7 (the operand value) or 500 (the address field). Indirect addressing asks for the address held in memory, one dereference short of the operand.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q09", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "An indexed instruction uses base register R1 = 2000 and index register R2 = 36; the effective address is computed as R1 + R2 with no scaling. The operand is read from memory address", "options": ["1964", "2000", "2036", "36"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Indexed addressing forms the effective address by adding a register (the index, which the program changes) to a base, which is what lets one instruction walk through an array.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>EA = base + index = 2000 + 36.</li><li>EA = 2036.</li><li>With a scale factor of 4 (a 4-byte element), the same instruction would give 2000 + 4 × 36 = 2144; the question states no scaling.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 2036</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Subtracting instead of adding, or applying an array-element scale that the question did not state. Scaling must be specified; it is not part of the definition of indexed addressing.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q10", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A two-word instruction occupies addresses 1000 and 1002. Its second word holds a PC-relative offset of +24, which is added to the PC value 1004 — the address of the next instruction. The branch target address is", "options": [], "answer_display": "1028", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> PC-relative addressing adds the offset to the PC *after* the instruction has been fetched, so the reference point is the next instruction, not the current one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The instruction occupies 1000 and 1002, so the next instruction begins at 1004.</li><li>Target = PC + offset = 1004 + 24.</li><li>Target = 1028.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1028</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the offset to 1000, the address of the branch itself, which gives 1024. Every PC-relative computation is anchored at the end of the instruction, and a two-word instruction ends 4 bytes later.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q11", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A program must run at a different load address on every execution without being reassembled. Which addressing mode makes the code position independent?", "options": ["Direct (absolute) addressing", "PC-relative addressing", "Indexed addressing with an absolute base address", "Indirect addressing through a fixed memory location"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Code is position independent when every address it forms is relative to the program counter, because the PC moves with the code while absolute addresses do not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Direct addressing embeds a fixed absolute address, so the code breaks when the base address changes.</li><li>Indexed addressing with an absolute base and indirect addressing through a fixed location both still contain an absolute address.</li><li>PC-relative addressing computes target = PC + offset, and both the PC and the target shift by the same amount, so the code relocates unchanged.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — PC-relative addressing</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any register-based mode is relocatable. Only the register that holds the moving base matters: the PC moves with the program, an arbitrary base register does not.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q12", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A byte-addressable machine has 16-bit instructions. A branch instruction at address 1000 carries an 8-bit two's-complement offset that is counted in *words* and added to the PC of the next instruction. The offset byte is `0xF8`. The branch target is", "options": ["986", "992", "1002", "1018"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A branch offset must be sign-extended and then scaled by the instruction size before it is added to the PC; ignoring either step gives a plausible but wrong target.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>0xF8 as an 8-bit two's-complement number is 248 − 256 = −8, so the branch goes backwards.</li><li>One word is 2 bytes, so the scaled offset is −8 × 2 = −16 bytes.</li><li>The next instruction is at 1000 + 2 = 1002.</li><li>Target = 1002 − 16 = 986.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 986</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 0xF8 as +248 and branching forwards, forgetting the word scaling and answering 994, or anchoring at 1000 instead of 1002. Three separate traps sit on this one instruction.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q13", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about addressing modes are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In register indirect addressing the effective address is held in a register, so fetching the operand costs one memory access.", "Immediate addressing requires a memory access to fetch the operand.", "In direct addressing the address field of the instruction is itself the effective address.", "In indexed addressing the effective address is formed by adding a base and an index."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An addressing mode is a rule for computing the effective address; the number of memory accesses it costs follows directly from how many levels of indirection the rule contains.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the register supplies the address, and only the operand fetch touches memory.</li><li>(B) is false: in immediate addressing the operand is a field of the instruction and is already in the instruction register, so no operand fetch happens at all.</li><li>(C) is true: that is the definition of direct (absolute) addressing.</li><li>(D) is true: EA = base + index, possibly with a scale factor.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that immediate addressing still needs a fetch because the value 'comes from memory eventually'. It does not: the value is inside the instruction word, which is why immediate is the cheapest mode for constants.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q14", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A load instruction uses base-plus-displacement addressing with base register = 3000 and a displacement field of −12. The effective address is", "options": [], "answer_display": "2988", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In base-plus-displacement addressing the displacement is a signed value, so it can address elements before the base as well as after it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>EA = base + displacement = 3000 + (−12).</li><li>EA = 2988.</li><li>A negative displacement is normal: it is how a frame pointer reaches locals and saved registers that lie below it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2988</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Ignoring the sign and answering 3012. The displacement is signed precisely so that one base register can serve a whole activation record on both sides of itself.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q15", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following addressing modes needs no memory access to obtain the operand itself?", "options": ["Direct addressing", "Indirect addressing", "Immediate addressing", "Indexed addressing"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The cost of a mode is the number of memory accesses between the instruction and the operand: immediate needs none, direct needs one, indirect needs two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Immediate: the operand is part of the instruction word, which has already been fetched — no additional access.</li><li>Direct: one operand fetch from the address in the instruction.</li><li>Indirect: one access for the address plus one for the operand.</li><li>Indexed: the address is computed in the ALU, then one operand fetch.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — immediate addressing</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing indirect because 'it is more flexible'. Flexibility is bought with memory cycles; the question asks which mode is cheapest, and that is immediate.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q16", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following is characteristic of a RISC instruction set?", "options": ["Arithmetic instructions may take one operand directly from memory", "Fixed-length instructions and a load/store architecture", "A microprogrammed control store that decodes most instructions", "A large number of instruction formats of different lengths"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RISC is defined by regularity: fixed-length instructions, a small set of simple formats, and memory reached only by explicit load and store instructions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) describes a memory-to-memory CISC arithmetic instruction; RISC ALU operands come from registers.</li><li>(B) is the defining pair: uniform words are easy to fetch and decode, and load/store keeps the data path simple.</li><li>(C) and (D) describe microcoded, variable-length CISC designs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating 'RISC' as a marketing word for 'faster'. The testable content is architectural: fixed length, load/store, many registers, simple addressing modes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q17", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following is characteristic of a CISC instruction set?", "options": ["Every instruction completes in exactly one clock cycle by design", "All arithmetic operands must be loaded into registers first", "Variable-length instructions and complex instructions implemented with microcode", "A single fixed 32-bit instruction format"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CISC spends instruction-set complexity to save program bytes and instructions: variable lengths, rich addressing modes, and microcode to decode the complex cases.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is a RISC goal, not a CISC property.</li><li>(B) is the load/store rule of RISC.</li><li>(C) matches CISC: variable length plus a microprogrammed control store.</li><li>(D) is a fixed-format RISC design.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming CISC means 'no pipelining' or 'old'. Modern CISC processors pipeline heavily and translate complex instructions into micro-operations; the instruction *format* is what stays complex.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q18", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements comparing RISC and CISC designs are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A RISC machine typically has a larger register file than a CISC machine, because operands must come from registers.", "A single CISC instruction can perform work that a RISC machine needs several instructions to do, such as a memory-to-memory add.", "RISC instructions are normally decoded by microcode held in a control store.", "RISC's simple, fixed-format instructions make pipelining easier than a variable-length CISC format."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two designs differ in where they spend complexity: RISC in the compiler and the register file, CISC in the decoder and the microcode.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: with load/store arithmetic, keeping values in registers is the only way to avoid traffic, so register files grew to 32 and beyond.</li><li>(B) is true: memory-to-memory add and string operations are classic CISC instructions.</li><li>(C) is false: hardwired control is the RISC norm precisely because the instructions are simple; microcode is the CISC answer to complexity.</li><li>(D) is true: a known instruction length lets the fetch stage take a whole instruction every cycle.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the control-unit story. Simple, regular instructions are cheap to hardwire; complex, irregular ones are what motivate a control store.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q19", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A 32-bit RISC machine has 32 general-purpose registers and a three-register instruction format. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Each register specifier needs 5 bits.", "The three register specifiers together occupy 15 bits of the instruction.", "If the machine had 64 registers, 6 bits per specifier would be enough.", "Four bits per specifier would still be able to name all 32 registers."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A register specifier is an index into the register file, so its width is ⌈log₂(number of registers)⌉ and the fields simply add up inside the instruction word.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>32 = 2⁵, so each specifier is 5 bits: (A) is true.</li><li>Three specifiers = 3 × 5 = 15 bits: (B) is true.</li><li>64 = 2⁶, so 6 bits per specifier would suffice: (C) is true.</li><li>4 bits name only 2⁴ = 16 registers, so (D) is false — 16 of the 32 registers would be unreachable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that register width is logarithmic in the register count, which makes 'a few more registers' cost only one extra bit per specifier — and three extra bits per instruction.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q20", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Why does a fixed-length RISC instruction format make pipelining easier than a variable-length CISC format?", "options": ["Because fixed-length instructions always complete in fewer clock cycles", "Because the instruction length is known before decoding, so fetch and decode need no length-dependent stalling or byte-alignment logic", "Because fixed-length instructions never create data hazards", "Because a RISC machine needs no branch handling in the pipeline"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pipeline works best when every stage's work per cycle is identical. A known instruction length lets the fetch stage take exactly one instruction per cycle without waiting to learn how long it is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With variable lengths, the fetch unit must decode the first bytes to learn the length, then shift by a variable amount; the pipeline needs extra alignment logic and can stall.</li><li>With a fixed length, instruction i is always at a fixed offset from instruction i − 1, so fetch, decode and issue stay in lockstep.</li><li>Cycle counts, data hazards and branch handling are separate issues: a fixed length does not remove them (a load-use hazard still stalls), so (A), (C) and (D) are false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the benefits of a fixed format with the elimination of hazards. RISC pipelines still need forwarding, stalls and branch prediction; the fixed length only removes the fetch/decode uncertainty.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q21", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A stack grows downwards and the stack pointer holds the address of the top element. Each item is 4 bytes. With the stack pointer at 1000, after three pushes the stack pointer is", "options": ["988", "996", "1000", "1012"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A downward-growing stack decrements the stack pointer before storing, so each push moves the pointer down by one item size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each push moves SP down by 4 bytes.</li><li>Three pushes move it 3 × 4 = 12 bytes.</li><li>SP = 1000 − 12 = 988.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 988</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding instead of subtracting because the pointer 'grows with the stack', or decrementing by 1 instead of by the item size. Both errors come from forgetting that the pointer counts bytes while the stack counts items.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q22", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A recursive procedure uses 32-byte activation records and the stack grows downwards. At the first call the stack pointer is 8000. When the recursion is five activations deep, the stack pointer is", "options": [], "answer_display": "7840", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every activation of a recursive procedure allocates its own record on the stack, so the depth of the recursion times the record size is exactly how far the stack pointer has moved.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Five activations means five records are live at once.</li><li>Total allocated = 5 × 32 = 160 bytes.</li><li>The stack grows downwards, so SP = 8000 − 160 = 7840.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7840</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by four (counting only the four returns still pending) or moving upwards. The record of the deepest activation is allocated as soon as it is entered, so all five records are live.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q23", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A subroutine saves its return address in one fixed memory location. It is then called recursively. What happens?", "options": ["The recursion works, because each call overwrites the location with the current return address", "The outer call's return address is overwritten, so when the inner call returns, control comes back to the same subroutine instead of the original caller", "The processor raises an overflow exception on the first recursive call", "Nothing changes, because the return address is also kept in the program counter"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A return address is per-activation state. A single fixed slot holds only the most recent one, so it cannot support nesting; a stack of return addresses can.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Call 1 stores its return address R1 in the fixed slot.</li><li>The recursive call stores R2 in the same slot, destroying R1.</li><li>The inner return uses R2 correctly, so control returns into the subroutine again.</li><li>When the outer level finally returns, the slot holds R2, so it jumps back into itself — an infinite loop rather than a return to the caller.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the PC 'also remembers' the address. The PC holds only the current instruction address; the return address exists solely where the linkage put it, and one slot means one level of nesting.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q24", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about subroutine linkage are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A call instruction saves the address of the instruction that follows the call.", "Using one fixed memory location for the return address prevents reentrant or recursive use of the subroutine.", "A stack-based linkage supports nested and recursive calls because every activation gets its own frame.", "The stack pointer must be restored by the caller after the callee returns, because the callee cannot know the caller's stack pointer."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Linkage is about where per-activation state lives. One slot allows one activation; a stack allows arbitrarily many, which is exactly the difference between a leaf-only helper and a reentrant procedure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true by definition: the return address is the address of the next instruction, not the call itself.</li><li>(B) is true: the second activation overwrites the first activation's return address.</li><li>(C) is true: each call pushes a frame holding its own return address, saved registers and locals.</li><li>(D) is false: the callee's epilogue restores the stack pointer (and the frame pointer) before returning, which is why the caller's frame survives.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the responsibility for the stack pointer. Restoring SP is part of the callee's epilogue; a callee that leaves the stack unbalanced is a bug, not a convention.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q25", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A stack machine evaluates the postfix expression `A B C * + D E * F + *`. What is the maximum number of operands the stack must hold at any moment?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In postfix evaluation each operand pushes one item and each operator pops two and pushes one, so the stack depth at any point is the running count, and its maximum is the extra storage the expression needs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A → 1; B → 2; C → 3 (maximum so far 3).</li><li>* → 2; + → 1. The first sub-expression needs depth 3.</li><li>D → 2; E → 3; * → 2; F → 3; + → 2; * → 1.</li><li>The maximum over the whole expression is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the number of distinct variables (6) or the depth at the end (1). The requirement is the peak of the running depth, which occurs while `A B C` and again while `D E * F` are on the stack.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q26", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A machine has 64 general-purpose registers and a 32-bit instruction format with one opcode field, two register specifiers and an 8-bit immediate. What is the widest opcode field this format can have?", "options": ["10 bits", "12 bits", "14 bits", "16 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An instruction format is a budget of bits: the opcode, the register specifiers and the immediate must together fill the word exactly, so the opcode width is whatever the other fields leave over.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>64 registers need 6-bit specifiers, because 2<sup>6</sup> = 64.</li><li>The two register fields therefore take 6 + 6 = 12 bits.</li><li>The immediate takes a further 8 bits, so 12 + 8 = 20 bits are already committed.</li><li>The opcode gets 32 &minus; 20 = 12 bits, and the check 12 + 6 + 6 + 8 = 32 confirms the format is legal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 12 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing 64 (the number of registers) as the register-field width, or counting only one register field and answering 18 bits, which does not even fit the word once both fields and the immediate are included.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q27", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A 24-bit instruction has a 5-bit opcode, a 4-bit register field and a 5-bit register field. Every remaining bit belongs to a signed immediate. How many bits wide is that immediate?", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Fields in a fixed-length instruction partition the word, so the last field is obtained by subtracting every other field from the word length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Opcode = 5 bits and the two register fields = 4 + 5 = 9 bits, so 14 bits are committed.</li><li>Immediate = 24 &minus; 14 = 10 bits.</li><li>Check: 5 + 4 + 5 + 10 = 24, so the partition is exact and no bit is wasted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Subtracting the register fields from 24 but forgetting the opcode, which gives 15; or assuming both register fields are the same width because they often are, which gives 11.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q28", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A 16-bit machine has a k-bit opcode and a single address field occupying all the remaining bits. It must provide at least 100 operation codes and must be able to address 512 words of memory. The smallest legal value of k is", "options": ["6", "7", "8", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Opcode width and address-field width compete for the same word. The address field must be wide enough for the memory, and whatever remains is available for the opcode &mdash; so the binding constraint is whichever of the two requirements is tighter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Addressing 512 words needs 9 bits, because 2<sup>9</sup> = 512.</li><li>That leaves 16 &minus; 9 = 7 bits for the opcode, and 2<sup>7</sup> = 128 &ge; 100, so 7 works.</li><li>A 6-bit opcode would give only 2<sup>6</sup> = 64 operation codes, which fails the 100 requirement even though it leaves 10 address bits.</li><li>So the smallest legal <i>k</i> is 7, and the format is 7 + 9 = 16 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 because the address field then has room to spare. A spare address bit is worthless here; the opcode requirement of 100 codes is the binding constraint and it forces 7.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q29", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about instruction formats and opcodes are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Widening the opcode field by one bit doubles the number of operation codes and removes one bit from some other field.", "A fixed-length instruction set can still use an expanding opcode.", "In a 24-bit instruction with a 7-bit opcode and three 4-bit register fields, an immediate field can be at most 5 bits.", "A two-address instruction is always shorter than a three-address instruction that uses the same opcode width."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An opcode is a code, so each extra bit doubles its capacity; and an expanding opcode changes how the available code space is <i>partitioned</i>, not how long the instruction is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Each extra opcode bit doubles the number of codes, and in a fixed-length word that bit has to come from another field. True.</li><li>(B) An expanding opcode keeps the word length fixed and varies only the number of bits used as opcode, so the two are compatible. True.</li><li>(C) 7 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 19 bits are committed, leaving 24 &minus; 19 = 5 for the immediate. True.</li><li>(D) A two-address instruction can be the same length as a three-address one; the bits freed by dropping an address are simply reused as immediate or opcode. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading (B) as false by confusing an expanding opcode with a variable-length instruction set. The opcode expands into a field that was already in the word; the word itself does not change size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q30", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A processor uses 16-bit instructions. A three-address instruction has a 4-bit opcode and three 4-bit register fields, and 14 of the 16 opcode values are used for such instructions. The two remaining values are escapes into a 4-bit secondary opcode field; a secondary value followed by two 4-bit register fields is a two-address instruction, and 30 two-address instructions are defined. Any secondary value still unused is an escape into a 4-bit tertiary opcode field, and a tertiary value followed by one 4-bit register field is a one-address instruction. How many one-address instructions can this encoding provide?", "options": [], "answer_display": "32", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An escape reserves one code in a field and spends it on a nested opcode field. Each level multiplies the available codes by 2<sup>k</sup> for the k bits it releases, and only the codes left over at a level become escapes into the next.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Level 1: 16 primary codes, 14 used for three-address instructions, so 16 &minus; 14 = 2 are escapes.</li><li>Level 2: each escape releases 4 bits, giving 2 &times; 2<sup>4</sup> = 32 two-address slots. 30 are defined, so 32 &minus; 30 = 2 remain as escapes.</li><li>Level 3: each of those 2 escapes releases 4 bits again, giving 2 &times; 2<sup>4</sup> = 32 one-address codes.</li><li>Each such code sits in a word of 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16 bits, exactly one register field left over, so all 32 are usable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 32 one-address instructions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying both escape levels together to get 2 &times; 16 &times; 16 = 512. A code spent on an escape at level 2 is no longer a two-address instruction, so the levels are consumed one at a time, not multiplied across the whole space.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q31", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A program contains 3,000 register-to-register operations and 2,000 memory-access operations. Machine X encodes every instruction in 32 bits. Machine Y encodes a register-to-register operation in 16 bits and a memory-access operation in 48 bits. How many bytes of code does Y save compared with X?", "options": ["1,000 bytes", "2,000 bytes", "4,000 bytes", "6,000 bytes"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Code size is the sum over instruction classes of (count &times; encoded length). Comparing two instruction sets means computing that sum for each, not comparing a single instruction length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>X: 5,000 instructions &times; 4 bytes = 20,000 bytes.</li><li>Y: register-to-register 3,000 &times; 2 = 6,000 bytes; memory access 2,000 &times; 6 = 12,000 bytes; total 18,000 bytes.</li><li>Saving = 20,000 &minus; 18,000 = 2,000 bytes, about 10% of X's code.</li><li>The saving exists only because the frequent class is short; a longer memory format does not by itself shrink the program.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 2,000 bytes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding that the variable-length machine must be smaller and then computing only one class, or treating the 48-bit format as a loss that cancels the gain without doing the arithmetic.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q32", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "instruction-formats-opcodes", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A 20-bit instruction word carries a 5-bit opcode. A Class A instruction uses the opcode plus two 5-bit register fields and a 5-bit immediate; a Class B instruction uses the opcode plus three 5-bit register fields. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The opcode alone tells the decoder whether the word is a Class A or a Class B instruction.", "Class A and Class B together can define at most 32 distinct operations.", "If 20 opcode values are given to Class B, at most 12 remain for Class A.", "The 5-bit immediate of a Class A instruction can represent the value 63."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When two instruction classes share one opcode field, the field is <i>partitioned</i> between them. The opcode is therefore self-describing, and the classes compete for the same finite code space.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Both formats put the opcode in the same five bits and both are 20 bits long, so no separate length or tag is needed: the opcode identifies the class. (A) is true.</li><li>A 5-bit field has 2<sup>5</sup> = 32 codes in total, and the two classes share them, so 32 is the ceiling on distinct operations. (B) is true.</li><li>Partitioning is a division of those 32 codes, so 32 &minus; 20 = 12 remain for Class A. (C) is true.</li><li>A 5-bit immediate holds 32 patterns: 0 to 31 unsigned, or &minus;16 to 15 in two's complement. 63 needs 6 bits or more. (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a 5-bit field as if it could hold any value below 100, and missing that the classes are sharing one code space rather than each owning a full 5-bit opcode.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q33", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A machine offers the post-increment mode `(R1)+`, in which the operand is fetched from the address held in R1 and R1 is then advanced by the operand size. Each operand is 4 bytes. With R1 = 100 and one access through `(R1)+`, the value of R1 after the instruction is", "options": ["96", "100", "104", "400"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Autoincrement modes combine the access and the pointer update in one instruction. Post-increment touches the operand first and updates the register afterwards, by the operand size, not by one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The access uses the current value 100 as the effective address.</li><li>Post-increment then adds the operand size, 4 bytes, to R1.</li><li>R1 becomes 100 + 4 = 104, and the next access through <code class=\"mm\">(R1)+</code> reads address 104.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 104.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Incrementing by 1 rather than by the operand size, giving 101, or applying a pre-decrement and answering 96. The direction and the step both matter.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q34", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "An instruction has address field 300 and uses direct addressing. `Memory[300]` holds 45. The effective address and the operand are respectively", "options": ["300 and 45", "45 and 300", "300 and 300", "345 and 45"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The effective address is the address the operand is actually read from. In direct addressing the address field <i>is</i> that address, so exactly one memory access is needed to fetch the operand.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Direct addressing: effective address = the address field = 300.</li><li>The operand is the content of that location, <code class=\"mm\">Memory[300]</code> = 45.</li><li>One memory access beyond the instruction fetch is enough; no pointer is dereferenced.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; effective address 300, operand 45.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the two, which is the classic slip: the address field is the location, not the value. Confusing this with indirect addressing, where 45 would itself be treated as an address.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q35", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A machine computes an indexed effective address as `base + s × index`, where s is the operand size in bytes. The base register holds 1000, the index register holds 7 and the operand size is 8 bytes. The effective address is", "options": [], "answer_display": "1056", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When the index register holds an array subscript rather than a byte offset, the hardware must scale it by the element size before adding it to the base.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Scaled index = 8 &times; 7 = 56 bytes.</li><li>Effective address = base + scaled index = 1000 + 56.</li><li>1000 + 56 = 1056, which is the address of the eighth 8-byte element starting at 1000.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1056.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the raw subscript to the base and answering 1007. Without the scale factor every subscript would address the same byte of a different element.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q36", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A branch instruction carries a 6-bit two's-complement displacement that is sign-extended and then multiplied by 4, the instruction size, before being added to the address of the next instruction. The reach of this branch, relative to the address of the next instruction, is", "options": ["−128 to +124 bytes", "−124 to +128 bytes", "−64 to +60 bytes", "−32 to +31 bytes"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A signed field is asymmetric: an n-bit two's-complement number ranges from &minus;2<sup>n&minus;1</sup> to 2<sup>n&minus;1</sup> &minus; 1. Scaling multiplies both ends, so the asymmetry survives the scaling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A 6-bit two's-complement value ranges from &minus;32 to +31.</li><li>Multiplying by the instruction size 4 scales both ends: &minus;32 &times; 4 = &minus;128 and +31 &times; 4 = +124.</li><li>The branch therefore reaches 128 bytes backwards and 124 bytes forwards from the next instruction's address.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; &minus;128 to +124 bytes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the range is symmetric and answering &minus;128 to +128, or forgetting the scale factor and answering the raw &minus;32 to +31. Note also that the anchor is the <i>next</i> instruction, not the branch itself.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q37", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A machine supports the addressing modes `(R)`, `(R)+`, `−(R)` and `d(R)`. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In `(R)+` the operand is at the address held in R, and R is then increased by the operand size.", "In `−(R)` the register is decreased by the operand size first, and the operand is then at the new address.", "`(R)+` and `−(R)` together let a stack that grows downwards be pushed and popped without separate push or pop instructions.", "In `(R)` the register is modified after the operand has been accessed."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Autoincrement and autodecrement are register-indirect modes with a side effect on the register. The position of the sign tells you whether the update happens before or after the access.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Post-increment: access first at the current address, then add the operand size. True.</li><li>(B) Pre-decrement: subtract first, then access at the new address. True.</li><li>(C) On a downward-growing stack, <code class=\"mm\">&minus;(R)</code> reserves space and stores (push) while <code class=\"mm\">(R)+</code> loads and releases (pop). True.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">(R)</code> is plain register indirect: the register is read to form the address and is left unchanged. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the pre/post convention, which turns a push into a pop. The sign sits on the side where the update happens: <code class=\"mm\">&minus;(R)</code> updates before the access, <code class=\"mm\">(R)+</code> after it.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q38", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A program executes 106 instructions. Every instruction fetch is one memory access, and 20% of the instructions are loads or stores that need one further memory access for their operand. One memory access takes 10 ns. What is the total memory-access time of the program, in milliseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of memory accesses is the instruction fetch count plus the operand accesses the addressing modes demand; each mode has a fixed access cost that can be read straight off its definition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Instruction fetches = 10<sup>6</sup> accesses.</li><li>Operand accesses = 20% of 10<sup>6</sup> = 2 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> accesses.</li><li>Total = 1.2 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> accesses &times; 10 ns = 1.2 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> ns.</li><li>1.2 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> ns = 12 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> ns = 12 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12 milliseconds.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the operand accesses (2 ms) or only the fetches (10 ms). The fetch is a memory access too, and it is the one every instruction pays.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q39", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "addressing-modes-effective-address", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A 32-bit machine computes an effective address as `base + scale × index + displacement` with all arithmetic modulo 232. The base register holds `0x00001000`, the index register holds `0x1FFFFFFF`, the scale is 8 and the displacement is 8. The effective address is", "options": ["0x00001000", "0x80001000", "0x1000FFF8", "0x00000FF8"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective-address arithmetic is performed in the machine's word size, so a carry out of the top bit is discarded rather than kept as a 33rd bit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Scale &times; index = 8 &times; 0x1FFFFFFF = 0xFFFFFFF8.</li><li>Add the base: 0xFFFFFFF8 + 0x00001000 = 0x1_00000FF8, and modulo 2<sup>32</sup> that is 0x00000FF8.</li><li>Add the displacement: 0x00000FF8 + 8 = 0x00001000.</li><li>The wraparound is not an error: the hardware keeps only the low 32 bits, so the effective address is 0x00001000, which is 4096.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 0x00001000 (4096).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Keeping the carry and answering 0x80001000, or stopping one step early at 0x00000FF8 and forgetting the displacement. Address arithmetic wraps exactly like any other 32-bit arithmetic.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q40", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A RISC processor normally uses hardwired control rather than microcode because", "options": ["microcode cannot implement conditional branches", "its simple, regular instructions can be decoded by fixed combinational logic, so no control-store read sits on the critical path", "it has more instructions to decode than a CISC machine", "hardwired control handles variable-length instructions better than microcode"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two control styles trade flexibility against speed. Microcode buys complex instructions at the price of a control-store read per micro-operation; hardwired logic is fast but only practical when the instruction set is small and regular.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A RISC instruction set is small, fixed-length and regular, so the control signals can be expressed as fixed Boolean functions of the opcode, flags and timing state.</li><li>No control memory is consulted, so the control path is a combinational delay rather than a memory access.</li><li>CISC instructions are numerous and complex, which is exactly the case where microcode's compactness wins.</li><li>Options (A) and (D) are simply false; (C) inverts the premise, since RISC has fewer, simpler instructions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing hardwired control is unconditionally faster. It is faster per cycle but needs a regular instruction set to be buildable, which is why CISC machines historically chose microcode.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q41", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A CISC instruction set reduces the number of instructions a program must execute mainly by", "options": ["using a larger register file so that fewer loads are needed", "providing powerful multi-step instructions, such as memory-to-memory operations, that the control unit expands into several micro-operations", "making every instruction exactly one word long", "moving address computation out of the hardware and into the compiler"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CISC moves complexity into the instruction encoding and the control unit; one instruction then does the work of several simpler ones, at the cost of variable length and longer execution.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A CISC instruction may read operands from memory, compute, and write back in a single encoding.</li><li>The control unit decomposes it into micro-operations, so the programmer or compiler sees one instruction where a RISC machine would need a load, an ALU operation and a store.</li><li>Fewer, richer instructions mean a smaller instruction count for the same source program.</li><li>A larger register file and compiler-side address computation are RISC traits, and fixed one-word length is the opposite of the CISC style.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing 'fewer instructions' with 'fewer cycles'. The CISC instruction count falls but the average CPI rises, and the two effects must both be counted before claiming a speed advantage.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q42", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A CISC machine runs a program in 1,000,000 instructions at an average CPI of 4 and a 2 GHz clock. A RISC machine runs the same program in 1,600,000 instructions at an average CPI of 1.2 and a 3 GHz clock. Which machine is faster, and by what factor?", "options": ["The CISC machine, by 1.5×", "The RISC machine, by 2.5×", "The RISC machine, by 3.125×", "The two machines take the same time"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Execution time is instruction count &times; CPI &divide; clock rate. All three factors must be combined, because instruction count, CPI and clock rate move in opposite directions between RISC and CISC.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>CISC: 10<sup>6</sup> &times; 4 &divide; (2 &times; 10<sup>9</sup>) = 4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> &divide; 2 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> = 2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> s = 2 ms.</li><li>RISC: 1.6 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> &times; 1.2 &divide; (3 &times; 10<sup>9</sup>) = 1.92 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> &divide; 3 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> = 0.64 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> s = 0.64 ms.</li><li>Ratio = 2 &divide; 0.64 = 3.125, so the RISC machine is faster by 3.125&times;.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; the RISC machine, by 3.125&times;.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Comparing instruction counts alone and concluding the CISC machine wins, or comparing clock rates alone. A higher instruction count is harmless if CPI and cycle time fall by more.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q43", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Two machines run the same program. Machine A executes 2.0 × 109 instructions at a CPI of 1.5 with a 3 GHz clock; Machine B executes 1.4 × 109 instructions at a CPI of 2.5 with a 4 GHz clock. What is the ratio of A's execution time to B's? Give the answer to two decimal places.", "options": [], "answer_display": "1.14", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Execution time = instruction count &times; CPI &divide; clock frequency. A ratio of two execution times is therefore a ratio of two such products, with the 10<sup>9</sup> factors cancelling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Machine A: 2.0 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; 1.5 &divide; 3 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> = 3.0 &divide; 3 = 1.00 s.</li><li>Machine B: 1.4 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; 2.5 &divide; 4 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> = 3.5 &divide; 4 = 0.875 s.</li><li>Ratio A : B = 1.00 &divide; 0.875 = 1.142857&hellip;</li><li>Rounded to two decimal places, the ratio is 1.14, so B is about 14% faster.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.14 (to two decimal places).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 1.142857 or 1.143 when two decimal places were asked for, or inverting the ratio to 0.88 by dividing B's time by A's. The question asks for A relative to B.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q44", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "risc-vs-cisc", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements is FALSE?", "options": ["A CISC memory-to-memory add touches memory more often per instruction than any RISC instruction does.", "Because its instructions are simpler, a RISC program always completes in fewer clock cycles than the equivalent CISC program.", "A RISC design leans on the compiler for register allocation and instruction scheduling.", "A CISC machine may still decode its most frequent simple instructions with fast hardwired logic."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Simplicity of an instruction is not the same as a lower total cycle count. A RISC program executes more instructions, so cycles per instruction and instructions per program have to be multiplied before any claim about total cycles is made.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A memory-to-memory add reads two operands from memory and writes one result, more data accesses than the single access of a RISC load or store. True.</li><li>(B) RISC instructions are simpler, but a RISC program needs more of them. Total cycles = instructions &times; CPI, and the larger instruction count can outweigh the lower CPI. The word 'always' makes the claim false.</li><li>(C) RISC exposes the pipeline to the compiler, which allocates registers and schedules instructions around load and branch delays. True.</li><li>(D) Modern CISC implementations translate their common simple instructions into internal RISC-like operations decoded by hardwired logic. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Accepting (B) because it sounds like the usual RISC-versus-CISC summary. The summary is about CPI, not about total cycles, and a lower CPI with many more instructions can still lose.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q45", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A call instruction pushes a 4-byte return address, and the callee's prologue then pushes three 4-byte callee-saved registers. The stack grows downwards and each push moves the stack pointer by 4 bytes. By how many bytes does the stack pointer move in total?", "options": ["4", "12", "16", "20"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each push moves the stack pointer by one item, so the total movement is the number of pushes multiplied by the item size, regardless of the direction of growth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Pushes performed: 1 return address + 3 saved registers = 4 pushes.</li><li>Each push moves the stack pointer by 4 bytes.</li><li>Total movement = 4 &times; 4 = 16 bytes, and the stack pointer is 16 bytes lower than before the call.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 16 bytes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the saved registers (12) or only the return address (4). The return address is a push like any other and must be included in the frame size.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q46", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "What does the return instruction of a subroutine normally do?", "options": ["It pops the return address from the stack into the program counter and restores the stack pointer.", "It clears the stack so that the next call starts from an empty stack.", "It copies the return address from the program counter into the stack.", "It jumps to the address held in the stack pointer register."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A call and a return are inverse operations on the same stack: the call pushes the address of the following instruction, and the return pops it back into the program counter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The call pushed the address of the instruction after the call.</li><li>The return pops that word into the program counter, so execution resumes at the caller's next instruction.</li><li>The stack pointer is advanced past the popped word, so the frame is released and the caller's stack is restored.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the return address is read from the stack pointer register itself. The stack pointer holds the <i>location</i> of the return address; the value must be loaded from memory into the program counter.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q47", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A frame holds a 4-byte return address, a 4-byte saved frame pointer and three 4-byte local variables. The stack grows downwards and the caller's frame pointer is 9000. After the callee's prologue has allocated its frame, the callee's frame pointer is", "options": ["8960", "8980", "9000", "9020"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A frame's size is the sum of its slots. On a downward-growing stack, allocating a frame of n bytes moves the pointer n bytes below its previous value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frame size = 4 (return address) + 4 (saved frame pointer) + 3 &times; 4 (locals) = 4 + 4 + 12 = 20 bytes.</li><li>The stack grows downwards, so allocation subtracts: 9000 &minus; 20 = 8980.</li><li>The callee's frame pointer therefore sits at 8980, and the frame occupies 8980 up to 8999.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 8980.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding instead of subtracting (9020) by ignoring the direction of growth, or counting two locals instead of three and answering 9000 &minus; 16 = 8984, which is not among the options for exactly that reason.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q48", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about activation records and stack discipline are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An activation record holds the return address, saved registers, local variables and incoming parameters.", "Under the usual C calling convention the caller removes the arguments from the stack after the callee returns.", "A leaf procedure that uses no callee-saved registers and needs no local storage can run without allocating a frame.", "Every procedure call must allocate a new frame, even a leaf procedure with no locals and no saved registers."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The activation record is the per-call state that must survive across calls. Anything that does not need to survive, or that fits in registers the callee is allowed to destroy, needs no stack space.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The frame is exactly the set of values a call must preserve: return address, saved registers, locals and parameters. True.</li><li>(B) In the cdecl convention the caller adjusts the stack pointer to discard the arguments after the return. True.</li><li>(C) A leaf procedure that saves nothing and stores nothing can keep everything in caller-saved registers, so it needs no frame. True.</li><li>(D) This contradicts (C): allocating a frame is a consequence of needing to save state, not of calling. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a frame is mandatory. Many compilers emit leaf procedures with no prologue or epilogue at all, which is a real optimisation, not a theoretical footnote.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q49", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A recursive procedure's activation record consists of 4 bytes for the return address, 12 bytes for three saved registers and 16 bytes for local variables. The stack region available to it is 64 KB. Starting from an empty stack, what is the greatest depth of recursion that fits?", "options": ["1024", "2048", "4096", "8192"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recursion depth is limited by stack capacity divided by the per-activation frame size. The frame size is the sum of every slot the prologue allocates, not just the locals.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frame size = 4 + 12 + 16 = 32 bytes per activation.</li><li>Available stack = 64 KB = 64 &times; 1024 = 65,536 bytes.</li><li>Maximum depth = 65,536 &divide; 32 = 2,048 activations, exactly filling the region with no slack.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 2,048.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing 64 KB by 16, the size of the locals alone, and answering 4,096. The return address and the saved registers are part of every frame and double the per-call cost.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U1-Q50", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "instructions-addressing", "subtopic": "stack-and-subroutines", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about evaluating an expression on a stack machine are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Pushing an operand increases the number of items on the stack by one.", "Applying a binary operator pops two items and pushes one result.", "A well-formed postfix expression leaves exactly one item on the stack once it has been fully evaluated.", "A stack machine evaluates an infix expression correctly without any knowledge of operator precedence."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A stack machine is a zero-address machine: operands are implicit on the stack, so every push adds one item and every binary operator reduces the count by one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A push is exactly the operation that adds one item. True.</li><li>(B) A binary operator consumes its two operands and leaves their result, a net change of &minus;1. True.</li><li>(C) A postfix expression with n operands and n&minus;1 binary operators ends with n &minus; (n&minus;1) = 1 item. True.</li><li>(D) Infix evaluation still needs precedence rules and parentheses handling, which is why infix is converted to postfix first. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that using a stack removes the need for precedence. The stack removes the need to name operands, not the need to know which operator binds tighter.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q51", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In a hardwired control unit, each control signal is produced by", "options": ["a control store that is read at an address taken from the microprogram counter", "a fixed combinational network whose inputs are the opcode, the timing state and the condition flags", "a decoder that expands the control field of a microinstruction", "the arithmetic unit, which emits the control word together with the result"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A control unit is hardwired when its output signals come from a fixed network of gates rather than from stored control words. The network reads the instruction opcode, the timing (T-state) signals and the condition flags.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The instruction decoder turns the opcode into exactly one active instruction line.</li><li>The sequence counter and its decoder produce the timing lines T0, T1, &hellip; for the current cycle.</li><li>Each control signal is a Boolean function of those instruction lines, timing lines and flags, built once in gates.</li><li>Nothing is stored per instruction, so the unit is fast and rigid: a new instruction means new logic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; a fixed combinational network fed by the opcode, the timing state and the flags.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a PLA-based controller as microprogrammed. A PLA is still combinational, so it is hardwired control; microprogrammed control is defined by control words held in a store, not by the shape of the logic.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q52", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A hardwired control unit sequences 13 timing states, T0 through T12, with a binary sequence counter. How many of the counter’s encoded states are never used?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">k</code>-bit binary counter has <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup></code> encoded states, and it must cover the number of timing states the longest instruction needs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The counter must reach 13 states, so <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>k</sup> &ge; 13</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">2<sup>3</sup> = 8 &lt; 13</code>, so 3 bits are not enough; <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>4</sup> = 16 &ge; 13</code>, so 4 bits are used.</li><li>Unused states = 16 &minus; 13 = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 13 (the number of states needed) or 16 (the number the counter provides). The question asks for the difference. A one-hot ring counter would have no unused states, but the question specifies a binary counter.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q53", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A control signal must be asserted in timing states T2 and T5, and only while the current instruction is ADD. The decoded instruction line is ADD and the decoded timing lines are T0 … T7. Which expression implements the signal?", "options": ["ADD + T2 + T5", "ADD · (T2 + T5)", "(ADD + T2) · (ADD + T5)", "ADD · T2 · T5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A control signal fires when its instruction condition and its timing condition are both true, so the instruction line is ANDed with a timing term; several timing states for the same signal are ORed inside that term.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Instruction condition: the signal belongs to ADD only, so the factor <code class=\"mm\">ADD</code> is required.</li><li>Timing condition: the signal is high in T2 or T5, so the factor is <code class=\"mm\">(T2 + T5)</code>.</li><li>AND them: <code class=\"mm\">ADD &middot; (T2 + T5)</code>, which is 1 in T2 and in T5 of ADD and 0 elsewhere.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">ADD &middot; (T2 + T5)</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> ORing the instruction line with the timing terms, which asserts the signal in every state of ADD and in T2/T5 of every instruction. The other trap is ANDing the two timing lines, which is identically 0 because T2 and T5 are never high together.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q54", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A control unit is implemented as a PLA with 8 inputs, 5 product terms and 6 outputs, where every input has a programmable link in each product-term row and every output has a programmable link in each product-term column. Two of the product terms turn out to be identical and are merged into one. By how many programmable links does the PLA shrink?", "options": ["16", "22", "24", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a PLA every product term is one row of the AND plane and one column of the OR plane, so a term costs links in both planes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>AND plane: 8 inputs, each needing a link for its true form and a link for its complement, so 2 &times; 8 = 16 links per product term.</li><li>OR plane: the term may drive any of the 6 outputs, so 6 links per product term.</li><li>Cost of one product term = 16 + 6 = 22 links.</li><li>Merging two identical terms deletes exactly one term, so 22 links disappear.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 22 links.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the AND plane (16) or only the OR plane (6). A merged term frees links in both planes, and each input occupies two links because both polarities must be programmable.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q55", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about hardwired control are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A control signal that depends only on the opcode, and not on the timing state, needs no timing term in its Boolean expression.", "Two different instructions can legitimately share the same timing signal produced by the sequence counter.", "A hardwired control unit cannot test a condition flag, because branching requires stored microcode.", "Implementing the control functions in a PLA lets several control signals share the same product term, so the PLA is usually smaller than the same functions built from separate gates."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Hardwired control is a set of Boolean functions of the opcode lines, the timing lines and the flags; each function contains only the variables it actually depends on.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. If the signal is high throughout an instruction, its expression reduces to a function of the opcode alone.</li><li>(B) True. The timing decoder is shared by every instruction, so the same T-line drives many signals and many instructions.</li><li>(C) False. A flag enters the logic as an ordinary input, so a branch is implemented by gating the next-state or PC-load term with the flag. No microcode is involved.</li><li>(D) True. A PLA shares product terms across all its outputs, which a bank of separate AND/OR gates cannot do.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming conditional branching is exclusive to microprogrammed control. Both kinds of control unit branch; the difference is whether the control word comes from a store or from gates.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q56", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "hardwired-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A hardwired control unit is built as a finite-state machine with a 5-bit state register (24 of its codes are used), an 8-bit opcode input and 40 control outputs. The next-state and output functions are held in one ROM that is addressed by the state and the opcode and that stores the next state together with the 40 control bits. How many bits does the ROM hold?", "options": ["368 640", "327 680", "204 800", "184 320"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A ROM-based controller stores the whole next-state and output function as a table, so the ROM is addressed by the present state concatenated with the external input and each word holds the next state plus the control outputs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Address width = 5 state bits + 8 opcode bits = 13 bits, so the ROM has <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>13</sup> = 8192</code> words. The 24 used states do not shrink the ROM; every 13-bit address must be decoded.</li><li>Word width = 5 next-state bits + 40 control bits = 45 bits.</li><li>Total = 8192 &times; 45 = 368 640 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 368 640 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Sizing the ROM from the 24 states actually used (giving 1024 or 2048 words) or multiplying by the 40 control bits alone. The state register has 5 bits, so the address space is 2<sup>13</sup> whatever the number of reachable states, and the next-state field is part of every word.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q57", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In a microprogrammed control unit, the address of the next microinstruction is normally obtained by", "options": ["incrementing the control address register, unless a sequencing field or a branch condition supplies a different address", "using the opcode of the machine instruction directly as a control-store address", "reading the next machine instruction from main memory", "incrementing the machine program counter"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Microprogrammed control fetches control words from a control store. The control address register (microprogram counter) points at the current microinstruction and is normally incremented, with an explicit next-address field or a conditional branch overriding it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fetch the microinstruction whose address is in the control address register.</li><li>If the microinstruction carries an unconditional next address, load it.</li><li>If it carries a condition, choose between the branch address and the fall-through address using the selected flag.</li><li>Otherwise increment the register.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; increment, unless a sequencing field or a branch condition overrides it.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the machine opcode is used directly as a control-store address. A mapping ROM converts the opcode into a starting address, because the opcode is much narrower than the control-store address and its code values have no reason to sit where the microcode begins.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q58", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A control store holds 256 microinstructions. Each microinstruction has 24 control bits and a 6-bit next-address field, and nothing else. What is the total size of the control store in bits?", "options": [], "answer_display": "7680", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The control store is an array: one word per microinstruction, and the word width is the sum of the fields that make up one microinstruction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Word width = 24 control bits + 6 sequencing bits = 30 bits.</li><li>Number of words = 256.</li><li>Total size = 256 &times; 30 = 7680 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7680 bits</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting the word width (30 bits) or 256 &times; 24 = 6144 bits, which drops the next-address field. Every field of the microinstruction is stored in every word.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q59", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A microinstruction controls 24 signals. In the horizontal form each signal gets its own bit. In the encoded form the signals are grouped into 3 fields of 8 mutually exclusive signals each, and every field is encoded in binary. How many bits does the encoded microinstruction need for these 24 signals?", "options": ["9", "12", "21", "24"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Encoding trades width for decoding: a field that selects one of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> mutually exclusive signals needs <code class=\"mm\">&#8968;log<sub>2</sub> n&#8969;</code> bits, and a decoder turns those bits back into the individual control lines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>8 mutually exclusive signals need <code class=\"mm\">log<sub>2</sub> 8 = 3</code> bits.</li><li>Three such fields need 3 &times; 3 = 9 bits.</li><li>The horizontal form would need 24 bits, so the encoded form saves 15 bits at the cost of three decoders.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 9 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 24 (the horizontal size, which is what encoding is meant to avoid) or 12 (using 4 bits per field instead of 3).</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q60", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Machine A executes an instruction in one hardwired cycle of 20 ns. Machine B implements the same instruction as 5 microinstructions, each taking one 8 ns clock, and both machines run the same program. How much longer does B take per instruction?", "options": ["1.6 times", "2 times", "2.5 times", "5 times"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Time per instruction = number of cycles per instruction &times; cycle time, so a machine with a shorter cycle can still be slower if it needs more cycles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Machine A: 1 cycle &times; 20 ns = 20 ns per instruction.</li><li>Machine B: 5 cycles &times; 8 ns = 40 ns per instruction.</li><li>Ratio = 40 / 20 = 2, so B is twice as slow.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 2 times.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Comparing clock periods alone (8 ns against 20 ns makes B look faster) or comparing the microinstruction count with the instruction count. Cycles per instruction and cycle time must be multiplied, never compared separately.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q61", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about microprogrammed control are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A horizontal microinstruction is wider than a vertical one and needs less decoding.", "A microinstruction can branch to another microinstruction on the value of a machine condition flag.", "A vertical microinstruction needs a decoder between the control-store output and the control lines.", "The control store must be read-only, so a microprogram can never be changed after the machine is built."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Horizontal microcode spends one bit per control signal and almost no decoding; vertical microcode packs signals into encoded fields and pays with decoders. Either form can branch on a flag through a condition-select field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. One bit per signal means a wide word but the bits drive the control lines almost directly.</li><li>(B) True. The condition-select field chooses a flag and the sequencing logic then picks the branch address or the fall-through address.</li><li>(C) True. Encoded fields must be expanded by a decoder before they can drive individual control lines.</li><li>(D) False. A writable control store is a standard design: microcode can be loaded at boot or patched in the field.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the control store is always a ROM. ROM control stores are common in fixed designs, but writable control stores exist precisely so microcode can be changed.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q62", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "microprogrammed-control", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Two microprogrammed machines use a 1024-word control store addressed by a 10-bit next-address field. Machine H stores one 80-bit horizontal microinstruction per machine instruction. Machine V stores a 20-bit vertical control field, a 2-bit field that selects which group of control signals is decoded, and the same 10-bit next-address field. How many more bits does H’s control store hold than V’s?", "options": ["32 768", "49 152", "61 440", "81 920"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Control-store size = number of words &times; word width, and the word width includes the sequencing field, which both machines share.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Machine H: word = 80 bits, so 1024 &times; 80 = 81 920 bits.</li><li>Machine V: word = 20 + 2 + 10 = 32 bits, so 1024 &times; 32 = 32 768 bits.</li><li>Difference = 81 920 &minus; 32 768 = 49 152 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 49 152 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Comparing the control fields alone (80 against 20) and ignoring the shared 10-bit address field, or forgetting the 2-bit select field in V. Both machines store the same number of words, so the whole difference is in the word width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q63", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A datapath register file has 2 read ports and 1 write port. In one clock cycle this allows", "options": ["one source operand to be read and one result written, so a two-operand operation needs two cycles", "two source operands to be read and one result written, so an instruction such as R3 ← R1 + R2 can finish in one cycle if the ALU and the write path also fit in that cycle", "two results to be written into two different registers", "any number of operands to be read at the same time"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The ports of a register file set how many operands can be supplied and how many results stored in a single cycle; two read ports plus one write port is the minimum for a two-operand ALU instruction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Port 1 supplies R1, port 2 supplies R2; both values reach the ALU in the same cycle.</li><li>The ALU produces the sum inside that cycle.</li><li>The single write port stores the sum into R3.</li><li>The cycle time must still cover read, ALU, and write-setup delays; ports remove the serialisation, not the delay.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; two reads and one write in the same cycle.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming one read port forces two cycles for every ALU instruction (that is a single-bus datapath, not a port-limited register file), or thinking one write port limits a single result, which is exactly one write.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q64", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A single-bus CPU completes exactly one bus transfer per clock cycle. The ALU has two input registers, A and B, both loaded from the bus, and the ALU result returns to a register through the same bus. How many clock cycles does R4 ← R1 + R2 need?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> On a single-bus datapath one word crosses the bus per cycle, so every operand read and every result write costs a cycle; the ALU input registers hold the operands while the other one is fetched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cycle 1: R1 is placed on the bus and latched into ALU input register A.</li><li>Cycle 2: R2 is placed on the bus and latched into ALU input register B.</li><li>Cycle 3: the ALU output is driven onto the bus and written into R4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 cycles</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2 by counting only the two operand reads, or 4 by adding a separate write-back cycle. The ALU result travels on the same bus in the same kind of cycle, so it needs one cycle, not two.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q65", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "For signed two’s-complement addition in an n-bit ALU, the overflow flag is", "options": ["the carry out of the most significant bit", "the exclusive-OR of the carry into the most significant bit and the carry out of it", "the OR of all the result bits", "the most significant bit of the result"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Overflow means the signed result does not fit: two operands of the same sign produced a result of the opposite sign. In the adder this happens exactly when the carry into the sign position and the carry out of it disagree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let <code class=\"mm\">c<sub>n&minus;1</sub></code> be the carry into bit <code class=\"mm\">n&minus;1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c<sub>n</sub></code> the carry out of it.</li><li>If both are 0 or both are 1 the sign is correct; if they differ the sign has flipped spuriously.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">V = c<sub>n&minus;1</sub> &oplus; c<sub>n</sub></code>.</li><li>Equivalently, <code class=\"mm\">V = 1</code> when the two operand sign bits agree and the result sign differs from them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; the XOR of the carry into and the carry out of the sign position.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the carry out alone: that is the unsigned carry, and it is 1 for perfectly legal signed sums such as <code class=\"mm\">(&minus;1) + (&minus;1)</code>. The sign bit alone only says whether the result is negative.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q66", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A 32-bit adder is built from eight 4-bit carry-lookahead blocks. Inside a block the sum bits are ready 4 ns after the block’s carry-in, and the block produces its carry-out 3 ns after its carry-in. What is the worst-case delay from the carry-in of the first block to the last sum bit?", "options": ["21 ns", "24 ns", "25 ns", "32 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Block carry-lookahead removes the carry chain inside a block but keeps a chain between blocks, so the delay is the carry chain across the blocks plus the internal sum delay of the last block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Carry into block 1 arrives at t = 0; each block adds 3 ns before its carry-out is ready.</li><li>The carry reaches the input of block 8 after seven block delays: 7 &times; 3 = 21 ns.</li><li>The sum bits of block 8 are ready 4 ns after its carry-in: 21 + 4 = 25 ns.</li><li>The carry-out of block 8 (24 ns) is not on the path to the last sum bit, so it does not matter here.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 25 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting eight block delays instead of seven, which gives 28 ns by charging the last block's carry-out delay as well, or treating every block as a full ripple stage, which gives 8 &times; 4 = 32 ns.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q67", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about datapath timing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The minimum clock period of a register-to-register path is the clk-to-Q delay plus the longest combinational delay plus the setup time of the destination register.", "A multiplexer placed on the result path adds its delay to the critical path, so removing it can shorten the clock period.", "The zero flag can be produced without waiting for the adder, because it depends only on the two input operands.", "A three-bus datapath usually shortens the critical path compared with a single-bus datapath, because both operands reach the ALU in the same cycle."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A register-to-register path must be closed in one clock period: the source register launches data after clk-to-Q, the combinational logic settles, and the destination register must see a stable value for its setup time before the next edge.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. <code class=\"mm\">T &ge; t<sub>clk&minus;to&minus;Q</sub> + t<sub>comb</sub> + t<sub>setup</sub></code>, plus any clock skew.</li><li>(B) True. A multiplexer is combinational logic on the path, so its delay is part of <code class=\"mm\">t<sub>comb</sub></code>.</li><li>(C) False. The zero flag is a function of the result bits, so it can only be produced after the adder settles; a NOR/OR tree of the result adds another gate delay after the sum.</li><li>(D) True. With three buses both operands arrive in one cycle, so the two serial bus transfers of a single-bus datapath leave the critical path.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the zero flag is available early. It depends on the result, not on the operands, so it is strictly later than the sum and may itself become the critical path when the adder is fast and the flag tree is deep.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q68", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A CPU with a single internal bus spends 3 cycles on the fetch phase and 1 cycle on decode. After that, a register-register instruction needs 2 more cycles, a memory-reference instruction 4 more, and a branch 3 more. A program has 40% register-register, 50% memory-reference and 10% branch instructions. What is the average CPI?", "options": ["6.0", "7.0", "7.1", "7.5"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CPI is the weighted average of the cycles each instruction class takes, and every class pays the shared fetch and decode cycles first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Shared front end = 3 + 1 = 4 cycles.</li><li>Register-register: 4 + 2 = 6 cycles. Memory-reference: 4 + 4 = 8 cycles. Branch: 4 + 3 = 7 cycles.</li><li>CPI = 0.40 &times; 6 + 0.50 &times; 8 + 0.10 &times; 7 = 2.4 + 4.0 + 0.7 = 7.1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 7.1 cycles per instruction.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Averaging only the extra cycles (0.4&times;2 + 0.5&times;4 + 0.1&times;3 = 3.1) or taking the unweighted mean of 6, 8 and 7, which gives 7.0 and ignores the instruction mix.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q69", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "alu-datapath-timing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The longest register-to-register path in a datapath is 8.0 ns, with the destination register’s setup time already included. The clock has 0.5 ns of skew. What is the highest clock frequency the datapath can run at?", "options": ["100.0 MHz", "117.6 MHz", "125.0 MHz", "133.3 MHz"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The clock period must cover the longest register-to-register delay and the skew between the launching and capturing edges; frequency is the reciprocal of the period.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Path delay = 8.0 ns, with setup already counted.</li><li>Add skew: <code class=\"mm\">T = 8.0 + 0.5 = 8.5</code> ns.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f = 1 / 8.5 ns = 117.647&hellip; MHz &asymp; 117.6 MHz</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 117.6 MHz.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 1 / 8.0 = 125 MHz by ignoring skew, or adding the setup time a second time although the question says it is already inside the 8.0 ns.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q70", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about bus organisation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A bus master is the device that initiates a transfer by driving the address lines.", "The address lines are normally driven in one direction only, from the master to the slaves, while the data lines are bidirectional.", "Wait states lengthen a bus cycle when the addressed slave cannot respond within the time the protocol allows.", "Doubling the data-bus width always doubles the number of bytes transferred per second, whatever the protocol overhead."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A bus is a shared set of wires with a master that initiates and slaves that respond; the address and control lines are driven by the master, the data lines carry information in both directions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Arbitration decides which device becomes master, and the master then drives address and control.</li><li>(B) True. A slave never needs to drive the address lines, so they are unidirectional; data must flow both ways.</li><li>(C) True. A wait state is exactly the mechanism by which a slow slave stretches the cycle.</li><li>(D) False. Address phases, arbitration and turnaround cycles are fixed overheads, so doubling the width improves throughput but does not double it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming bandwidth scales linearly with width. Useful bandwidth is bytes per transfer divided by the whole transfer time, and the whole transfer time includes phases that carry no data.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q71", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "On a synchronous bus, a device that inserts wait states is", "options": ["the bus master, which always knows how fast the slave is", "the addressed slave, when it cannot complete the transfer within the cycles the protocol allows", "the clock generator, which stretches the clock for the whole bus", "the arbiter, which grants the bus to a master"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A synchronous bus fixes how many cycles a transfer may take. A slave that needs longer asserts a ready/wait line, and the master holds address and data until the slave releases it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The master starts the transfer and expects data within a fixed number of cycles.</li><li>The slave cannot meet that time, so it de-asserts ready (or asserts wait) for one or more cycles.</li><li>The master stretches the data phase accordingly; the clock itself never changes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; the addressed slave.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the clock generator stretches the clock. On a synchronous bus the clock is fixed for everyone; only the number of cycles in the transfer changes, which is why a slow device does not slow down unrelated transfers.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q72", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A synchronous bus runs at 200 MHz. A burst read uses one address cycle, then eight data cycles, and the slave inserts one wait state in total. How long does the whole burst take, in nanoseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "50", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every phase of a synchronous transfer costs a whole number of clock cycles, so the time is the total cycle count multiplied by the clock period.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Clock period = 1 / 200 MHz = 5 ns.</li><li>Cycles = 1 address + 8 data + 1 wait = 10.</li><li>Time = 10 &times; 5 ns = 50 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 50 ns</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 45 ns by forgetting the wait state, or 40 ns by forgetting the address cycle. The address phase carries no data but still costs a full clock period.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q73", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In daisy-chain bus arbitration, the device nearest the arbiter", "options": ["has the lowest priority, because its request must pass through every other device", "has the highest priority, and a device near the arbiter that keeps requesting can starve the devices farther down the chain", "has the same priority as every other device, because the grant is rotated", "cannot become a master at all, because the grant line ends at it"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Daisy-chain arbitration passes a grant signal physically from device to device; a device that wants the bus takes the grant and does not pass it on, so position in the chain is priority.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A device raises its request line, which is wired-OR to the arbiter.</li><li>The arbiter issues one grant, which travels through the devices in order.</li><li>The first requesting device on the path absorbs the grant and keeps it for the transfer.</li><li>Devices farther down therefore wait for every nearer device, which is why a busy near device can starve them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; highest priority, with possible starvation of the far devices.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing daisy-chain arbitration with round-robin. Rotating the grant is a different scheme; a plain daisy chain has a fixed priority order decided by wiring.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q74", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A synchronous bus has a 128-bit data path and a 100 MHz clock. Every transfer is one address cycle followed by a four-word burst with one word moved per cycle and no wait states. What is the effective bandwidth in MB/s? (Take 1 MB = 106 bytes.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1280", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Effective bandwidth is the useful data moved divided by the total time of the transfer, and the total time includes the address phase that carries no data.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>128-bit data path = 16 bytes per word.</li><li>Burst = 4 words &times; 16 B = 64 bytes.</li><li>Cycles = 1 address + 4 data = 5; period = 1 / 100 MHz = 10 ns; time = 50 ns.</li><li>Bandwidth = 64 B / 50 ns = 1.28 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> B/s = 1280 MB/s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1280 MB/s</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1600 MB/s, which is 16 B &times; 100 MHz and ignores the address cycle, or 1280 misread as 1.28 GB/s and then converted with 1024.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U2-Q75", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "datapath-control-unit", "subtopic": "bus-architectures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about bus protocols and arbitration are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A split-transaction bus releases the bus between a request and its response, so other masters can use it while a slow slave works.", "Doubling the clock frequency of a synchronous bus always doubles its useful bandwidth, even when the memory cannot keep up.", "Fixed-priority arbitration can starve a low-priority master indefinitely, whereas round-robin arbitration bounds how long it waits.", "A bus bridge lets two buses of different widths and different clock rates exchange data by buffering and re-timing the transfers."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bus protocols trade bus occupancy against complexity, and arbitration policy decides who waits; a bridge hides differences between two buses at the cost of buffering.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Splitting request from response frees the wires during the slave's latency, which is what makes a split-transaction bus faster under load.</li><li>(B) False. If the memory cannot supply data in the shorter cycle, wait states appear and the useful bandwidth does not double.</li><li>(C) True. A fixed priority order lets a high-priority master win every time; round-robin rotates the grant so every master is served within a bounded number of grants.</li><li>(D) True. A bridge contains buffers and its own bus interface on each side, so width and frequency can differ.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a faster clock always means more throughput. Protocol overhead and device speed, not the clock alone, set the useful bandwidth, which is why wait states exist.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q51", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A byte-addressable processor issues 32-bit addresses to a 16 KB direct-mapped cache whose block size is 64 bytes. How many bits of the address select the cache line?", "options": ["6", "8", "10", "14"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a direct-mapped cache each block has exactly one line it can occupy, so the address is split into tag | line index | block offset, and the index must be wide enough to name every line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The number of lines is cache size / block size = 16384 / 64 = 256.</li><li>The index field must distinguish 256 lines, so it needs log&#8322; 256 = 8 bits.</li><li>The block offset needs log&#8322; 64 = 6 bits, and the remaining 32 &minus; 8 &minus; 6 = 18 bits form the tag.</li><li>So 8 bits select the line.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 bits &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 6 is the block offset, the bits that choose a byte inside a block; 14 is log&#8322; of the cache size in bytes; 10 is the index width of a 1 KB direct-mapped cache. The index width depends on the number of lines, never on the number of bytes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q52", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A 32 KB cache is 4-way set associative with 64-byte blocks and 32-bit byte addresses. How many tag bits does each cache line store?", "options": null, "answer_display": "19", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The tag is whatever is left of the address after the index and the block offset: tag = address width &minus; index bits &minus; offset bits. The index names a set, and a set holds one line per way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of sets = cache size / (block size &times; associativity) = 32768 / (64 &times; 4) = 128 sets.</li><li>Index bits = log&#8322; 128 = 7.</li><li>Block offset bits = log&#8322; 64 = 6.</li><li>Tag bits = 32 &minus; 7 &minus; 6 = 19.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 19 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing only by the block size gives 512 sets, 9 index bits and 17 tag bits; that is the arithmetic of a direct-mapped cache. The associativity divides the number of sets, not the number of lines.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q53", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A 256 KB cache is 8-way set associative with 64-byte blocks, and the physical address is 40 bits wide. Which split into (tag, index, block offset) is correct?", "options": ["(22, 12, 6)", "(25, 6, 9)", "(28, 6, 6)", "(25, 9, 6)"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Set-associative mapping divides the cache into sets of <code class=\"mm\">ways</code> lines. The index selects the set, the offset selects the byte inside the block, and the tag holds the rest of the address.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of sets = 262144 / (64 &times; 8) = 512, so the index needs 9 bits.</li><li>The block offset needs log&#8322; 64 = 6 bits.</li><li>The tag takes the remaining 40 &minus; 9 &minus; 6 = 25 bits.</li><li>The split is therefore (25, 9, 6).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (25, 9, 6) &mdash; option D.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> (22, 12, 6) is the direct-mapped split, 4096 lines giving 12 index bits. (25, 6, 9) swaps the index and the offset. (28, 6, 6) uses 64 sets, which forgets the eight ways.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q54", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about where a block may be placed in a cache are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In a direct-mapped cache a block can occupy exactly one line, so no search among ways is needed after the tag comparison", "Raising the associativity reduces conflict misses but can lengthen the hit time, because a multiplexer must pick among the ways after every tag has been compared", "A fully associative cache has no index field, so every line's tag is compared with the address in parallel", "In a set-associative cache the block offset bits are stored with the line as part of its tag"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Placement is a trade-off between the freedom to choose a line and the cost of finding the block. Direct mapping fixes the line, full associativity frees it entirely, and set associativity sits between the two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. With one candidate line there is nothing to search: the index computes the line and one comparator decides hit or miss.</li><li>(B) True. The ways must be searched in parallel and their results selected, and that selection sits on the hit path.</li><li>(C) True. With one set the whole cache is the set, so every tag is compared simultaneously and no address bits are spent on an index.</li><li>(D) False. The offset is not stored: it is the same for the block and for the line, so only the tag needs to be kept for the comparison.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) sounds harmless but doubles the stored tag for nothing. The line does not need to know where inside the block the byte is; that is computed from the address at access time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q55", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A 64 KB two-way set-associative cache has 32-byte blocks and 32-bit byte addresses. Besides the data, every line stores its tag, a valid bit and a dirty bit. What is the total number of bits of tag and status storage in the cache?", "options": ["34 816", "36 864", "38 912", "40 960"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Tag and status storage is counted per line, not per set: a two-way set holds two independent lines, each with its own tag, valid bit and dirty bit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of sets = 65536 / (32 &times; 2) = 1024, so the index is 10 bits and the offset is 5 bits.</li><li>Tag bits per line = 32 &minus; 10 &minus; 5 = 17.</li><li>Each line therefore needs 17 tag bits + 1 valid bit + 1 dirty bit = 19 bits.</li><li>Number of lines = 1024 sets &times; 2 ways = 2048, so the total is 2048 &times; 19 = 38 912 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 38 912 bits &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting 1024 lines instead of 2048 halves the answer. 36 864 is 2048 &times; 18, which forgets one of the two status bits, and 40 960 is 2048 &times; 20, one bit too many.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q56", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "direct-associative-setassociative-mapping", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A 64 KB cache with 128-byte blocks is 4-way set associative. How many sets does it contain?", "options": ["64", "256", "128", "512"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A set holds one line per way, so the number of sets is the number of lines divided by the associativity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Number of lines = 65536 / 128 = 512.</li><li>Number of sets = 512 / 4 = 128.</li><li>Equivalently, sets = 65536 / (128 &times; 4) = 128.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 128 sets &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 512 is the number of lines, which is the answer to a different question. Forgetting the four ways and calling 512 the number of sets makes the index two bits too wide.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q57", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "For a single-level cache, the average memory access time is", "options": ["(hit time + miss penalty) / miss rate", "hit time &times; miss rate + miss penalty", "hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty", "hit time + miss penalty"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every access pays the hit time, and a fraction of the accesses equal to the miss rate also pays the extra cost of going to the next level, the miss penalty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every access costs the hit time, so that term is never multiplied by a fraction.</li><li>A fraction equal to the miss rate pays the miss penalty as well.</li><li>Adding the two contributions gives AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty.</li><li>Dividing by the miss rate or adding the full penalty to every access overstates the cost.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A divides instead of weighting, and option D charges the miss penalty to every access. Only the misses pay the penalty.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q58", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A cache has a hit time of 2 ns, a hit rate of 95% and a miss penalty of 40 ns. What is the average memory access time, in nanoseconds?", "options": null, "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT = hit time + miss rate &times; miss penalty. The hit rate must be converted to a miss rate before it is used.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss rate = 1 &minus; 0.95 = 0.05.</li><li>Average penalty = 0.05 &times; 40 ns = 2 ns.</li><li>AMAT = 2 ns + 2 ns = 4 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 0.95 as the miss rate gives 2 + 38 = 40 ns, which charges the penalty to the hits instead of the misses.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q59", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "An L1 cache has a hit time of 1 ns and a local miss rate of 5%. The L2 cache behind it has a hit time of 8 ns and a local miss rate of 20%. A miss in L2 costs a further 60 ns in main memory. What is the average memory access time?", "options": ["1.6 ns", "2.0 ns", "4.4 ns", "9.0 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With two levels, the L1 miss penalty is the L2 hit time plus the L2 miss rate times the memory cost; that whole penalty is then weighted by the L1 miss rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>L2 miss penalty = 8 ns + 0.20 &times; 60 ns = 8 + 12 = 20 ns.</li><li>L1 miss penalty = 20 ns, weighted by the L1 miss rate: 0.05 &times; 20 = 1 ns.</li><li>AMAT = 1 ns + 1 ns = 2.0 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.0 ns &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 1.6 ns comes from multiplying the two miss rates into a 1% global miss rate and then charging the full 60 ns. 4.4 ns forgets to weight the L2 miss rate by the L1 miss rate, and 9.0 ns simply adds the two hit times to the L1 hit time.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q60", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about cache performance are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Halving the miss rate halves the average memory access time", "A higher L2 hit rate reduces the average miss penalty that the L1 cache sees", "Doubling the block size may lower the compulsory miss rate but can raise the miss penalty, because every miss now transfers twice as many bytes", "If the clock frequency is doubled while every memory latency in nanoseconds stays the same, the miss penalty measured in cycles stays the same"], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AMAT has two terms, a hit-time term that every access pays and a miss term that only misses pay. Changing the miss behaviour cannot shrink the hit-time term, and a latency in nanoseconds becomes more cycles when the clock is faster.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) False. Only the miss term halves; the hit-time term is unchanged, so AMAT falls by less than half.</li><li>(B) True. A higher L2 hit rate means fewer L1 misses fall through to memory, so the average penalty per L1 miss is smaller.</li><li>(C) True. A larger block brings in neighbours that may be used, but each miss moves more data and so costs more time.</li><li>(D) False. The same nanosecond latency spans twice as many cycles at twice the frequency, so the penalty in cycles doubles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (A) is the usual trap: the hit time is paid on every single access, so it survives any improvement in the miss rate. Statement (D) confuses a time with a count of cycles.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q61", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A 2 GHz processor has a base CPI of 1.0 when every access hits in the cache. 30% of instructions are loads or stores, the miss rate per memory access is 4%, and the miss penalty is 50 ns. What is the effective CPI?", "options": ["1.12", "1.6", "2.2", "3.0"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Memory stalls per instruction = (fraction of instructions that access memory) &times; (miss rate per access) &times; (miss penalty in cycles). The penalty must be converted from nanoseconds to cycles before it is used.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Miss penalty in cycles = 50 ns &times; 2 GHz = 100 cycles.</li><li>Memory instructions per instruction = 0.30.</li><li>Stall cycles per instruction = 0.30 &times; 0.04 &times; 100 = 1.2.</li><li>Effective CPI = 1.0 + 1.2 = 2.2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.2 &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 1.6 comes from multiplying 0.30 &times; 0.04 &times; 50 without converting nanoseconds to cycles. 3.0 comes from applying the 4% miss rate to all instructions instead of only the memory instructions.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q62", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "cache-hit-miss-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "An L1 cache has a hit time of 1 cycle and a miss rate of 8%. The L2 cache behind it has a hit time of 10 cycles and a local miss rate of 20%; a miss in L2 costs a further 200 cycles. What is the average memory access time, in cycles?", "options": null, "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each level contributes its own hit time weighted by the fraction of accesses that reach it. The L1 miss penalty is the L2 hit time plus the L2 miss rate times the memory cost.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>L2 miss penalty = 10 + 0.20 &times; 200 = 10 + 40 = 50 cycles.</li><li>Every access reaches L1, so the L1 term is 1 cycle.</li><li>8% of accesses miss in L1 and pay the 50-cycle penalty: 0.08 &times; 50 = 4 cycles.</li><li>AMAT = 1 + 4 = 5 cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 cycles.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding 10 and 200 and then weighting by 8% gives 17.8, which treats every L2 access as a miss. Using the two miss rates as 8% and 20% of all accesses without nesting them also overstates the answer.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q63", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A two-way set holds block 12, last used 40 accesses ago, and block 31, last used 3 accesses ago. A new block maps to this set and the cache replaces by LRU. Which line is replaced?", "options": ["Block 31, because it is the more recently used of the two", "Block 12, because it was used least recently", "Whichever block has the smaller address", "Neither; the request waits until a line becomes free"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Least recently used means the block whose most recent reference is farthest in the past. It is about recency of use, not about size, address or arrival order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The last use of block 12 was 40 accesses ago and of block 31 was 3 accesses ago.</li><li>40 accesses is further in the past than 3, so block 12 is the least recently used.</li><li>LRU evicts block 12 and installs the new block in its place.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Block 12 &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Address order and arrival order are not recency. Block 12 may well have arrived later than block 31 and still be the one to go, because it has not been touched for longer.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q64", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A three-way set-associative cache replaces blocks by LRU. Within one set, the blocks referenced are 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. How many of the twelve references miss?", "options": null, "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Simulating LRU needs the set contents after every reference plus the recency order. A reference misses when the block is absent, and every miss also changes the recency order of the block that is installed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1, 2, 3 fill the set: three misses, order 1 2 3.</li><li>4 misses and evicts 1 (least recent): order 2 3 4.</li><li>1 misses and evicts 2: order 3 4 1. 2 misses and evicts 3: order 4 1 2.</li><li>5 misses and evicts 4: order 1 2 5.</li><li>1 hits (order 2 5 1). 2 hits (order 5 1 2).</li><li>3 misses and evicts 5: order 1 2 3. 4 misses and evicts 1: order 2 3 4. 5 misses and evicts 2: order 3 4 5.</li><li>Misses: 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 3, 4, 5 = 10; hits: the second 1 and the second 2 only.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 misses.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 9 is the FIFO count for the same string, so a simulation that quietly uses a FIFO queue looks right. LRU evicts by recency, so a block that was just re-referenced is protected even if it arrived long ago.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q65", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which statement about Belady's anomaly is correct?", "options": ["LRU can show it and FIFO cannot", "Both FIFO and LRU can show it", "FIFO can show it, so a larger cache sometimes produces more misses, while LRU cannot", "Neither policy can show it; more lines never means more misses"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A policy is a stack algorithm when the set of blocks it holds with n lines is always contained in the set it holds with n + 1 lines. LRU and the optimal policy are stack algorithms; FIFO is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Under LRU the resident blocks with n lines are exactly the n most recently used blocks, which is a subset of the n + 1 most recently used blocks.</li><li>A subset relation means every hit with n lines is also a hit with n + 1 lines, so misses can never increase.</li><li>FIFO keeps the n oldest arrivals, and the n oldest arrivals are not necessarily a subset of the n + 1 oldest arrivals.</li><li>So FIFO can produce more misses with more lines; the classic string 1 2 3 4 1 2 5 1 2 3 4 5 gives 9 misses with three lines and 10 with four.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: FIFO can show it and LRU cannot &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Belady's anomaly is a property of FIFO, not of caches in general, and it is not a claim that a larger cache is useless. Under LRU and under the optimal policy more capacity is always at least as good.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q66", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about replacement policies are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["LRU is a stack algorithm, so the blocks it holds with n ways are a subset of those it holds with n + 1 ways, and a larger set never produces more misses", "The optimal policy needs knowledge of future references, so it is used as a lower bound against which real policies are measured", "Random replacement guarantees that a block is never evicted while it is still being reused", "In a direct-mapped cache the replacement policy is irrelevant, because each block can occupy only one line"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A replacement policy decides which resident block gives up its line. Its properties matter only when a set has more than one candidate line, and its quality is judged against the optimal policy and against the stack property.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The n most recently used blocks are a subset of the n + 1 most recently used blocks, so extra capacity can only help.</li><li>(B) True. The optimal policy evicts the block whose next use is farthest away, which requires the future string; it is a benchmark, not an implementation.</li><li>(C) False. Random replacement can evict a block that is about to be reused, and it makes no promise at all about reuse.</li><li>(D) True. With one candidate line there is no choice to make, so no policy can change the outcome.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (C) is the trap: randomness gives no guarantee, though it is cheap and avoids pathological patterns. Statement (D) is often marked false by candidates who assume every cache needs a policy.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q67", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A three-way set-associative cache replaces blocks by FIFO. Within one set, the blocks referenced are 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. How many of the twelve references hit?", "options": ["2", "3", "5", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FIFO evicts the block that arrived earliest, whatever its recent use. A hit does not change the arrival order, which is exactly where FIFO differs from LRU.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1, 2, 3 fill the set: three misses, queue 1 2 3.</li><li>4 misses and evicts 1: queue 2 3 4. 1 misses and evicts 2: queue 3 4 1.</li><li>2 misses and evicts 3: queue 4 1 2. 5 misses and evicts 4: queue 1 2 5.</li><li>1 hits and 2 hits; a hit leaves the queue unchanged, so it is still 1 2 5.</li><li>3 misses and evicts 1: queue 2 5 3. 4 misses and evicts 2: queue 5 3 4. 5 then hits, because it is still resident.</li><li>That gives 9 misses (1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 3, 4) and so 12 &minus; 9 = 3 hits (1, 2, 5).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 hits &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 2 is the LRU hit count for the same string, which is the commonest slip: under LRU the just-referenced blocks are protected, under FIFO they are not. 9 is the miss count.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q68", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "replacement-algorithms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A three-way set-associative cache is compared with the optimal policy on the reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 6. By how many misses does LRU exceed the optimal policy?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The optimal policy evicts the block whose next use is farthest in the future. Its miss count includes every compulsory miss, so it is a lower bound, and the gap to LRU measures how much avoidable traffic a real policy creates.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Simulating LRU over the twenty references with three ways gives 15 misses.</li><li>Simulating the optimal policy over the same string gives 11 misses; both policies take the same first-touch misses, of which there are seven distinct blocks.</li><li>The difference is 15 &minus; 11 = 4 misses.</li><li>The four extra misses are the ones LRU creates by evicting a block that is needed again before the block it kept.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 misses &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 takes the seven compulsory misses for the six blocks after the first one, or subtracts a hit count from a miss count. The optimal policy also pays every compulsory miss; only the difference between the two policies is avoidable traffic.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q69", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "When a write hits in a write-through cache,", "options": ["only the cache copy is updated and a dirty bit is set", "the cache and main memory are both updated at once, so memory never holds a stale copy and no dirty bit is needed", "the line is invalidated and memory is updated later", "the write goes to memory and the line is removed from the cache"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A write-through policy keeps the memory copy current on every write. A write-back policy updates only the cache and defers the memory write until the line is evicted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A write hit changes the cached block.</li><li>Write-through also sends the word to the next level immediately, so the two copies agree at all times.</li><li>Because memory is never stale, a line can be dropped without writing it back, and no dirty bit is required.</li><li>The dirty bit belongs to the write-back policy, where it records that the memory copy is out of date.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Both copies are updated at once &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option A is the write-back behaviour. The dirty bit exists precisely because a write-back cache may hold a value that memory does not have.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q70", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about write policies are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A write-through cache can discard a block on eviction without writing it back, because memory already holds the current value", "A write-back cache sends a block to memory only when the block is dirty and about to be evicted, so a block rewritten many times is sent once", "A write-back cache needs a dirty bit for every line", "A write-through cache always generates less memory traffic than a write-back cache"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two policies differ in when memory is brought up to date: immediately on every write, or lazily when a modified line leaves the cache. The dirty bit is the record that makes the lazy policy safe.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Every write has already reached memory, so eviction needs no write-back.</li><li>(B) True. Repeated writes to one resident block collapse into a single memory write at eviction.</li><li>(C) True. Without a dirty bit the cache could not tell which lines must be written back.</li><li>(D) False. Write-through sends every store to memory, so a workload that rewrites the same block many times produces far more traffic than write-back.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) reverses the trade-off: write-back saves traffic on rewritten blocks but costs a write-back on eviction, and it complicates coherence because memory can be stale.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q71", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A write-through cache with no write-allocate handles 2 &times; 10&#8310; stores of 8 bytes each, and every store is passed on to main memory. How many megabytes does it write to main memory? (Take 1 MB = 10&#8310; bytes.)", "options": null, "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With write-through and no write-allocate, each store produces exactly one memory write of the stored size; there is no block fetch and no write-back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bytes per store = 8.</li><li>Number of stores = 2 &times; 10&#8310;.</li><li>Total bytes written = 2 &times; 10&#8310; &times; 8 = 16 &times; 10&#8310; bytes.</li><li>Dividing by 10&#8310; bytes per MB gives 16 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 MB.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by the block size instead of the store size, or counting a block fetch per miss as no-write-allocate would not, inflates the answer. With no write-allocate nothing is fetched.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q72", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A write miss occurs in a write-back cache. Which policy is normally used, and why?", "options": ["No-write-allocate: send the word straight to memory and leave the cache untouched, because fetching the block wastes bandwidth", "Write through the miss and mark the line valid without fetching the rest of the block", "Bypass the cache for every write, whatever the access pattern", "Write-allocate: fetch the block into the cache and write into it, so later writes to the same block hit and the dirty line goes to memory once at eviction"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-allocate and write-back go together. The block is fetched so that the writes that follow it land in the cache, and the deferred write-back then absorbs them all in one memory transfer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A write miss with write-allocate first fetches the block from the next level.</li><li>The word is then written into the cached copy and the dirty bit is set.</li><li>Subsequent writes to the same block hit, so no further memory traffic occurs.</li><li>At eviction one write-back carries the whole block, which is why write-allocate pays off with write-back.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Write-allocate &mdash; option D.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> No-write-allocate is the natural partner of write-through, not write-back. Marking a line valid without fetching it, as option B suggests, would leave the rest of the block holding stale data.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q73", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A cache line's dirty bit is set when", "options": ["the line has not been read since it was loaded", "the line is invalid and holds no useful data", "the line has been written since it was brought in, so its copy in memory is stale and it must be written back before the line is reused", "the line's tag matches more than one block"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The dirty bit records a difference between the cached copy and the memory copy. It is meaningful only in a write-back cache, where a write updates the cache alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On a fetch the line is clean: the cache and memory hold the same data.</li><li>A write to the line changes only the cached copy, so the two copies now differ.</li><li>The dirty bit is set to record that difference.</li><li>On eviction a dirty line is written back, and a clean line is simply overwritten.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: The line has been written since it was loaded &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The dirty bit says nothing about validity or about reads. A valid bit answers whether the line holds a real block; the dirty bit answers whether memory is out of date.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q74", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A program performs 2 &times; 10&#8310; stores of 4 bytes, spread over 125 000 distinct 32-byte blocks, each of which is written 16 times. A write-back cache writes a block to memory once, when it is evicted; a write-through cache with no write-allocate sends every store to memory. By what factor is the write-back traffic smaller?", "options": ["4", "2", "8", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-back traffic is counted in whole blocks written once, while write-through traffic is counted store by store. The comparison depends on how many stores fall inside one block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write-through traffic = 2 &times; 10&#8310; stores &times; 4 bytes = 8 &times; 10&#8310; bytes = 8 MB.</li><li>Write-back traffic = 125 000 blocks &times; 32 bytes = 4 &times; 10&#8310; bytes = 4 MB.</li><li>The ratio is 8 / 4 = 2, so write-back sends half as much.</li><li>The saving exists because each 32-byte block absorbs 64 bytes of stores before it is evicted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A factor of 2 &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 16 is the number of stores per block, not the ratio of traffic, and it ignores that a write-back moves the full 32-byte block. Write-back loses when a block is written only a few bytes before eviction.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U3-Q75", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "cache-memory", "subtopic": "write-policies-write-through-back", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about write buffers and write policies are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A write buffer lets a write-through cache complete the store in the cache and continue before memory has accepted the data", "A write-back cache sends the block to memory on every write hit", "Write-allocate is used with write-back caches because the fetched block usually absorbs the later writes to the same block", "Without a write buffer, every store in a write-through cache would stall the processor until memory responded"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A write-through policy needs a small buffer to hide memory latency, while a write-back policy hides that latency behind the residency of the line. Both are about where the store waits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The buffer holds the store while the processor continues, and memory drains it in the background.</li><li>(B) False. A write hit in a write-back cache changes only the cached copy and sets the dirty bit; memory is written at eviction.</li><li>(C) True. Fetching the block on a write miss makes the following writes to that block hit, and the single write-back then carries them all.</li><li>(D) True. Write-through sends every store to memory, so without buffering the processor would wait for memory on each one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (B) describes write-through, not write-back. A write buffer also has a limit: when it fills, the processor stalls, which is why a burst of stores can still expose memory latency.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q51", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "In a DRAM chip the row address and the column address are driven onto the same set of pins, the row being latched by <b>RAS</b> and the column by <b>CAS</b>. The main reason for sending the two halves of the address this way is that it", "options": ["lets the chip store two bits per cell, one per address phase.", "halves the number of address pins a given capacity needs, which keeps the package small and cheap.", "removes the need for periodic refresh of the cell array.", "makes a DRAM access as fast as an SRAM access."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DRAM array is addressed by a row number and a column number. The two are driven on the same pins in two phases, the row latched by RAS and the column by CAS, so the pin count is a design variable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An array of 2<sup>r</sup> rows and 2<sup>c</sup> columns needs r + c address bits in total.</li><li>Sending them in two phases needs only max(r, c) pins, not r + c. For the common 13-row, 11-column chip that is 13 pins instead of 24.</li><li>Fewer pins means a smaller, cheaper package and a smaller board, at the cost of the RAS-then-CAS sequencing delay. That trade is the whole point of the multiplexing.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the row and column addresses share pins to halve the address-pin count.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A DRAM cell stores one bit, as charge on a capacitor; the two address phases select one cell, not two.</li><li>(C) Refresh is forced by capacitor leakage and is needed whatever the pin arrangement.</li><li>(D) SRAM stays faster: a six-transistor bistable latch versus charge sensing on a capacitor.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the multiplexing as a speed trick. It is a pin-count (cost) trick: it actually adds the precharge-and-activate sequencing delay.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q52", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A DRAM array keeps the last row it touched in an internal row buffer. An access whose row is already open takes 20 ns; an access to a closed row takes 60 ns, because the row must be precharged and then activated. In a trace of 1000 accesses, 900 of them find their row already open. What is the average access time?", "options": ["20 ns", "24 ns", "26 ns", "56 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A row-buffer hit costs only the column access; a row-buffer miss adds precharge and activate. The average is the weighted mean of the two costs, weighted by how often each occurs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit fraction = 900 / 1000 = 0.9, miss fraction = 0.1.</li><li>Average = 0.9 &times; 20 + 0.1 &times; 60.</li><li>0.9 &times; 20 = 18 ns and 0.1 &times; 60 = 6 ns.</li><li>18 + 6 = 24 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 24 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 20 ns is the all-hit cost, which the 10% of closed-row accesses cannot reach.</li><li>(C) 26 ns comes from 0.9 &times; 20 + 0.1 &times; 80, using a wrong miss cost.</li><li>(D) 56 ns is the reverse weighting, 0.1 &times; 20 + 0.9 &times; 60.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Averaging the two times without weighting, which gives 40 ns, or attaching the 0.9 weight to the 60 ns cost, which gives 56 ns.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q53", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A memory is built from several identical banks, each able to serve one access at a time. Which statement about <b>high-order</b> interleaving is TRUE?", "options": ["Consecutive addresses are spread across different banks, so a sequential scan overlaps accesses in several banks.", "Consecutive addresses stay in the same bank, so a sequential scan gains no overlap between banks and only that bank's cycle time limits it.", "It eliminates bank conflicts, because each bank decodes its own addresses.", "It requires the memory bus to be wider than one word."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Interleaving decides which address bits choose the bank. Low-order interleaving uses the low bits, so consecutive addresses land in consecutive banks; high-order interleaving uses the high bits, so consecutive addresses land in the same bank.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>High-order interleaving partitions the address space into one contiguous block per bank.</li><li>A sequential scan therefore stays inside one block, that is inside one bank, for a long time.</li><li>The other banks sit idle during the scan, so there is no pipelining across banks and the scan runs at the single-bank cycle time.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — consecutive addresses stay in one bank, so a scan gets no inter-bank overlap.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) That is low-order interleaving.</li><li>(C) High-order interleaving does not remove conflicts; it concentrates them, since one bank serves a whole contiguous block.</li><li>(D) Bus width is independent of the interleaving order.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the two schemes: the description in option (A) is exactly low-order interleaving.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q54", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A memory has 8 banks with low-order interleaving. Each bank delivers one word per access and its cycle time is 40 ns. The memory bus can transfer one word every 10 ns. For a long stream of consecutive words, how many words does the memory deliver in each 40 ns interval?", "options": ["1", "2", "4", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With low-order interleaving the throughput is set by whichever is slower: the banks (k words per cycle time) or the bus (one word per transfer time). Take the minimum.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bank side: 8 banks, one word each, in 40 ns, so the banks can supply 8 words per 40 ns.</li><li>Bus side: one word every 10 ns, so the bus can carry 40 / 10 = 4 words per 40 ns.</li><li>The bus is the slower of the two, so it is the bottleneck: 4 words per 40 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 words per 40 ns — the bus, not the banks, is the bottleneck.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 1 word per 40 ns ignores the eight banks entirely.</li><li>(B) 2 words per 40 ns matches neither the bus nor the banks.</li><li>(D) 8 words per 40 ns is the bank capability, which the 10 ns bus cannot carry.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 8 from the bank count alone, or 1 from the cycle time alone. Interleaving only helps until the bus saturates.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q55", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A machine has 32-bit logical addresses and a page size of 8 KB. It uses a single-level page table. How many entries does the page table have?", "options": ["2<sup>19</sup>", "2<sup>13</sup>", "2<sup>21</sup>", "2<sup>32</sup>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Split the logical address into a page number and a page offset. The page number indexes the page table, so the table has one entry per page of the logical address space.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>8 KB = 2<sup>13</sup> bytes, so the page offset is 13 bits.</li><li>Page-number bits = 32 &minus; 13 = 19.</li><li>A 19-bit page number addresses 2<sup>19</sup> = 524,288 pages, so the table needs 2<sup>19</sup> entries.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2<sup>19</sup> entries.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 2<sup>13</sup> is the number of offsets inside one page.</li><li>(C) 2<sup>21</sup> comes from a 19-bit offset and a 13-bit page number, the two fields swapped.</li><li>(D) 2<sup>32</sup> would be one entry per byte, which paging does not do.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 2<sup>13</sup>, the offset count, instead of the page count, or 2<sup>21</sup> from subtracting the wrong way.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q56", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "An L1 cache is virtually indexed and physically tagged: the set index comes from the virtual address, while the tag is compared against a physical tag. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The cache must be flushed on every context switch, because the index is virtual.", "The index bits lie inside the page offset, so any virtual address that indexes a set has the same offset inside some physical page; no synonym problem is created and the index lookup can run in parallel with the TLB lookup.", "A virtually indexed cache cannot be used together with paging.", "The tag stays virtual, so the TLB is not needed for the access."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cache index is safe to take from the virtual address when every index bit is also a page-offset bit, because page offset bits are identical in the virtual and the physical address.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The page offset is copied unchanged from virtual to physical address; only the page number is translated.</li><li>If the cache is small enough that index bits fall entirely inside the offset (for example a 32 KB, 8-way cache on 4 KB pages uses 6 index bits, well inside the 12 offset bits), the virtual index equals the physical index of the same byte.</li><li>Two different virtual addresses that index the same set therefore belong to the same offset class, so the cache cannot be tricked into storing two physical lines in one slot: no synonym or alias arises.</li><li>The index can be computed immediately while the TLB translates the tag in parallel, which is exactly why VIPT caches are popular.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the index sits inside the page offset, so there is no synonym problem.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Flushing is needed for a virtually indexed and virtually tagged cache, not for VIPT.</li><li>(C) VIPT is a standard design precisely for paged machines.</li><li>(D) The tag is physical here; the TLB is what produces it.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any virtual index is unsafe. Only index bits that reach into the page number (as in a VIVT cache) create synonyms and force flushes.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q57", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A 32-bit machine gives each process a 4 GB logical address space, uses 4 KB pages and 4-byte page-table entries. Four processes each keep a complete single-level page table in main memory. How much main memory do the four page tables occupy?", "options": ["4 MB", "8 MB", "16 MB", "64 MB"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The size of a single-level page table is (logical address space / page size) &times; entry size, and every process needs its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Pages per process = 4 GB / 4 KB = 2<sup>32</sup> / 2<sup>12</sup> = 2<sup>20</sup> = 1,048,576.</li><li>Table per process = 2<sup>20</sup> &times; 4 B = 2<sup>22</sup> B = 4 MB.</li><li>Four processes: 4 &times; 4 MB = 16 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 MB for the four page tables.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 4 MB is one process's table.</li><li>(B) 8 MB is two processes' tables.</li><li>(D) 64 MB over-counts the number of processes.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at 4 MB for one process, or multiplying by the entry size twice.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q58", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A 64-bit machine has 8 GB of physical memory, 4 KB pages and an inverted page table with 8-byte entries, one entry per physical frame. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The table has one entry per virtual page, so it is far larger than physical memory.", "The table has 2<sup>21</sup> entries and occupies 16 MB, and its size depends on physical memory rather than on the size of the virtual address space.", "The table removes the need for a TLB, since its hash lookup replaces translation.", "The table needs one entry for every page table of every process."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An inverted page table has exactly one entry per physical frame, indexed by frame number (in practice through a hash on the virtual page number), instead of one entry per virtual page.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frames = physical memory / page size = 8 GB / 4 KB = 2<sup>33</sup> / 2<sup>12</sup> = 2<sup>21</sup>.</li><li>Table size = 2<sup>21</sup> &times; 8 B = 2<sup>24</sup> B = 16 MB.</li><li>Because the entry count is the frame count, growing the virtual address space to 2<sup>57</sup> bytes changes nothing, while adding physical memory does. That is the whole advantage.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 2<sup>21</sup> entries, 16 MB, sized by physical memory.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) That is the conventional table, the very cost the inverted table removes.</li><li>(C) A lookup still needs a hash plus a possible chain walk, so a TLB remains essential.</li><li>(D) There is a single system-wide table, not one per process.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting the table to scale with the 64-bit virtual address space. That is what a conventional page table does and exactly what the inverted table avoids.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q59", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A page-table entry holds a frame number together with control bits. Which bit must the MMU test in order to raise a page fault?", "options": ["the reference bit", "the dirty bit", "the valid (present) bit", "the cache-disable bit"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The valid bit says whether the entry holds a translation for a page that is currently in memory. A clear valid bit means the page is not resident, which is a page fault.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The MMU forms the physical address from the frame number only if the translation is usable.</li><li>The valid bit is that test: set means the page is in memory, clear means it is on disk.</li><li>A clear valid bit makes the MMU trap to the operating system, which brings the page in — a page fault.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — the valid (present) bit triggers the fault.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The reference bit feeds the replacement algorithm.</li><li>(B) The dirty bit says the page has been written since it was loaded.</li><li>(D) The cache-disable bit controls caching of the page, not its presence.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the dirty bit with the valid bit. The dirty bit decides whether the page must be written back, not whether it is present.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q60", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A program makes 1,000,000 memory references and 950,000 of them find their translation in the TLB. What are the TLB hit ratio and the number of page-table walks?", "options": ["0.95 and 50,000", "0.95 and 950,000", "0.05 and 50,000", "0.95 and 5,000"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The hit ratio is hits divided by references; each miss, and only a miss, forces one page-table walk.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit ratio = 950,000 / 1,000,000 = 0.95.</li><li>Misses = 1,000,000 &minus; 950,000 = 50,000.</li><li>Each miss costs one page-table walk (assuming the page is present), so there are 50,000 walks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0.95 hit ratio and 50,000 page-table walks.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 950,000 walks would mean every hit also walked the table.</li><li>(C) 0.05 is the miss ratio, not the hit ratio.</li><li>(D) 5,000 drops a factor of ten.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting the number of hits as the number of walks, or treating the miss ratio as the walk count.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q61", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A demand-paged system has a memory access time of 100 ns and a page-fault service time of 8 ms. The TLB is ignored. If 1% of all references cause a page fault, what is the effective access time?", "options": ["100 ns", "1,000 ns", "80,099 ns", "8,000,100 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With demand paging the EAT is a weighted mean: the normal access cost on a hit, the fault service time on a fault, weighted by the fault rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Convert units: 8 ms = 8,000,000 ns.</li><li>No-fault path: 0.99 &times; 100 = 99 ns.</li><li>Fault path: 0.01 &times; 8,000,000 = 80,000 ns.</li><li>EAT = 99 + 80,000 = 80,099 ns &asymp; 80.1 &micro;s.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 80,099 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 100 ns is the cost only if no reference ever faults.</li><li>(B) 1,000 ns under-counts the fault service time by three orders of magnitude.</li><li>(D) 8,000,100 ns assumes every reference faults and then adds a normal access.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the 99 ns of the no-fault path and answering 80,000 ns, or averaging 100 ns and 8 ms, which gives about 4 ms.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q62", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A system looks up the TLB first and then walks a single-level page table on a miss. TLB access takes t and one memory access takes m. A TLB hit costs t + m. What does a TLB miss cost, assuming the page is present?", "options": ["t + m", "t + 2m", "2t + m", "2m"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In the hierarchical (TLB-first) model the TLB is always consulted. On a miss the page-table entry must be fetched from memory before the data access can be made.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>TLB lookup: t, paid on every reference.</li><li>Miss: one extra memory access for the page-table entry, m.</li><li>Then the data access itself, m.</li><li>Total = t + m + m = t + 2m.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: t + 2m.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) t + m is the hit cost.</li><li>(C) 2t + m charges the TLB twice.</li><li>(D) 2m omits the TLB lookup that a miss also pays.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing 2m and dropping the TLB time, or assuming the page-table walk is free because the TLB was already consulted.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q63", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A TLB is accessed in 10 ns and main memory in 100 ns, the page table is single-level and held in memory, and the TLB is looked up first. The measured effective access time is 120 ns. What is the TLB hit ratio?", "options": ["0.80", "0.90", "0.95", "1.00"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> EAT = h(t + m) + (1 &minus; h)(t + 2m). With EAT known, this is a linear equation in h.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = 10 + 100 = 110 ns; miss path = 10 + 100 + 100 = 210 ns.</li><li>110h + 210(1 &minus; h) = 120.</li><li>210 &minus; 100h = 120.</li><li>100h = 90, so h = 0.90.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: h = 0.90.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) h = 0.80 gives EAT = 130 ns.</li><li>(C) h = 0.95 gives EAT = 115 ns.</li><li>(D) h = 1.00 gives EAT = 110 ns, the all-hit cost.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 2m = 200 ns as the miss path and dropping the TLB time, which gives 200 &minus; 90h = 120 and a wrong h.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q64", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "In a pure segmented system every segment-table entry holds a base and a limit. What does the limit bound?", "options": ["the number of segments the process may own.", "the largest offset allowed inside that segment: an offset greater than or equal to the limit traps.", "the size of the page table that backs the segment.", "the number of processes that may share the segment."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The base gives where the segment starts in physical memory; the limit gives its length, so it bounds the offset that may be added to the base.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The MMU compares the offset d with the limit L.</li><li>If d &ge; L the access is out of range and traps, which is how segmentation catches a stray pointer.</li><li>If d &lt; L the physical address is base + d.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the limit bounds the offset inside the segment.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The number of segments is bounded by the segment-number field width.</li><li>(C) Pure segmentation has no page table.</li><li>(D) Sharing is a permission decision, not the limit.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the limit as a bound on the segment number. The segment number is bounded by the table size, not by the limit.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q65", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which statement correctly contrasts pure paging with pure segmentation?", "options": ["Paging suffers external fragmentation while segmentation suffers none.", "Paging suffers internal fragmentation, in the last page of each region, but no external fragmentation; pure segmentation suffers external fragmentation as segments are allocated and freed.", "Both suffer only internal fragmentation.", "Neither suffers fragmentation, because both use a translation table."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Paging cuts memory into fixed frames, so any hole is filled by some page and the only waste is the unused tail of a region's last page. Segmentation allocates variable-sized pieces, so holes of the wrong size accumulate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Paging: every frame is the same size, so any free frame can hold any page — no unusable hole remains. The cost is the slack at the end of the last page of each region: internal fragmentation.</li><li>Segmentation: a segment must occupy one contiguous run. When segments of assorted sizes are created and destroyed, free runs appear that are too small for the next request — external fragmentation.</li><li>So the contrast is internal-only waste for paging against external waste for segmentation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — paging wastes internally, segmentation externally.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The two kinds of waste are swapped.</li><li>(C) Pure segmentation has no internal fragmentation: a segment is exactly as large as requested.</li><li>(D) Both schemes fragment; only the kind differs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a translation table removes fragmentation. A table changes how addresses are formed, not how memory is allocated.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q66", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which of the following statements about memory interleaving are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["With low-order interleaving, consecutive words live in different banks, so a sequential stream can overlap accesses in several banks.", "With high-order interleaving, a sequential scan of one large array touches mostly a single bank and gains little from the other banks.", "Interleaving shortens the cycle time of each individual bank.", "With low-order interleaving over k banks, an access pattern that strides by k words hits the same bank every time."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Interleaving distributes addresses over banks; it changes how many banks work in parallel, never how fast one bank is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The bank is chosen by low-order bits, so consecutive addresses rotate through the banks and their cycle times overlap.</li><li>(B) True. The bank is chosen by high-order bits, so a contiguous scan stays in one bank.</li><li>(C) False. The bank's own cycle time is unchanged; interleaving only hides that time behind other banks.</li><li>(D) True. Adding k words advances the bank index by k mod k = 0, so every access in that stride lands in the same bank — the classic bank-conflict pattern.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is the false one: the per-bank cycle time is a property of the silicon, not of the address mapping.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing interleaving makes memory faster per access. It raises sustained bandwidth, and only when the accesses are spread over banks.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q67", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which of the following statements about a TLB are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A TLB entry usually caches a page-table entry: the frame number together with protection and dirty bits.", "Tagging entries with an address-space identifier (ASID) lets a context switch keep most of the TLB, because entries of different processes no longer collide.", "A TLB miss always means the page is not in memory.", "For a fixed number of entries, increasing associativity removes conflict misses between pages that map to the same set."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A TLB is a small cache of translations. It answers the address-translation question, not the page-residency question.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Caching the whole entry is what lets the MMU check protection and the dirty bit without a second memory access.</li><li>(B) True. With an ASID the tag distinguishes address spaces, so entries of the old process stay valid after a switch and only entries of a reused ASID must be invalidated.</li><li>(C) False. A miss only says the translation is not cached; the page-table walk may well find the page present. Only a clear valid bit in the walked entry is a page fault.</li><li>(D) True. Conflict misses come from several live pages mapping to one set; more ways per set removes them (capacity misses remain).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is the false one: a TLB miss costs a walk, and most walks succeed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Equating a TLB miss with a page fault. The two are separated by the page-table walk.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q68", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "For a single-level paged system with hierarchical (TLB-first) access, EAT = h(t + m) + (1 &minus; h)(t + 2m), where h is the TLB hit ratio, t the TLB access time and m the memory access time. Which changes reduce the EAT? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Enlarging the TLB so that h rises from 0.90 to 0.98.", "Replacing the single-level page table with a two-level page table, keeping h unchanged.", "Reducing the memory access time m, for example by using faster memory.", "Removing the TLB and relying on the page table in memory, so that h becomes 0."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> EAT = t + m + (1 &minus; h)m. Only t, m and h appear; anything that increases the number of memory accesses on the miss path works against you.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Rewrite: EAT = t + m + (1 &minus; h)m, so the miss penalty is (1 &minus; h)m.</li><li>(A) True. Raising h from 0.90 to 0.98 cuts the miss penalty from 0.10m to 0.02m.</li><li>(B) False. A two-level table adds a second page-table memory access on a miss, making the miss path t + 3m; EAT rises unless h improves.</li><li>(C) True. m appears with a coefficient 1 + (1 &minus; h) &gt; 0, so a smaller m always lowers EAT.</li><li>(D) False. h = 0 turns every reference into a full walk, the slowest case in the formula.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (D) both lengthen the path taken on a TLB miss.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating multi-level paging as a pure win. It saves page-table memory, not access time; on a miss it costs an extra memory access per level.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q69", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which of the following statements about pure segmentation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Each segment carries its own base and limit, so protection and sharing can be decided per segment.", "A logical address is the pair (segment number, offset), and the hardware adds the base to the offset after a limit check.", "Segments are fixed-size blocks chosen by the hardware, exactly as pages are.", "Because segments are variable-sized, a long run of allocation and release can leave external fragmentation."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Segmentation is the programmer's view of memory: variable-sized, named pieces with their own protection. Paging is the hardware's view: fixed-size, invisible pieces.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. The base and limit of one segment are independent of every other segment, so per-segment read, write and execute rights — and sharing — are natural.</li><li>(B) True. That is the translation: check d &lt; limit, then physical = base + d.</li><li>(C) False. Segments are variable-sized and sized by the program's logical units; it is pages that have a fixed hardware-chosen size.</li><li>(D) True. Variable-sized requests leave unusable holes between allocated segments.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is the false one: fixed size is the defining property of paging.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Importing paging's fixed size into segmentation. The whole contrast is variable-size logical units against fixed-size physical frames.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q70", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which of the following statements about fragmentation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Paging removes external fragmentation but can waste up to one page in the last page of a region.", "Pure segmentation can suffer external fragmentation.", "The internal fragmentation of paging is bounded by the page size.", "Segmentation with paging removes internal fragmentation completely."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Internal fragmentation is waste inside an allocated block; external fragmentation is free memory that is unusable because it is split into pieces that are too small.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Frames are interchangeable, so no hole is unusable; the waste is the slack at the end of the last page.</li><li>(B) True. Segments must be contiguous, so freed space of assorted sizes may not fit the next request.</li><li>(C) True. A region needs ceil(size / page) pages, so at most page size &minus; 1 byte is wasted per region.</li><li>(D) False. Segmentation with paging still allocates whole pages to the last piece of each segment, so internal fragmentation survives — it is only the external kind that paging removes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the false one.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that combining the two schemes removes all waste. Paging removes external fragmentation; the internal slack inside the last page of every segment remains.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q71", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "dram-sram-organization", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A DRAM chip has 13 row-address pins and 11 column-address pins, with the two addresses multiplexed onto the same pins, and its data width is 8 bits. What is its capacity in megabits?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "128", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of addressable locations is 2<sup>(rows + columns)</sup> because the two address halves are used one after the other, not simultaneously.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Locations = 2<sup>13</sup> &times; 2<sup>11</sup> = 2<sup>24</sup> = 16,777,216.</li><li>Each location holds 8 bits, so capacity = 2<sup>24</sup> &times; 8 bits = 2<sup>27</sup> bits.</li><li>2<sup>27</sup> / 2<sup>20</sup> = 2<sup>7</sup> = 128 Mbit.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 128 Mbit.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using max(13, 11) = 13 address bits because the pins are shared, which gives 2<sup>13</sup> locations; the sharing reduces pins, not the number of locations.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q72", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A machine uses 48-bit virtual addresses, 4 KB pages and a single-level page table. How many bits of the virtual address index the page table?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "36", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The virtual address splits into a page-number field, which indexes the table, and a page-offset field, which is passed through unchanged.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>4 KB = 2<sup>12</sup> bytes, so the offset field is 12 bits.</li><li>Page-number bits = 48 &minus; 12 = 36.</li><li>The table therefore has 2<sup>36</sup> entries — which is exactly why a 48-bit machine uses multi-level paging.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 36 index bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 12, the offset width, or 48, the whole address width.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q73", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "paging-tlb-hit-ratio", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A TLB has 256 entries and the page size is 4 KB. What is the TLB reach in KB, that is, how much memory can the TLB map at one time?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1024", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> TLB reach = number of entries &times; page size, because each entry maps exactly one page.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Entries = 256, page size = 4 KB.</li><li>Reach = 256 &times; 4 KB = 1024 KB = 1 MB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1024 KB (1 MB).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by the entry size in bytes instead of the page size, or reporting 256 KB by using 1 KB pages.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q74", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "effective-access-time", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A system uses three-level paging. A TLB access takes 5 ns, one memory access takes 100 ns, and the TLB is looked up before memory is touched. The TLB hit ratio is 0.95. What is the effective access time, in ns?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "120", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> On a hit the reference costs one TLB access plus one data access; on a miss it costs one TLB access, one memory access per page-table level, and then the data access.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Hit path = 5 + 100 = 105 ns.</li><li>Miss path = 5 + 3 &times; 100 + 100 = 405 ns (three levels of page table, then the data word).</li><li>EAT = 0.95 &times; 105 + 0.05 &times; 405 = 99.75 + 20.25 = 120 ns.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 120 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only one page-table access on the miss path, which ignores the other two levels, or charging the TLB only on a hit.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U4-Q75", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "memory-management-unit", "subtopic": "segmentation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A process has the segment table below. What physical address does the logical address (3, 950) produce?<br/>Segment 0: base 1200, limit 400<br/>Segment 1: base 3000, limit 800<br/>Segment 2: base 500, limit 200<br/>Segment 3: base 8000, limit 1000<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8950", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Segmentation translates (s, d) by checking d against the segment's limit and then adding the base.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Segment number 3 has base 8000 and limit 1000.</li><li>Limit check: 950 &lt; 1000, so the offset is legal.</li><li>Physical address = base + offset = 8000 + 950 = 8950.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8950.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the limit as an end address (8000 + 1000 = 9000) or adding the offset to the limit instead of the base.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q51", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A five-stage instruction pipeline runs on a 2 ns clock, with balanced stages and no hazards. In steady state, its throughput in millions of instructions per second (MIPS) is", "options": ["100", "200", "500", "1000"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In steady state an ideal pipeline retires one instruction per clock cycle, so throughput is 1/T whatever the number of stages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One instruction completes every clock, and the clock period is T = 2 ns.</li><li>Rate = 1 / (2 ns) = 0.5 instructions per nanosecond.</li><li>0.5 per ns = 0.5 &#215; 10<sup>9</sup> per second = 500 &#215; 10<sup>6</sup> per second = 500 MIPS.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &#8212; 500 MIPS.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 100 MIPS divides the single-cycle rate by the five stages; the stage count does not divide steady-state throughput.</li><li>(B) 200 MIPS would need a 5 ns clock.</li><li>(D) 1000 MIPS would need a 1 ns clock.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing the throughput by the number of stages. With k stages one instruction's <i>latency</i> is k cycles, but the <i>throughput</i> is still one per cycle.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q52", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Which one of the following is a structural hazard in a pipelined processor?", "options": ["Two instructions in the same cycle both need the single memory port.", "An instruction needs a register value that an earlier instruction has not yet written.", "The address of the next instruction is unknown because a branch is unresolved.", "A cache miss forces the pipeline to wait for main memory."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Hazards are classified by what blocks the next instruction: a resource conflict (structural), a data dependence (data) or a control dependence (control).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) has two instructions wanting the same hardware in the same cycle. That resource conflict is the definition of a structural hazard.</li><li>(B) is a data hazard, a RAW dependence.</li><li>(C) is a control hazard.</li><li>(D) delays one instruction for memory; it is a cache miss stall, not a conflict between two instructions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) A RAW dependence is a data hazard; no hardware is contested.</li><li>(C) An unresolved branch is a control hazard.</li><li>(D) A cache miss lengthens one access; nothing else is competing for a resource.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling every stall a structural hazard. A structural hazard needs two instructions competing for one piece of hardware in the same cycle.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q53", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "In a five-stage in-order pipeline the pair add r1, r2, r3 followed immediately by sub r4, r1, r5 creates a hazard. Which dependence is it?", "options": ["WAR (anti-dependence): the sub must write r1 before the add reads it.", "RAW (true dependence): the sub reads r1 after the add produces it.", "WAW (output dependence): both instructions write r1.", "Control dependence: the value of r1 decides whether the sub executes."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Name a dependence by the order of the two accesses to the register: read-after-write is a true (RAW) dependence, and it is the one forwarding repairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The <code class=\"mm\">add</code> writes r1 in WB; the <code class=\"mm\">sub</code> reads r1 in ID.</li><li>The read comes after the write in program order, so the dependence is read-after-write.</li><li>RAW is the only class that can cost a stall in this simple pipeline, because reads happen in ID and writes in WB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; a RAW (true) dependence.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A WAR would need the read to come first in program order; here the write comes first.</li><li>(C) Only the <code class=\"mm\">add</code> writes r1; a WAW needs two writes.</li><li>(D) Nothing here is conditional.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the dependence. The later instruction <i>reads</i> what the earlier one <i>writes</i>, which is RAW; a WAR would need the opposite order.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q54", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Why does a control hazard exist in a pipeline?", "options": ["Because the register file can supply only one value per cycle.", "Because the address of the next instruction is not known while the branch is still being decoded or executed.", "Because a load's data arrives too late for the instruction that follows it.", "Because two instructions need the ALU in the same cycle."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A control hazard is a fetch-time problem: the fetch unit must choose an address before the branch has decided which address is correct.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The pipeline fetches every cycle, so it needs the next PC every cycle.</li><li>A conditional branch fixes the next PC only when its condition and target are resolved, which happens in ID or EX.</li><li>The instructions fetched in the meantime are speculative and must be discarded if the branch is taken: that is the control hazard.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A port shortage is a structural hazard.</li><li>(C) A late load value is a data hazard.</li><li>(D) An ALU conflict is a structural hazard.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a control hazard as a data problem. The data path is fine; what is missing is the correct next address.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q55", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "An unpipelined datapath is cut into four stages of 4 ns, 3 ns, 6 ns and 3 ns, and each pipeline register adds 1 ns. For a very long instruction stream, the speedup of the pipelined datapath over the unpipelined one is closest to", "options": ["4.00", "2.29", "2.67", "1.78"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup = (unpipelined time per instruction) / (pipeline clock period), where the clock period is the slowest stage plus the register overhead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unpipelined time per instruction = 4 + 3 + 6 + 3 = 16 ns.</li><li>Pipelined clock = slowest stage + register overhead = 6 + 1 = 7 ns.</li><li>Speedup = 16 / 7 = 2.2857&#8230; &#8776; 2.29.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; about 2.29.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 4.00 assumes perfectly balanced stages and no register overhead.</li><li>(C) 2.67 comes from ignoring the register overhead: 16 / 6 = 2.67.</li><li>(D) 1.78 inverts the ratio or uses 9 ns as the clock.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the register overhead, which is what keeps the speedup well below the stage count.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q56", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "An ISA includes fused multiply-add instructions of the form a = b*c + d, which read three registers in ID. The register file of a five-stage pipeline has only two read ports. What is the minimum change that removes the structural hazard in ID?", "options": ["Nothing: the third register is read during EX, so no extra port is needed.", "Split ID into two cycles, or add a third read port, so that one instruction's three reads fit in one cycle.", "Add a write port, because the hazard is between the destination write and the reads.", "Duplicate the register file so that each instruction reads from its own copy."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A port is a resource. A structural hazard exists when one instruction's accesses in a single cycle exceed the ports available, and the cure is either more ports or more cycles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The fused multiply-add reads b, c and d in ID: three reads in one cycle.</li><li>Two read ports deliver only two values per cycle, so the third read cannot happen.</li><li>Either ID is split into two cycles (two reads, then one) or a third read port is added. The first costs a cycle, the second costs area; both remove the conflict.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Reading an operand in EX is not possible in this pipeline: operands are read in ID and the ALU works on latched values.</li><li>(C) The write happens in WB, a different cycle, so it does not compete with the ID reads.</li><li>(D) Duplicating the file doubles the ports only if each copy is written as well, which is more than the minimum change.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Blaming the destination write. The conflict is among the reads, which all fall in ID.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q57", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Why does even a complete forwarding path fail to remove the stall that follows a load?", "options": ["The loaded value leaves memory only at the end of MEM, after the consumer needs it at the start of EX.", "Loads write the register file only in WB, and forwarding can read only the EX output.", "The single memory port is busy, so the load's result cannot be forwarded.", "Forwarding works only between ALU instructions that use the same functional unit."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Forwarding removes a stall only when the producer's value exists before the consumer enters EX. The load's timing is what breaks that condition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A load computes its address in EX and reads data in MEM, so the value exists only at the end of MEM.</li><li>The very next instruction enters EX in the same cycle in which the load is in MEM, and it needs the value at the start of that cycle.</li><li>One cycle of delay is therefore unavoidable; after that the value can be forwarded MEM to EX.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Forwarding paths include MEM to EX and WB to EX; the load is not restricted to WB.</li><li>(C) The memory port is a structural matter and is not what makes the value late for EX.</li><li>(D) Forwarding is not restricted to one functional unit; ALU to ALU forwarding is exactly what works.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a forwarding path is a timing cure-all. It moves a value earlier, but it cannot move it earlier than the stage that produces it.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q58", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A 2-bit saturating branch predictor starts in state 01 (states 00 and 01 predict not taken, 10 and 11 predict taken). The next seven branch outcomes are T, T, N, T, N, T, T. How many mispredictions occur?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A 2-bit saturating counter changes state only after the outcome is known, and it predicts taken in the upper two states. Simulate it outcome by outcome.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start in 01, predict N. Outcome T: misprediction, state 01 &#8594; 10. (1 miss)</li><li>State 10, predict T. Outcome T: correct, state 10 &#8594; 11.</li><li>State 11, predict T. Outcome N: misprediction, state 11 &#8594; 10. (2 misses)</li><li>State 10, predict T. Outcome T: correct, state &#8594; 11.</li><li>State 11, predict T. Outcome N: misprediction, state &#8594; 10. (3 misses)</li><li>State 10, predict T. Outcome T: correct, state &#8594; 11. Then T again: correct.</li><li>Total mispredictions = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; 3 mispredictions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 2 misses would need the counter to adapt faster than a 2-bit saturating counter does.</li><li>(C) 4 counts the last two T outcomes as misses; the counter is already in state 11 and predicts them correctly.</li><li>(D) 5 is the count a 1-bit predictor would not even reach here; it ignores the state transitions.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the outcome as if the predictor saw it in advance. The state used for each prediction is the one left by the previous outcome.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q59", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A six-stage pipeline with a 1.5 ns clock executes 200 independent instructions with no hazards. Including fill and drain, the total execution time is", "options": ["300 ns", "306 ns", "307.5 ns", "309 ns"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For n instructions in a k-stage pipeline the cycle count is k + (n &#8722; 1): k cycles to fill and drain, one instruction per cycle in between.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cycles = 6 + (200 &#8722; 1) = 205.</li><li>Time = 205 &#215; 1.5 ns = 307.5 ns.</li><li>Check the limits: 200 &#215; 1.5 = 300 ns would be zero fill overhead, and 206 &#215; 1.5 = 309 ns over-counts the drain.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &#8212; 307.5 ns.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 300 ns ignores fill and drain altogether.</li><li>(B) 306 ns uses 204 cycles, one short.</li><li>(D) 309 ns counts k + n cycles instead of k + n &#8722; 1.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using k + n instead of k + (n &#8722; 1). The last instruction needs only its remaining stages, not a whole new pipeline's worth.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q60", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A 2-way superscalar in-order processor issues two instructions per cycle. Its register file has 4 read ports and 2 write ports; each instruction reads at most two registers in ID and writes at most one in WB. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["A structural hazard occurs whenever both issued instructions write a register.", "A structural hazard occurs whenever both issued instructions read two registers each.", "No register-file structural hazard occurs for any legal issue pair.", "A structural hazard occurs only when one instruction reads two registers and the other writes one."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count the worst-case demand of one issue group and compare it with the ports: two instructions need at most 2 &#215; 2 = 4 reads and 2 &#215; 1 = 2 writes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Worst case for reads: both instructions read two registers, needing 4 read ports. The file has 4.</li><li>Worst case for writes: both instructions write one register each, needing 2 write ports. The file has 2.</li><li>Every legal pair therefore fits within the ports in every cycle, so the register file never causes a structural hazard.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Two writes need 2 write ports, which the file has.</li><li>(B) Four reads need 4 read ports, which the file has.</li><li>(D) Reads and writes happen in different stages (ID and WB) and use separate ports, so a read-write pair cannot conflict.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that any simultaneous pair must conflict. A hazard needs demand to exceed the ports, and here it never does.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q61", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "In a program 30% of the instructions are loads, and 40% of those loads are immediately followed by an instruction that uses the loaded value. With full forwarding, including the load-use path, each such pair costs one stall cycle and there are no other hazards. The CPI is", "options": ["1.03", "1.12", "1.30", "1.40"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CPI = base CPI + (events per instruction) &#215; (cycles per event). Only the fraction of loads that are actually followed by a consumer contributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Base CPI with no stalls is 1.</li><li>Load-use stalls per instruction = 0.30 &#215; 0.40 = 0.12, each costing one cycle.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.12 &#215; 1 = 1.12.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; 1.12.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 1.03 is what a 10% load-use rate would give.</li><li>(C) 1.30 charges a stall to every load, ignoring that only 40% are followed by a consumer.</li><li>(D) 1.40 treats 40% of all instructions as load-use pairs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging the stall to every load instead of to the load-use pairs only. The event rate is the product of the two fractions.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q62", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A datapath has a total combinational delay of 12 ns and is pipelined into k balanced stages, each pipeline register adding 0.4 ns. For a very long instruction stream, which statement about the speedup as k increases is TRUE?", "options": ["It grows without bound.", "It increases and approaches 30 but never reaches it.", "It is maximised at k = 4.", "It falls once k exceeds 6."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup(k) = D / (D/k + t), where D is the total logic delay and t the register overhead. As k &#8594; &#8734; the stage delay vanishes and only the overhead remains.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stage delay = 12/k ns, so the clock period is 12/k + 0.4 ns.</li><li>Speedup = 12 / (12/k + 0.4).</li><li>k = 4 gives 12/3.4 = 3.53; k = 8 gives 12/1.9 = 6.32; k = 20 gives 12/1.0 = 12.</li><li>The sequence increases monotonically and its limit is 12 / 0.4 = 30, which is never attained for finite k.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; the speedup approaches 30 asymptotically.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Unbounded growth would need zero register overhead.</li><li>(C) Speedup keeps increasing past k = 4.</li><li>(D) With balanced stages and fixed overhead the speedup never falls.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming more stages always help in proportion. Each extra stage adds register overhead to every cycle, which caps the gain at D/t.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q63", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A five-stage pipeline resolves conditional branches in ID. When a branch is taken, the instruction fetched alongside the branch's ID must be discarded, costing one cycle. 20% of instructions are branches and 60% of branches are taken. The extra CPI from control hazards is", "options": ["0.08", "0.12", "0.20", "0.60"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Extra CPI = (branches per instruction) &#215; (fraction taken) &#215; (cycles lost per taken branch).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Branches per instruction = 0.20.</li><li>Taken branches per instruction = 0.20 &#215; 0.60 = 0.12.</li><li>Each taken branch costs 1 cycle, so extra CPI = 0.12 &#215; 1 = 0.12.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; 0.12.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 0.08 uses the 40% of branches that are not taken.</li><li>(C) 0.20 charges a penalty to every branch, taken or not.</li><li>(D) 0.60 is the taken fraction, not a CPI contribution.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging every branch. A not-taken branch needs no flush at all, so only the taken fraction pays.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q64", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A loop branch is executed 50 times, taken 45 times and not taken 5 times, in the pattern ten taken outcomes followed by one not-taken, repeated five times. A 1-bit predictor starts in the taken state. How many mispredictions occur?", "options": ["5", "9", "10", "14"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A 1-bit predictor predicts whatever the last outcome was, so every change of outcome costs exactly one misprediction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first block of ten taken outcomes is predicted correctly, because the predictor starts in the taken state.</li><li>The not-taken that ends the block is a misprediction, and it flips the predictor to not-taken. That is one per block, so 5.</li><li>The first taken outcome of each later block is then predicted as not-taken: another misprediction, and it flips the predictor back. That happens for blocks 2 to 5, so 4 more.</li><li>The remaining nine taken outcomes in each block are predicted correctly. Total = 5 + 4 = 9.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; 9 mispredictions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 5 counts only the not-taken exits and misses the mispredicted re-entry.</li><li>(C) 10 counts two mispredictions per block including the first block, whose first taken outcome is predicted correctly.</li><li>(D) 14 over-counts; a 1-bit predictor has at most two mispredictions per outcome change.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the re-entry. After the loop exits, the predictor is left predicting not-taken, so the next iteration's first taken outcome is wrong too.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q65", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Which hazard can forwarding alone never remove?", "options": ["An ALU-to-ALU RAW hazard between adjacent instructions.", "A load-use RAW hazard.", "A RAW hazard with a gap of two instructions between producer and consumer.", "A RAW hazard where the producer writes in WB and the consumer reads in ID."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Forwarding supplies a value from a later pipeline stage to an earlier one. If the producer's stage is later than the consumer's need, no path can help.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>ALU to ALU: the result exists at the end of EX, in time for the next instruction's EX. No stall.</li><li>Producer two instructions ahead, or WB-to-ID: the value already exists. No stall.</li><li>Load to use: the value exists only at the end of MEM, after the consumer's EX has begun. One stall remains whatever forwarding path exists.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &#8212; the load-use hazard.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) ALU results are forwarded EX to EX and cost nothing.</li><li>(C) Two intervening instructions give the producer time; no stall is needed.</li><li>(D) A WB-to-ID path (or a same-cycle write-then-read file) handles this case.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing forwarding removes all data stalls. It removes the ones where the value already exists somewhere in the pipeline.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q66", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Which of the following statements about an ideal k-stage pipeline (balanced stages, no hazards, no register overhead) are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In steady state it completes one instruction per clock cycle.", "Executing n instructions takes k + n − 1 clock cycles.", "Its speedup over the equivalent unpipelined datapath is exactly k for any n.", "Its clock period equals the delay of the slowest stage."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An ideal pipeline trades latency for throughput: one instruction per cycle, k cycles of fill and drain, and a clock set by the slowest stage.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: after fill, each cycle retires one instruction.</li><li>(B) True: k &#8722; 1 cycles of fill plus n cycles of issue.</li><li>(C) False: the speedup is k n / (k + n &#8722; 1), which equals k only in the limit n &#8594; &#8734;.</li><li>(D) True: every stage must fit in one clock, so the slowest one sets the period.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Quoting k as the speedup for a real program. It is the asymptotic limit; fill, drain and hazards keep the measured speedup lower.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q67", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Which of the following are structural hazards? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Two instructions in the same cycle both need the single unified memory.", "An instruction needs a register value still being computed by an earlier instruction.", "In a 2-issue machine, two instructions both need the register-file write port in the same cycle.", "A functional unit is not fully pipelined, so a new operation cannot start every cycle."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A structural hazard is a shortage of hardware: two instructions wanting one resource in one cycle, or a unit that cannot accept a new operation each cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A memory port conflict: structural.</li><li>(B) A RAW dependence: a data hazard, not a resource conflict.</li><li>(C) One write port, two writers: structural.</li><li>(D) A non-pipelined unit accepts work only every few cycles: structural.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Classifying a data dependence as a resource conflict because both instructions touch the same register. Registers are a resource only when ports are exhausted.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q68", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A five-stage pipeline has full forwarding, including the load-use path. Which statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An ALU-to-ALU RAW dependence between adjacent instructions costs no stall cycle.", "A load immediately followed by a consumer of the loaded value still costs one stall cycle.", "Forwarding can eliminate the control hazard of a taken branch.", "In lw r1, 0(r2) followed by sw r1, 8(r3), forwarding from the end of MEM to the store's data input avoids a stall."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Forwarding moves a value to where it is needed in time. Whether a stall disappears depends on which stage produces the value and which stage needs it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: the ALU result exists at the end of EX, exactly when the next instruction's EX begins.</li><li>(B) True: the load's value exists only at the end of MEM, one cycle too late for the next instruction's EX.</li><li>(C) False: a branch needs the correct next address, not a data value; prediction and early resolution address it.</li><li>(D) True: a store needs its data only in MEM, so a MEM-to-MEM path meets the timing with no stall.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking forwarding is a general fix for hazards. It repairs value timing only, and only when the value already exists.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q69", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Which of the following reduce the penalty paid when a branch is taken? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Resolving the branch in ID instead of EX.", "Filling the delay slot with a useful instruction.", "A more accurate branch predictor.", "Raising the clock frequency without changing the pipeline depth."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The penalty is the number of cycles of work thrown away. Reduce it by resolving the branch earlier, by doing useful work while waiting, or by not guessing wrong.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Earlier resolution means fewer speculative instructions to flush.</li><li>(B) A filled delay slot does useful work instead of being discarded.</li><li>(C) A better predictor means fewer taken branches that were predicted the wrong way.</li><li>(D) A faster clock shortens every cycle but does not change how many cycles are lost.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing time with cycles. A faster clock shortens the penalty in nanoseconds but leaves the cycle count, and therefore the CPI, unchanged.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q70", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "Which statements about pipeline speedup are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["With a total logic delay D and per-stage register overhead t, the speedup is bounded above by D/t as the number of stages grows.", "With unbalanced stages the clock period is set by the slowest stage, so the speedup stays below k.", "Doubling the number of stages always doubles the speedup.", "Fill and drain overhead becomes negligible as the number of instructions grows."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup is a ratio of times: unpipelined D against pipelined (stage delay + overhead). Both the stage count and the overhead matter, and fill cost shrinks relatively as n grows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: speedup = D / (D/k + t), whose limit is D/t as k &#8594; &#8734;.</li><li>(B) True: the clock is set by the slowest stage, so the ideal k-fold gain is never reached when stages are unequal.</li><li>(C) False: each extra stage adds overhead, so the gain per stage shrinks; doubling k gives less than double the speedup.</li><li>(D) True: k &#8722; 1 fill cycles against n cycles of work becomes a vanishing fraction as n grows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the stage count as the speedup. The stage count is an upper bound that overhead and imbalance both eat into.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q71", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A datapath has five stages of 2 ns each and no register overhead. A program of 200 instructions runs on it unpipelined, then on the same datapath pipelined. What is the speedup? (Give your answer to two decimal places.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4.90", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Speedup = unpipelined time / pipelined time, where the pipelined time includes fill and drain: (k + n &#8722; 1) cycles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unpipelined: 200 instructions &#215; 10 ns = 2000 ns.</li><li>Pipelined: (5 + 199) cycles = 204 cycles &#215; 2 ns = 408 ns.</li><li>Speedup = 2000 / 408 = 4.9019&#8230;, which rounds to 4.90.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.90 (2000 ns against 408 ns; the exact value is 4.9019&#8230;).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 200 cycles instead of 204 and answering 5.00. Fill and drain are real time, and they matter at n = 200.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q72", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "structural-hazards", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A five-stage pipeline uses one unified memory for both instruction fetch and data access. A program of 100 instructions contains 20 loads and no stores. How many memory accesses does the program make in total? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "120", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a five-stage pipeline every instruction is fetched once, and only loads and stores make a second access, in MEM.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fetches: one per instruction = 100.</li><li>Data accesses: 20 loads, no stores = 20.</li><li>Total = 120. The single port must serve both, which is why 20 of these accesses can collide with a fetch.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 120.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting two accesses for every instruction, or forgetting the fetch. Only memory-type instructions add a second access.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q73", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "data-hazards-forwarding", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A program runs on a five-stage pipeline with full forwarding. 25% of the instructions are loads, and 60% of those loads are immediately followed by a dependent instruction, each such pair costing one stall cycle. 15% of the instructions are branches, each costing a two-cycle penalty. Ignoring all other hazards, what is the CPI? (Give your answer to two decimal places.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1.45", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CPI = 1 + sum over hazard types of (events per instruction) &#215; (cycles per event).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Load-use stalls per instruction = 0.25 &#215; 0.60 = 0.15, at 1 cycle each: 0.15.</li><li>Branch penalty per instruction = 0.15 &#215; 2 = 0.30.</li><li>CPI = 1 + 0.15 + 0.30 = 1.45.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1.45.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the raw percentages (25% + 15%) instead of the stall events. A load only stalls when a consumer follows it.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q74", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "pipeline-stages-throughput", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "An unpipelined datapath has four stages of 3 ns, 5 ns, 2 ns and 4 ns. It is pipelined into those same four stages with 1 ns of register overhead per stage. For a very long program, what is the speedup? (Give your answer to two decimal places.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2.33", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a long program the speedup is (sum of stage delays) / (slowest stage + register overhead).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unpipelined time per instruction = 3 + 5 + 2 + 4 = 14 ns.</li><li>Pipeline clock = 5 + 1 = 6 ns.</li><li>Speedup = 14 / 6 = 2.3333&#8230; &#8776; 2.33.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.33.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 14 / 5 = 2.80 by dropping the register overhead, which is part of every cycle.</p>"}, {"id": "CO-U5-Q75", "subject": "computer-architecture", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "pipelining", "subtopic": "control-hazards-branch-penalty", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-05", "text": "A loop body of ten instructions ends in a conditional branch that is resolved in EX, with a two-cycle penalty whenever the branch is taken. The loop runs 20 times and the branch is taken on every iteration except the last. How many cycles are lost to control hazards in total? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "38", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Total lost cycles = (taken branches) &#215; (cycles lost per taken branch). The final iteration's not-taken branch costs nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Branch instances = 20; taken = 19.</li><li>Each taken branch costs 2 cycles: 19 &#215; 2 = 38.</li><li>The last branch is not taken, so no flush occurs there.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 38.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging all 20 branches (40 cycles) and forgetting that a not-taken branch needs no flush.</p>"}];
